c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc","sha":"46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc"}],"sha":"54d7d8c0bf939687702fbcf63fe8bbf1ddc8dc73","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/54d7d8c0bf939687702fbcf63fe8bbf1ddc8dc73"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T08:50:14Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T08:50:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(cases): case::open echoes the primary insight''s money, unit-checked (#207)\n\nNothing anywhere wrote `impact_rate`. `case::open` hardcoded it to `None`,\nno verb or reactor set it after, and no pack mentioned it — so the case\nplane''s COST column and two of its three money tiles were structurally\nblank on every node, for every case, for ever. The UI drew the rate line\ncorrectly and it simply never fired.\n\nThis is one more field through a path that already exists, not a new\nmechanism: `case::open` already echoes `category`/`site`/`scope`/`severity`\nfrom the primary insight, and now it echoes the rate from that insight''s\nshipped `analysis.estimated_impact` too.\n\nTHE UNIT IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED. `Quantity.unit` is free text and its own\ndoc comment warns that a consumer must \"group by `unit` and refuse to add\nunlike units\". `Case.impact_rate` is a bare f64 that the queue sums and the\nowner report ranks by, so a `{ value: 3.2, unit: \"sigma\" }` copied into it\ndoes not look wrong — it becomes $3.20/day in front of a customer. The value\nis therefore copied only when the unit parses as `<ISO4217>/day`; anything\nelse copies no rate and logs the refusal. A pattern, not an allowlist: a\ncurrency table is 180 rows lb has no business owning, and the shape is\nenough to separate money from `sigma`, `kL`, `%` and `kWh`.\n\nTier is `claimed`, never `modelled`. From this seam a rule''s own estimate\nand a tariff-priced one are the same JSON, so it can only state the weaker\nclaim honestly; `modelled` rolls into the owner headline and has to be\nearned by a producer-side signal that does not exist yet.\n\n`withheld` still wins, and is tested with both halves — a caveated case\nKEEPS its rate (it becomes showable when the caveat clears) and carries\n`impact_tier: withheld`, so the figure is suppressed by the tier and not by\na missing number. A change deleting the withhold would otherwise pass a test\nthat only checked the tier.\n\n`case.split` deliberately takes NO money: the new case is about a different\nprimary, so inheriting the source''s rate would double-count the same money\nacross two open cases.\n\nThe rate is frozen at open, like every facet except `caveated` —\n`plane_immunity.rs` already pinned that across 50 re-raises.\n\nTests: 8 unit over the parse table, 4 integration over a real booted node\n(real store, real bus, real caps, the real call_tool bridge — no mocks).\n`cargo test -p lb-host --test case_suite`: 108 passed.\n\nSCOPE: rubix-ai docs/scope/insights/case-money-producer-scope.md\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d9ec5e9aca6dfb1f8417386d7398b33e985c12b7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/d9ec5e9aca6dfb1f8417386d7398b33e985c12b7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc","verification":{"payload":"tree d9ec5e9aca6dfb1f8417386d7398b33e985c12b7\nparent 8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789375814 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789375814 +0700\n\nfeat(cases): case::open echoes the primary insight''s money, unit-checked (#207)\n\nNothing anywhere wrote `impact_rate`. `case::open` hardcoded it to `None`,\nno verb or reactor set it after, and no pack mentioned it — so the case\nplane''s COST column and two of its three money tiles were structurally\nblank on every node, for every case, for ever. The UI drew the rate line\ncorrectly and it simply never fired.\n\nThis is one more field through a path that already exists, not a new\nmechanism: `case::open` already echoes `category`/`site`/`scope`/`severity`\nfrom the primary insight, and now it echoes the rate from that insight''s\nshipped `analysis.estimated_impact` too.\n\nTHE UNIT IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED. `Quantity.unit` is free text and its own\ndoc comment warns that a consumer must \"group by `unit` and refuse to add\nunlike units\". `Case.impact_rate` is a bare f64 that the queue sums and the\nowner report ranks by, so a `{ value: 3.2, unit: \"sigma\" }` copied into it\ndoes not look wrong — it becomes $3.20/day in front of a customer. The value\nis therefore copied only when the unit parses as `<ISO4217>/day`; anything\nelse copies no rate and logs the refusal. A pattern, not an allowlist: a\ncurrency table is 180 rows lb has no business owning, and the shape is\nenough to separate money from `sigma`, `kL`, `%` and `kWh`.\n\nTier is `claimed`, never `modelled`. From this seam a rule''s own estimate\nand a tariff-priced one are the same JSON, so it can only state the weaker\nclaim honestly; `modelled` rolls into the owner headline and has to be\nearned by a producer-side signal that does not exist yet.\n\n`withheld` still wins, and is tested with both halves — a caveated case\nKEEPS its rate (it becomes showable when the caveat clears) and carries\n`impact_tier: withheld`, so the figure is suppressed by the tier and not by\na missing number. A change deleting the withhold would otherwise pass a test\nthat only checked the tier.\n\n`case.split` deliberately takes NO money: the new case is about a different\nprimary, so inheriting the source''s rate would double-count the same money\nacross two open cases.\n\nThe rate is frozen at open, like every facet except `caveated` —\n`plane_immunity.rs` already pinned that across 50 re-raises.\n\nTests: 8 unit over the parse table, 4 integration over a real booted node\n(real store, real bus, real caps, the real call_tool bridge — no mocks).\n`cargo test -p lb-host --test case_suite`: 108 passed.\n\nSCOPE: rubix-ai docs/scope/insights/case-money-producer-scope.md\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqp7VGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAABIQAIoecNoh5CWDIRQCZHXqjznv\nBpqxsIe65UcinlsimNGzNVY5I/OHIcx6t0hXu10m2/7aMRxlAzQkZFbv8uccHt7/\nFyhqsFRsWW3TA3cJZsc60MsFt9AcneC+LJfzwbsJ5TwhrqcxUy1FdBqUL6JzYhB9\nA0CwHLFaMwYwfXqfMFoxGUZmXI5KcZkhgZn2X+axPsLJ+AJZgyz74/W5MSjJNs79\nVqi536lY16aWIv8kLV6XEMcVqCamxsHOlLwm9V59XA8yI4zIjWseyQFLYBq4j6kO\n9b/gPFX3t90YJDqqitiRrGtKqnBjhUSIBZdYsVgvgPE4/d/QvykPOLtAHAj7xdK+\nOZ7unbgbO71dqocXovTdg9HK1Wrp7KNYwayiJ9QxW4PM0GDxP58Z4GcIlT0nc6bX\nfLiF+Wu6uh3el/0PPWJH32/jqP34NxRkqEd34D/+IarbPkvPGgSuPo5C5/StUwLT\nO9ZUNNnUHUXtRDANSo2C/Lvd1cCZ674kD9pKZeau9O8xMpu1DWRYBB9m/SMAcY9j\nSalS4EBKn3fHwstdUOw0BS0TaTejqRP72q3LtfW6Y2eKWDUwqYkq3151MYqIlFRL\nfk7WVpCedXS1OrKQZwMLotspMFrq/PtvaF4o3Ql5ydBVqEytjUZ04t0UbE/sZCZa\nnWRBKFJn29I2gK6OGJjR\n=BPgh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T08:50:14Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDQ2YTE2NDI4NWQ5YWQ3NGM5NzU1MTRjZTlhZGM4OWM4OWQ4ZDEwYmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79","sha":"8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79"}],"sha":"46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/46a164285d9ad74c975514ce9adc89c89d8d10bc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T03:10:39Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T03:10:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(insights): a write door for the workspace tag vocabulary (#204)\n\n* feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity\n\nA membership row holds an id. The drawer that renders the page therefore drew a\ncolumn of ULIDs — while an *unauthenticated* contractor on the token page saw a\nheadline, because that surface''s one verb carries the text with it.\n\nThe echo is resolved at READ time in the host, in one query. Not stored: a copy\nof a title goes stale the moment the insight is retitled and nothing would ever\nrepair it — the same argument `CaseRow.member_count` settles by deriving rather\nthan holding. Not in `lb-cases`: the insight plane is `lb-insights`, and making\nthe work plane a consumer of the detection plane for a display string is the\nwrong arrow. The host depends on both, so the join sits beside `case_list`''s\nresolution of who \"me\" is.\n\nOne query whatever the page size, through the `insight.case_id` back-reference\nthe grouping pass already writes for exactly this purpose (\"saves 200 extra\nreads\"). The two alternatives both lose: an `insight.get` per member is 200 reads\na page, and pushing it to the client is the same N+1 over the wire AND demands\n`insight.get` caps of a reader who may hold `case.get` and not — the two planes\nare two gates on purpose.\n\nAdditive on the wire. Both fields are `Option` and skipped when absent, so a\nclient written against the shipped shape is unaffected and one written against\nthe echo falls back to the id. A member whose insight is gone keeps its row with\nno title, and a store failure resolving the echo costs the labels, not the page:\nthe citations are the answer, the headlines are a decoration on them.\n\n`members_all` becomes public beside the paged read, for callers that want the\nMEMBERSHIP rather than the drawer''s page — merge, split, and the host''s two echo\nwalks, all of which were asking for `MAX_MEMBER_PAGE` with no cursor and would\notherwise now pay for titles nobody on those paths reads.\n\nTests: the echo arrives on every row of a merged case and of a 12-member one;\nthe page cursor is still the `insight_id` and not the echo; a deleted insight\nleaves its citation titleless without disturbing its neighbour. That last one\nneeds `mcp:insight.delete:call`, which is deliberately NOT in the suite''s shared\n`ALL` — `insight.delete` gates on itself so the read/act caps do not imply it,\nand widening the fixture would erase that distinction for every test in the file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* docs(cases): the tool catalog says case.members carries the title\n\nThe catalog line is what an agent reads to decide whether it needs a second\ncall. \"the insights a case cites, paged\" invites exactly the `insight.get`\nper member the echo exists to remove.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* feat(insights): a write door for the workspace tag vocabulary\n\n`tag_vocab` is an engine-owned record — `raise` refuses a category outside\n`values`, the caveat stamp reads `gates`, and the case plane keys its SLA matrix\non the same set — and it had no verb. A workspace could only author one through\nthe generic `store.write`, which is a UI writing a record the engine then has to\ntrust.\n\nThat is not hypothetical. The downstream entity grid types every column as text,\nso authoring a row through it writes `[\"a\"]` as the STRING `\"[\\\"a\\\"]\"` — which\ndecodes to nothing and silently disarms the validation. The same shape already\nproduces `party` rows that `party.list` drops without a word.\n\n`insight.vocab.list` / `insight.vocab.set`, both admin, one cap per verb.\n\n**`validate_vocab` is the point.** It judges shape, never meaning (rule 10 — what\na category IS remains the workspace''s business): a key that is one record-id\nsegment, non-blank values, no duplicates, a bounded set, and **`gates ⊆ values`**.\nThat last rule is the one with teeth — a gating value nothing can be raised as\narms a caveat that never fires, and a caveat that never fires is indistinguishable\nfrom a workspace with nothing to caveat. Validation runs BEFORE the write, so a\nrejected declaration leaves the live one exactly as it was (asserted).\n\n**No delete verb.** Declaring an empty `values` list re-opens the key, because\nlb''s rule is that an undeclared vocabulary validates nothing — \"no row\" and \"a row\ndeclaring nothing\" are the same statement, and a second verb could only disagree\nwith that one.\n\n**A cap per verb and NO gate alias.** The first cut gated the roster on the write\ncap and aliased it in `tool_gate.rs` — which was cleverness buying a\nshipped-but-unusable risk, and it pushed that file (already at 399 of its 400-line\nlimit) over. `policy.sla.*` and `party.*` are already a cap per verb and need no\narm; following them removed the alias, the risk and the violation together.\n\nTests (`case/caveat_vocab_door.rs`, 6): both halves Denied for the queue''s caps; a\ndeclaration round-trips AND the raise path then enforces it (a door writing a row\nnothing reads would be decorative); a gate outside the values is refused and the\nroster is unchanged after; each shape rule refuses with its own sentence; an empty\ndeclaration re-opens the key; and a declaration does not cross the workspace wall\n— nor govern the next workspace.\n\nOne bug fixed on the way in: `list_vocab` first read `row.data` without unwrapping\nthe store''s `{data, rev}` envelope. Every field on `TagVocab` is `serde(default)`,\nso that decoded silently into an all-default vocabulary — the roster came back the\nright LENGTH and said nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2fb116cf70eda82e2d81fc47327e088e0a62d3f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/2fb116cf70eda82e2d81fc47327e088e0a62d3f5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79","verification":{"payload":"tree 2fb116cf70eda82e2d81fc47327e088e0a62d3f5\nparent 5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789355439 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789355439 +0800\n\nfeat(insights): a write door for the workspace tag vocabulary (#204)\n\n* feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity\n\nA membership row holds an id. The drawer that renders the page therefore drew a\ncolumn of ULIDs — while an *unauthenticated* contractor on the token page saw a\nheadline, because that surface''s one verb carries the text with it.\n\nThe echo is resolved at READ time in the host, in one query. Not stored: a copy\nof a title goes stale the moment the insight is retitled and nothing would ever\nrepair it — the same argument `CaseRow.member_count` settles by deriving rather\nthan holding. Not in `lb-cases`: the insight plane is `lb-insights`, and making\nthe work plane a consumer of the detection plane for a display string is the\nwrong arrow. The host depends on both, so the join sits beside `case_list`''s\nresolution of who \"me\" is.\n\nOne query whatever the page size, through the `insight.case_id` back-reference\nthe grouping pass already writes for exactly this purpose (\"saves 200 extra\nreads\"). The two alternatives both lose: an `insight.get` per member is 200 reads\na page, and pushing it to the client is the same N+1 over the wire AND demands\n`insight.get` caps of a reader who may hold `case.get` and not — the two planes\nare two gates on purpose.\n\nAdditive on the wire. Both fields are `Option` and skipped when absent, so a\nclient written against the shipped shape is unaffected and one written against\nthe echo falls back to the id. A member whose insight is gone keeps its row with\nno title, and a store failure resolving the echo costs the labels, not the page:\nthe citations are the answer, the headlines are a decoration on them.\n\n`members_all` becomes public beside the paged read, for callers that want the\nMEMBERSHIP rather than the drawer''s page — merge, split, and the host''s two echo\nwalks, all of which were asking for `MAX_MEMBER_PAGE` with no cursor and would\notherwise now pay for titles nobody on those paths reads.\n\nTests: the echo arrives on every row of a merged case and of a 12-member one;\nthe page cursor is still the `insight_id` and not the echo; a deleted insight\nleaves its citation titleless without disturbing its neighbour. That last one\nneeds `mcp:insight.delete:call`, which is deliberately NOT in the suite''s shared\n`ALL` — `insight.delete` gates on itself so the read/act caps do not imply it,\nand widening the fixture would erase that distinction for every test in the file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* docs(cases): the tool catalog says case.members carries the title\n\nThe catalog line is what an agent reads to decide whether it needs a second\ncall. \"the insights a case cites, paged\" invites exactly the `insight.get`\nper member the echo exists to remove.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* feat(insights): a write door for the workspace tag vocabulary\n\n`tag_vocab` is an engine-owned record — `raise` refuses a category outside\n`values`, the caveat stamp reads `gates`, and the case plane keys its SLA matrix\non the same set — and it had no verb. A workspace could only author one through\nthe generic `store.write`, which is a UI writing a record the engine then has to\ntrust.\n\nThat is not hypothetical. The downstream entity grid types every column as text,\nso authoring a row through it writes `[\"a\"]` as the STRING `\"[\\\"a\\\"]\"` — which\ndecodes to nothing and silently disarms the validation. The same shape already\nproduces `party` rows that `party.list` drops without a word.\n\n`insight.vocab.list` / `insight.vocab.set`, both admin, one cap per verb.\n\n**`validate_vocab` is the point.** It judges shape, never meaning (rule 10 — what\na category IS remains the workspace''s business): a key that is one record-id\nsegment, non-blank values, no duplicates, a bounded set, and **`gates ⊆ values`**.\nThat last rule is the one with teeth — a gating value nothing can be raised as\narms a caveat that never fires, and a caveat that never fires is indistinguishable\nfrom a workspace with nothing to caveat. Validation runs BEFORE the write, so a\nrejected declaration leaves the live one exactly as it was (asserted).\n\n**No delete verb.** Declaring an empty `values` list re-opens the key, because\nlb''s rule is that an undeclared vocabulary validates nothing — \"no row\" and \"a row\ndeclaring nothing\" are the same statement, and a second verb could only disagree\nwith that one.\n\n**A cap per verb and NO gate alias.** The first cut gated the roster on the write\ncap and aliased it in `tool_gate.rs` — which was cleverness buying a\nshipped-but-unusable risk, and it pushed that file (already at 399 of its 400-line\nlimit) over. `policy.sla.*` and `party.*` are already a cap per verb and need no\narm; following them removed the alias, the risk and the violation together.\n\nTests (`case/caveat_vocab_door.rs`, 6): both halves Denied for the queue''s caps; a\ndeclaration round-trips AND the raise path then enforces it (a door writing a row\nnothing reads would be decorative); a gate outside the values is refused and the\nroster is unchanged after; each shape rule refuses with its own sentence; an empty\ndeclaration re-opens the key; and a declaration does not cross the workspace wall\n— nor govern the next workspace.\n\nOne bug fixed on the way in: `list_vocab` first read `row.data` without unwrapping\nthe store''s `{data, rev}` envelope. Every field on `TagVocab` is `serde(default)`,\nso that decoded silently into an all-default vocabulary — the roster came back the\nright LENGTH and said nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqp2WvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6dAQAEgmc7sgcgt2cg7EmIxsLa/D\nJGjvju+AjPiFmS7rm1+AYgddyTwW3ro+oVBKdSfjMlNVl0yRPTEbdOOyOS3xSa3Z\nuJAALhtQy7n0Outti9i8bJX+0RFZcohE0gwfA1NxONtGHmJVGgO/m6Fu+UyLO3YO\n1zlqLMhGbHro6k7DSjd8p3DQvkDX20k7/Bc4v6ctl76KDFGvhpfO7bJH4GgSGfAS\niOuhIb3ZeZFbPSMz6jsJ94m8y1tygcZiwfiauaf22EoBXKKA96tg3oxUFLl/oGXY\n+hHCUyOdr5RT3AzU1w9iagKaTGm1cwUbgGVlcyydgGeUc6RHolELraSjjP5OtLYJ\n2A4dLz5IhNkpx8r1tkJv6CftOvkaONcyRllic9/5kCO97+8wBrZVw6KDOmoom0y7\n2qrKRVf8bY+LdloL82pz+xJEGUxtAVTQKK1FaAYmKyEpUDh1YkWVeZgZPg6U8IMW\nuEtvBStFIag6FomTnm6JldVUWE/ktdy69o0k4QKht/3OrR9uXhGqVhDEUoEj/bFb\nbrOgZy4DLolsId9XRq3PLTEEHIKRWRnnFGXBywcfUoGl6VAdO9lDyx/EBH51jtoq\nC1n9whYpomt6YZ/lzAd4PpDj6apV88UEGib1+TuQNv/y0mZwc8quTLbe0eHd/r4C\nGF+k4AwoqlnLUekMV6xL\n=70KF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T03:10:40Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDg0NjNhZDA2ZTVjMjE0Y2ZiMmEzNWZmZjRkNWU0ZjAzZjRlNDJhNzk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23","sha":"5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23"}],"sha":"8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/8463ad06e5c214cfb2a35fff4d5e4f03f4e42a79"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T03:08:22Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T03:08:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(cases): retire and delete, for parties and service policies (#205)\n\n* feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity\n\nA membership row holds an id. The drawer that renders the page therefore drew a\ncolumn of ULIDs — while an *unauthenticated* contractor on the token page saw a\nheadline, because that surface''s one verb carries the text with it.\n\nThe echo is resolved at READ time in the host, in one query. Not stored: a copy\nof a title goes stale the moment the insight is retitled and nothing would ever\nrepair it — the same argument `CaseRow.member_count` settles by deriving rather\nthan holding. Not in `lb-cases`: the insight plane is `lb-insights`, and making\nthe work plane a consumer of the detection plane for a display string is the\nwrong arrow. The host depends on both, so the join sits beside `case_list`''s\nresolution of who \"me\" is.\n\nOne query whatever the page size, through the `insight.case_id` back-reference\nthe grouping pass already writes for exactly this purpose (\"saves 200 extra\nreads\"). The two alternatives both lose: an `insight.get` per member is 200 reads\na page, and pushing it to the client is the same N+1 over the wire AND demands\n`insight.get` caps of a reader who may hold `case.get` and not — the two planes\nare two gates on purpose.\n\nAdditive on the wire. Both fields are `Option` and skipped when absent, so a\nclient written against the shipped shape is unaffected and one written against\nthe echo falls back to the id. A member whose insight is gone keeps its row with\nno title, and a store failure resolving the echo costs the labels, not the page:\nthe citations are the answer, the headlines are a decoration on them.\n\n`members_all` becomes public beside the paged read, for callers that want the\nMEMBERSHIP rather than the drawer''s page — merge, split, and the host''s two echo\nwalks, all of which were asking for `MAX_MEMBER_PAGE` with no cursor and would\notherwise now pay for titles nobody on those paths reads.\n\nTests: the echo arrives on every row of a merged case and of a 12-member one;\nthe page cursor is still the `insight_id` and not the echo; a deleted insight\nleaves its citation titleless without disturbing its neighbour. That last one\nneeds `mcp:insight.delete:call`, which is deliberately NOT in the suite''s shared\n`ALL` — `insight.delete` gates on itself so the read/act caps do not imply it,\nand widening the fixture would erase that distinction for every test in the file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* docs(cases): the tool catalog says case.members carries the title\n\nThe catalog line is what an agent reads to decide whether it needs a second\ncall. \"the insights a case cites, paged\" invites exactly the `insight.get`\nper member the echo exists to remove.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* feat(insights): a write door for the workspace tag vocabulary\n\n`tag_vocab` is an engine-owned record — `raise` refuses a category outside\n`values`, the caveat stamp reads `gates`, and the case plane keys its SLA matrix\non the same set — and it had no verb. A workspace could only author one through\nthe generic `store.write`, which is a UI writing a record the engine then has to\ntrust.\n\nThat is not hypothetical. The downstream entity grid types every column as text,\nso authoring a row through it writes `[\"a\"]` as the STRING `\"[\\\"a\\\"]\"` — which\ndecodes to nothing and silently disarms the validation. The same shape already\nproduces `party` rows that `party.list` drops without a word.\n\n`insight.vocab.list` / `insight.vocab.set`, both admin, one cap per verb.\n\n**`validate_vocab` is the point.** It judges shape, never meaning (rule 10 — what\na category IS remains the workspace''s business): a key that is one record-id\nsegment, non-blank values, no duplicates, a bounded set, and **`gates ⊆ values`**.\nThat last rule is the one with teeth — a gating value nothing can be raised as\narms a caveat that never fires, and a caveat that never fires is indistinguishable\nfrom a workspace with nothing to caveat. Validation runs BEFORE the write, so a\nrejected declaration leaves the live one exactly as it was (asserted).\n\n**No delete verb.** Declaring an empty `values` list re-opens the key, because\nlb''s rule is that an undeclared vocabulary validates nothing — \"no row\" and \"a row\ndeclaring nothing\" are the same statement, and a second verb could only disagree\nwith that one.\n\n**A cap per verb and NO gate alias.** The first cut gated the roster on the write\ncap and aliased it in `tool_gate.rs` — which was cleverness buying a\nshipped-but-unusable risk, and it pushed that file (already at 399 of its 400-line\nlimit) over. `policy.sla.*` and `party.*` are already a cap per verb and need no\narm; following them removed the alias, the risk and the violation together.\n\nTests (`case/caveat_vocab_door.rs`, 6): both halves Denied for the queue''s caps; a\ndeclaration round-trips AND the raise path then enforces it (a door writing a row\nnothing reads would be decorative); a gate outside the values is refused and the\nroster is unchanged after; each shape rule refuses with its own sentence; an empty\ndeclaration re-opens the key; and a declaration does not cross the workspace wall\n— nor govern the next workspace.\n\nOne bug fixed on the way in: `list_vocab` first read `row.data` without unwrapping\nthe store''s `{data, rev}` envelope. Every field on `TagVocab` is `serde(default)`,\nso that decoded silently into an all-default vocabulary — the roster came back the\nright LENGTH and said nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* feat(cases): retire and delete, for parties and service policies\n\nThe roster had no way out. `party.upsert` and `policy.sla.set` shipped with no\ncounterpart, so a workspace could add a contractor and never remove one — the\ndownstream admin page had to reach for raw `store.delete`, which is exactly the\ndoor that page exists to stop using.\n\nBoth gestures, because they answer different questions.\n\n**`active: bool` retires a row that has been USED.** A retired party leaves\n`party.list`''s default read and `case.request.send` REFUSES it, while `party_get`\nstill resolves it — so every request already sent keeps rendering their name for\nthe history and the nudge ladder. That asymmetry is the whole feature: a\ncontractor you stop using does not stop having been the contractor on forty\nclosed cases. A retired policy is skipped by `policy_match` (enforced in the\nladder, not only in the settings list — a flag the resolver ignored would be\ndisabled in name only), while cases it already clocked keep their stamped\ndeadlines.\n\n**`party.delete` erases a row with nothing to preserve, and REFUSES one that has\nhistory**, naming the count and pointing at retirement. The alternative is a\n`case_request` whose `party_id` resolves to nothing, which turns \"who did we send\nthis to?\" into a dangling id. `policy.sla.delete` is unconditional by contrast,\nand the asymmetry is deliberate: a case carries the deadlines it was stamped with\nrather than re-deriving them, so nothing is orphaned.\n\n`active` defaults to TRUE via `#[serde(default = \"yes\")]`, NOT `#[serde(default)]`\n— a bare bool default is `false`, which would have read every row written before\nthis field as retired and emptied every workspace''s roster on upgrade. There is a\ntest that writes a flagless row straight to the store and asserts it lists.\n\nBoth deletes take their own cap rather than aliasing the writer''s: they are the\ndestructive half and the distinction is meaningful, unlike `case.merge`/`split`.\nThat also kept `tool_gate.rs` — already at 399 of its 400-line limit — out of the\nchange, which is the second time this session that the size rule pushed toward\nthe better design rather than away from it.\n\n`case/tool.rs` crossed 400 too, and split along a real seam rather than an\narbitrary one: `tool_config.rs` now holds the six `party.*` / `policy.sla.*`\narms. Those are admin settings ABOUT the plane; `case.*` is work done ON a case.\nDifferent authority, different audience, different rate of change.\n\nTests (`case/request_roster_lifecycle.rs`, 8): the full retire → invisible →\nrefused → still-readable-by-id → re-enable walk; a flagless row reads as active;\ndelete refuses a party with history and the roster is unchanged after; delete\nerases an unused one and is idempotent; the authoring caps buy neither delete;\na retired policy is skipped by the ladder; a policy deletes unconditionally.\n\n`case_suite` 104, `lb-cases` 58, catalog + authz green. No new file-layout\nviolation.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2fb116cf70eda82e2d81fc47327e088e0a62d3f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/2fb116cf70eda82e2d81fc47327e088e0a62d3f5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23","verification":{"payload":"tree 2fb116cf70eda82e2d81fc47327e088e0a62d3f5\nparent ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789355302 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789355302 +0800\n\nfeat(cases): retire and delete, for parties and service policies (#205)\n\n* feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity\n\nA membership row holds an id. The drawer that renders the page therefore drew a\ncolumn of ULIDs — while an *unauthenticated* contractor on the token page saw a\nheadline, because that surface''s one verb carries the text with it.\n\nThe echo is resolved at READ time in the host, in one query. Not stored: a copy\nof a title goes stale the moment the insight is retitled and nothing would ever\nrepair it — the same argument `CaseRow.member_count` settles by deriving rather\nthan holding. Not in `lb-cases`: the insight plane is `lb-insights`, and making\nthe work plane a consumer of the detection plane for a display string is the\nwrong arrow. The host depends on both, so the join sits beside `case_list`''s\nresolution of who \"me\" is.\n\nOne query whatever the page size, through the `insight.case_id` back-reference\nthe grouping pass already writes for exactly this purpose (\"saves 200 extra\nreads\"). The two alternatives both lose: an `insight.get` per member is 200 reads\na page, and pushing it to the client is the same N+1 over the wire AND demands\n`insight.get` caps of a reader who may hold `case.get` and not — the two planes\nare two gates on purpose.\n\nAdditive on the wire. Both fields are `Option` and skipped when absent, so a\nclient written against the shipped shape is unaffected and one written against\nthe echo falls back to the id. A member whose insight is gone keeps its row with\nno title, and a store failure resolving the echo costs the labels, not the page:\nthe citations are the answer, the headlines are a decoration on them.\n\n`members_all` becomes public beside the paged read, for callers that want the\nMEMBERSHIP rather than the drawer''s page — merge, split, and the host''s two echo\nwalks, all of which were asking for `MAX_MEMBER_PAGE` with no cursor and would\notherwise now pay for titles nobody on those paths reads.\n\nTests: the echo arrives on every row of a merged case and of a 12-member one;\nthe page cursor is still the `insight_id` and not the echo; a deleted insight\nleaves its citation titleless without disturbing its neighbour. That last one\nneeds `mcp:insight.delete:call`, which is deliberately NOT in the suite''s shared\n`ALL` — `insight.delete` gates on itself so the read/act caps do not imply it,\nand widening the fixture would erase that distinction for every test in the file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* docs(cases): the tool catalog says case.members carries the title\n\nThe catalog line is what an agent reads to decide whether it needs a second\ncall. \"the insights a case cites, paged\" invites exactly the `insight.get`\nper member the echo exists to remove.\n\nCo-Authored-By: C