`policy.sla.*` and `party.*` are already a cap per verb and need no\narm; following them removed the alias, the risk and the violation together.\n\nTests (`case/caveat_vocab_door.rs`, 6): both halves Denied for the queue''s caps; a\ndeclaration round-trips AND the raise path then enforces it (a door writing a row\nnothing reads would be decorative); a gate outside the values is refused and the\nroster is unchanged after; each shape rule refuses with its own sentence; an empty\ndeclaration re-opens the key; and a declaration does not cross the workspace wall\n— nor govern the next workspace.\n\nOne bug fixed on the way in: `list_vocab` first read `row.data` without unwrapping\nthe store''s `{data, rev}` envelope. Every field on `TagVocab` is `serde(default)`,\nso that decoded silently into an all-default vocabulary — the roster came back the\nright LENGTH and said nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* feat(cases): retire and delete, for parties and service policies\n\nThe roster had no way out. `party.upsert` and `policy.sla.set` shipped with no\ncounterpart, so a workspace could add a contractor and never remove one — the\ndownstream admin page had to reach for raw `store.delete`, which is exactly the\ndoor that page exists to stop using.\n\nBoth gestures, because they answer different questions.\n\n**`active: bool` retires a row that has been USED.** A retired party leaves\n`party.list`''s default read and `case.request.send` REFUSES it, while `party_get`\nstill resolves it — so every request already sent keeps rendering their name for\nthe history and the nudge ladder. That asymmetry is the whole feature: a\ncontractor you stop using does not stop having been the contractor on forty\nclosed cases. A retired policy is skipped by `policy_match` (enforced in the\nladder, not only in the settings list — a flag the resolver ignored would be\ndisabled in name only), while cases it already clocked keep their stamped\ndeadlines.\n\n**`party.delete` erases a row with nothing to preserve, and REFUSES one that has\nhistory**, naming the count and pointing at retirement. The alternative is a\n`case_request` whose `party_id` resolves to nothing, which turns \"who did we send\nthis to?\" into a dangling id. `policy.sla.delete` is unconditional by contrast,\nand the asymmetry is deliberate: a case carries the deadlines it was stamped with\nrather than re-deriving them, so nothing is orphaned.\n\n`active` defaults to TRUE via `#[serde(default = \"yes\")]`, NOT `#[serde(default)]`\n— a bare bool default is `false`, which would have read every row written before\nthis field as retired and emptied every workspace''s roster on upgrade. There is a\ntest that writes a flagless row straight to the store and asserts it lists.\n\nBoth deletes take their own cap rather than aliasing the writer''s: they are the\ndestructive half and the distinction is meaningful, unlike `case.merge`/`split`.\nThat also kept `tool_gate.rs` — already at 399 of its 400-line limit — out of the\nchange, which is the second time this session that the size rule pushed toward\nthe better design rather than away from it.\n\n`case/tool.rs` crossed 400 too, and split along a real seam rather than an\narbitrary one: `tool_config.rs` now holds the six `party.*` / `policy.sla.*`\narms. Those are admin settings ABOUT the plane; `case.*` is work done ON a case.\nDifferent authority, different audience, different rate of change.\n\nTests (`case/request_roster_lifecycle.rs`, 8): the full retire → invisible →\nrefused → still-readable-by-id → re-enable walk; a flagless row reads as active;\ndelete refuses a party with history and the roster is unchanged after; delete\nerases an unused one and is idempotent; the authoring caps buy neither delete;\na retired policy is skipped by the ladder; a policy deletes unconditionally.\n\n`case_suite` 104, `lb-cases` 58, catalog + authz green. No new file-layout\nviolation.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqp2UmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQIcQADgmpdeqkqEUfnsPQOeXAZX6\niPOOndnT5D31EfjVJliGewbeIEcyis/7PPrdmYq2DRGP5pPFwSvZnAfBkizH4Aju\nFvypgW3cah/ltpi+nwRqUwSCFf80SHCnvppiJjL/BIvyM7Y6Gw/E7zs0Ykv36DvA\nKF4MSMmxIKdWXJzw94nfuSxIhTUZBQYFBNFYe07kFhTP6XaxH9hfdvPw1rPTKx6e\nGge81rZMx8b83GrvE0OSGMYtOf+W6jhS8J7/GcvxEf5DtSz14Sm55j3J71VBefw/\n2QHjE6ymz/et+wIdvQO3dL2TzildHn42CJ9/+gwakKhfi3RoJAk3f2x/zzDKIeVc\n3aFqzsojwPBzjNKUnzm+lPuG8wiDwoz7Qn19UHZ5Zsw6X2nOLurso17E5d5J32iy\nRQVth7BxTVyGAiIs4zopeShuHIhSXCYU4WjtK4uFYm44JHIHql1NN+s6KX1czFWB\n/rKHYzgtcIEFHc0VP7+b4KBa5dZro1oloKUheDd6iD84J4wHPCjGUfFBDq3gEgj0\nQ48WoeiPjHhdmjGd6YmG0UhUpGlVWfv9ffM0PnoF9S/ie9V5EpdM0faHctxqHURX\nh9uxyHERmUIJBAmn36fbrIaLqXfVSMLrwrjcQXuNkQTxE0xm8Pt7Qf2rcoWhRSyz\nZIYyOAIBmZ0w75Z6hiK/\n=SKxV\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T03:08:22Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDVjZGVlN2JkOTdjYThiZGYzNmRmNWJkMDU3OGYyZTFiNTMxODJhMjM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9","sha":"ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9"}],"sha":"5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/5cdee7bd97ca8bdf36df5bd0578f2e1b53182a23"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-14T02:59:32Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-14T02:59:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity (#203)\n\n* feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity\n\nA membership row holds an id. The drawer that renders the page therefore drew a\ncolumn of ULIDs — while an *unauthenticated* contractor on the token page saw a\nheadline, because that surface''s one verb carries the text with it.\n\nThe echo is resolved at READ time in the host, in one query. Not stored: a copy\nof a title goes stale the moment the insight is retitled and nothing would ever\nrepair it — the same argument `CaseRow.member_count` settles by deriving rather\nthan holding. Not in `lb-cases`: the insight plane is `lb-insights`, and making\nthe work plane a consumer of the detection plane for a display string is the\nwrong arrow. The host depends on both, so the join sits beside `case_list`''s\nresolution of who \"me\" is.\n\nOne query whatever the page size, through the `insight.case_id` back-reference\nthe grouping pass already writes for exactly this purpose (\"saves 200 extra\nreads\"). The two alternatives both lose: an `insight.get` per member is 200 reads\na page, and pushing it to the client is the same N+1 over the wire AND demands\n`insight.get` caps of a reader who may hold `case.get` and not — the two planes\nare two gates on purpose.\n\nAdditive on the wire. Both fields are `Option` and skipped when absent, so a\nclient written against the shipped shape is unaffected and one written against\nthe echo falls back to the id. A member whose insight is gone keeps its row with\nno title, and a store failure resolving the echo costs the labels, not the page:\nthe citations are the answer, the headlines are a decoration on them.\n\n`members_all` becomes public beside the paged read, for callers that want the\nMEMBERSHIP rather than the drawer''s page — merge, split, and the host''s two echo\nwalks, all of which were asking for `MAX_MEMBER_PAGE` with no cursor and would\notherwise now pay for titles nobody on those paths reads.\n\nTests: the echo arrives on every row of a merged case and of a 12-member one;\nthe page cursor is still the `insight_id` and not the echo; a deleted insight\nleaves its citation titleless without disturbing its neighbour. That last one\nneeds `mcp:insight.delete:call`, which is deliberately NOT in the suite''s shared\n`ALL` — `insight.delete` gates on itself so the read/act caps do not imply it,\nand widening the fixture would erase that distinction for every test in the file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* docs(cases): the tool catalog says case.members carries the title\n\nThe catalog line is what an agent reads to decide whether it needs a second\ncall. \"the insights a case cites, paged\" invites exactly the `insight.get`\nper member the echo exists to remove.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2e657fc6a56c2d54bd884a0aa5213f91c4d354be","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/2e657fc6a56c2d54bd884a0aa5213f91c4d354be"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9","verification":{"payload":"tree 2e657fc6a56c2d54bd884a0aa5213f91c4d354be\nparent 29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789354772 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789354772 +0800\n\nfeat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity (#203)\n\n* feat(cases): case.members echoes each cited insight''s title and severity\n\nA membership row holds an id. The drawer that renders the page therefore drew a\ncolumn of ULIDs — while an *unauthenticated* contractor on the token page saw a\nheadline, because that surface''s one verb carries the text with it.\n\nThe echo is resolved at READ time in the host, in one query. Not stored: a copy\nof a title goes stale the moment the insight is retitled and nothing would ever\nrepair it — the same argument `CaseRow.member_count` settles by deriving rather\nthan holding. Not in `lb-cases`: the insight plane is `lb-insights`, and making\nthe work plane a consumer of the detection plane for a display string is the\nwrong arrow. The host depends on both, so the join sits beside `case_list`''s\nresolution of who \"me\" is.\n\nOne query whatever the page size, through the `insight.case_id` back-reference\nthe grouping pass already writes for exactly this purpose (\"saves 200 extra\nreads\"). The two alternatives both lose: an `insight.get` per member is 200 reads\na page, and pushing it to the client is the same N+1 over the wire AND demands\n`insight.get` caps of a reader who may hold `case.get` and not — the two planes\nare two gates on purpose.\n\nAdditive on the wire. Both fields are `Option` and skipped when absent, so a\nclient written against the shipped shape is unaffected and one written against\nthe echo falls back to the id. A member whose insight is gone keeps its row with\nno title, and a store failure resolving the echo costs the labels, not the page:\nthe citations are the answer, the headlines are a decoration on them.\n\n`members_all` becomes public beside the paged read, for callers that want the\nMEMBERSHIP rather than the drawer''s page — merge, split, and the host''s two echo\nwalks, all of which were asking for `MAX_MEMBER_PAGE` with no cursor and would\notherwise now pay for titles nobody on those paths reads.\n\nTests: the echo arrives on every row of a merged case and of a 12-member one;\nthe page cursor is still the `insight_id` and not the echo; a deleted insight\nleaves its citation titleless without disturbing its neighbour. That last one\nneeds `mcp:insight.delete:call`, which is deliberately NOT in the suite''s shared\n`ALL` — `insight.delete` gates on itself so the read/act caps do not imply it,\nand widening the fixture would erase that distinction for every test in the file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n* docs(cases): the tool catalog says case.members carries the title\n\nThe catalog line is what an agent reads to decide whether it needs a second\ncall. \"the insights a case cites, paged\" invites exactly the `insight.get`\nper member the echo exists to remove.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xxivcJUTm9MsJv1rXWcmz\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqp2MUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsWsQAAdxRucByRzY0nOHDABwvQUW\nsC6Fjv20E3+cODucN7i0nZkcd031AZoqJxPkYLlOVrQuUusrM3w04RZ7tJiIOiNl\nKVBwymvAKGjBSjpjgA4Yz+zEC1qL0xaMhD2KK+MzV4U6dxbYRpBNenSCYxvy0Yuj\nC58jj8KRU7k/5B8KeDbE1omqEj+L+Ns57Ku6NSNWcQ8F9+iBOfQyvFUACOtLqb9s\nFPbPVIY66dQofnSX8WnX8UkLbrR908/5t3uWldkYFq41gHiE2yLMXwchaIMpIeW2\nKB5ik3PH8UAz1T4rgHRySuvb1p9n8L8XjjxQqECR1sXzGhBf4vfcH2ZxUWD3b1mF\n6p2eCzimDOD8xaW7gXoEPzQOLA2gPRztk7nM8lz/Pq070e4sMQobfyPP5RrcoYIz\nJ+qr9mTYbsop7x1LLY/QraO4oSjIa1mR1QiekXw7oT0Is7RzMHPQ7NsJNfaS+X8B\nRuqmPo1/bjXNcr0gBeS2ArZgLJDZL7YBMqCR/EqsmkZEpzE16sQ79kQju0jPEUEx\nnMMIihTLxlh2VWGbxmx1v4iUy4FRucmvOlawLMDCR3kipalBXHvMfg4hELt9Ex+x\nIdqB7kg0dUaEJGT1/3QUkMx2HoOsSny11kXdqQBH8PeUA5tfUkBc1mqoz/Plg5kY\nnEKrJnPjRwOuWRLCZ4Ai\n=TGna\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-14T02:59:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKGVjNDU0YTZiOTYzOWM5YmRiZmI4OTZkMzUxZmI0ZmJlNWI2ZmMwYzk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc","sha":"29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc"}],"sha":"ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/ec454a6b9639c9bdbfb896d351fb4fbe5b6fc0c9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-11T07:24:45Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-11T07:24:45Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(agent,pack): reach agent.invoke over MCP, vary a scheduled job_id, and let a pack declare its schedule (#198)\n\n* fix(agent): dispatch `agent.invoke` over MCP, and vary a scheduled run''s job_id\n\n`agent.invoke` was built on every layer except the MCP dispatch table: the cap\n(`mcp:agent.invoke:call`), the `tools.catalog` descriptor and the routed edge→hub\npath (`invoke_remote`) all existed, but `call_agent_tool` had no `invoke` match, so\na call over `/mcp/call` fell through to `NotFound` and answered an opaque\n`403 \"no such tool\"` to a caller HOLDING the grant — a missing `match` arm that\nreads as a permissions bug.\n\nIt survived because every shipped caller uses another transport (browser →\n`POST /agent/invoke`, palette → `postAgent`, channel worker → the host fn). The\nfirst caller that needs the bridge is a REMINDER: an `Action::McpTool` fires\nthrough the `call_tool` chokepoint and has no browser, so \"run the agent on a\nschedule\" was unbuildable.\n\nThe new arm mirrors the HTTP route exactly (`reachable_tools` menu,\n`invoke_via_runtime` with `runtime = None`, same persona precedence, same opaque\nerror mapping) and adds no authority — `authorize_invoke` still runs inside and\nthe run stays bounded by `agent ∩ caller`. Two deliberate differences: `job_id` is\nrequired (a derived id would be identical on every firing), and `agent.invoke` is\nfiltered out of the menu handed to the model, since the dispatcher applies no\ndepth ceiling to host-native verbs.\n\nThe same work exposed a second defect: reminder args are static and the loop is\nidempotent on `job_id`, so a scheduled run would replay its first answer forever,\nnever calling the model. Fixed generically in `reminder/range.rs` — the module\nthat already owns fire-time payload substitution — with a `{{fire_ts}}` token\nreplaced by the fire clock''s epoch seconds in any string value. Action-agnostic\nper rule 10, and additive: a payload without the token is unchanged.\n\nProven live, not only unit-tested: `agent.invoke` over `/mcp/call` answered\n`{\"answer\":\"ok\",\"jobId\":\"mcp-real-1\"}` in 9.6s driven by a local offline Qwen3,\nand a per-minute probe reminder wrote memory slug `probe-1788764348`.\nRED-then-green verified: disabling the arm fails the guard with its message.\n\nTests: agent_invoke_mcp_test (6, incl. the regression guard and capability-deny),\n4 new range.rs unit tests. agent_suite 173/173, lb-host lib 534/534, all five\nreminder suites green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(pack): a pack declares its own schedule — the `reminders:` block\n\nEvery other pack section seeds a thing that sits inert until something drives\nit: a rule does nothing until `rules.run`, a channel until someone posts. A pack\nthat ships seven FDD rules and no schedule ships a product that detects nothing\nuntil an operator wires the cron by hand — off-manifest, undocumented, outside\nthe receipt, and not reproducible on a fresh box. The schedule IS the driver,\nand it was the one part of a domain pack that could not be declared.\n\nAdded on the `retention:` model, the closest precedent:\n\n- `lb_packs::manifest_reminder` — a dependency-free MIRROR of the\n  `reminder.create` args. `lb-reminders` depends on `lb-store` and `lb-packs` is\n  the pure half, so reusing `Action` would invert the layering. The cost is that\n  `action_kind` is a `String`, which is what makes the lint necessary.\n- `lb_packs::validate_reminder` — the lint the mirror pays for: an unknown\n  `action_kind` against the closed set, a kind missing the fields it needs, a\n  6-field quartz cron, and — as a WARNING — a static `job_id`.\n- `pack/reminder_action.rs` + a `Kind::Reminder` arm driving the same\n  `reminder_create` the public verb calls.\n\n**No cap smuggling.** The reminder is stored with the applying principal, and\nevery firing re-resolves that principal''s caps at the `call_tool` chokepoint. A\n`pack.apply` without `mcp:reminder.create:call` gets `denied` on the reminder\nobjects and an honest partial receipt; a pack-seeded schedule carries no\nauthority its admin lacks, and revoking that admin''s grant stops it.\n\nThe `job_id` warning is a warning rather than an error because the manifest\ncannot know which verbs are idempotent on which field. `job_id` without\n`{{fire_ts}}` is the one shape where the intent is unmistakable — and it is the\nexact replay trap the previous commit fixed: a static id returns the first run''s\nanswer forever, a frozen agent that looks perfectly alive.\n\nReminders plan LAST: a schedule firing `rules.run` is only meaningful once the\nrule it names exists.\n\nTests: `pack_reminders_test` (a pack seeds a schedule that will really fire —\nlistable AND with a `nextAttemptTs` past the apply clock, and `{{fire_ts}}`\nstored UNSUBSTITUTED, since baking the apply clock in would freeze every\nfiring; the per-object caps wall; LWW on re-apply; both lints) plus unit tests\nin `validate_reminder` and `manifest`. lb-packs 90/90, lb-host --lib 534/534,\nevery pack + reminder suite green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(rules): grid.size() spoke SurrealQL to DataFusion — and ai.classify was collateral\n\nThree bugs, one root: the FEDERATION seam''s shape was handled in `records()` and\nnowhere else. Found while testing whether a rule can call the model (the\n\"rule decides, model renders\" pattern, rubix-ai rules-calling-ai-scope.md).\n\n1. `size()` emitted `SELECT count() AS v FROM (…) GROUP ALL` — SurrealQL — on\n   EVERY source. Correct on the store, a hard parse error on any federation\n   source, and the error names a column position past the end of the author''s own\n   SQL so it reads as a bug in their query.\n\n   No spelling works on both: the obvious portable one-liner (`COUNT(*)`, no\n   `GROUP ALL`) fixed federation and BROKE the store (\"Unexpected token `*`\").\n   Verified both directions, then dispatched on `SourceKind` — which is already on\n   the grid; the module doc even says \"the grid carries its SourceKind so a…\".\n\n2. With the SQL fixed, `size()` still returned 0 on federation: it read the count\n   with `r.get(\"v\")`, but the federation seam returns column-aligned ARRAYS\n   (`[3]`, not `{\"v\":3}`). A wrong answer, not an error — and 0 rows reads as\n   \"always under the cap\", which defeats `ai.classify`''s over-large-grid guard\n   entirely.\n\n3. `ai.classify` then built its output with `as_object_mut()`, which is None on an\n   array row, so the label was silently dropped and it returned the UNLABELLED\n   rows. Fixed by normalizing with `row_to_map` first — the same normalizer\n   `records()` uses.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: `ai.classify` had never once worked on a federation source, i.e. on\nany time-series data — the only data the BAS/FDD path has. It is also the verb best\nsuited to a small local model (bounded rows in, one label each out, no set logic),\nso this was blocking the most promising direction, not a corner.\n\nVerified live on one node, same rule shape:\n  size() platform            5     (unchanged)\n  size() federation LIMIT 3  parse error -> 3\n  size() federation all      parse error -> 1152 (matches SELECT COUNT(*))\n  ai.classify on federation  parse error -> labels land, and are CORRECT:\n                             meter-020-zt (flatlined) -> frozen-sensor\n                             meter-021-zt (healthy)   -> healthy-sensor\n                             one metered call, 8 tokens\n\nWHY THE SUITE MISSED IT: tests/support/mod.rs''s seam keys its count branch on\n`query.contains(\"count()\")` — the SurrealQL spelling — and returns object rows for\nBOTH kinds. It bakes in the very platform assumption it is meant to test. The new\n`grid_dialect_test.rs` models each backend faithfully instead: right dialect, right\nrow shape, and each rejects the other''s. RED-then-green verified — 2 of 3 fail on\nthe original code, and the platform test passes throughout (no regression).\n\nThe lesson, and it is about which failures are cheap: the dialect bug threw and got\nfound. The two row-shape bugs returned plausible wrong values and would have sat\nthere indefinitely. A wrong answer costs more than a crash.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(prefs,ingest,viz,dashboard): unit provenance, a BAS vocabulary, 55 catalog views, a prefs tz default — and a 1000x epoch bug\n\nFive gaps found by measuring a real bench (44 dashboards / 269 cells) against\nwhat a mobile client would need to render dashboards and honour timezone/unit\npreferences. Written up downstream in rubix-ai''s\napp/docs/scope/dashboards/api-gaps-review.md.\n\n1. A SERIES NOW CARRIES ITS UNIT.\n\n`series_meta` gains a `unit` column and the viz `Field` an optional `unit`,\nstamped onto a frame''s numeric columns by host/viz/unit_attach.rs. This was the\nmissing link that made a *correct* converter unreachable: format.quantity needs\na from_unit and nothing on the data path had one — series_meta had two columns,\nseries.list returns bare strings, and Field had none while its own header\npromised \"SI/base units\".\n\nA producer declares it as a `unit` LABEL, validated against the closed enum:\n\"degrees celsius\" is REFUSED rather than recorded, because provenance that looks\nreal but converts to nothing is worse than an absent unit. Absent stays legal and\nmeans unknown. The frame never CONVERTS — that would bake one viewer''s prefs into\nshared, cacheable data.\n\n2. THE VOCABULARY LEARNED WHAT A BUILDING SPEAKS.\n\n8 dimensions / 29 units -> 18 / 58: energy, power, electric potential/current,\napparent power, volume, volume flow, illuminance, concentration, frequency, plus\nkPa which was missing from pressure all along. The measurement that forced it:\n85 of 143 unit-bearing panels (59%) declared a unit lb could not convert.\n\nVA is its OWN dimension, not power, and energy is not power: uom cannot tell VA\nfrom W, so VA->W and kWh->kW are structural CrossDimension refusals rather than\nplausible wrong numbers (the corpus contains a `custom:kW/kWh` unit).\n\n3. THE WIDGET CATALOG CARRIES 55 VIEWS, WAS 22.\n\nThe downstream shell kept a 31-entry WIRE_ALIASES table persisting a `geomap` as\nview:\"table\", so 45 of 269 cells stored a view that LIES and any client trusting\ncell.view drew ~20% of the corpus as a table, silently.\n\nThat table is not Grafana compatibility — it is a workaround for THIS catalog\nrejecting the ids on dashboard.save, and nearly every entry says \"retire once the\nlb catalog carries the id\". So the ids land here rather than adding a\n`view_resolved` field for clients to undo the lie with.\n\nNote: existing tests used \"heatmap\" as their canonical UNKNOWN view, which is now\na real one; they move to \"sunburst\".\n\n4. time.range.resolve DEFAULTS tz FROM THE CALLER''S PREFS.\n\nDST-correct calendar snapping already existed and already took a tz — it just\nnever learned who was asking, so \"today\" meant \"today in Greenwich\" for everyone,\nwhile prefs.timezone was read in exactly ONE place (format.datetime). An explicit\ntz still wins; no preference still means UTC. The verb is no longer purely\nstore-free; that one prefs read is the only touch.\n\n5. A LATENT 1000x WINDOW BUG.\n\nviz/time_override.rs claimed epoch SECONDS twice in its own header and subtracted\na seconds-valued duration from an ms clock, so timeFrom:\"6h\" asked for a\n21.6-SECOND window: silently empty, never an error. Every consumer on the path\n(series.read buckets, viz::resolution, $__timeFrom) is ms, and the client sends\nms.\n\nIt survived because no cell in the measured corpus had ever set timeFrom — and\nbecause its own tests were written from the same wrong premise and were green\nover the bug for the life of the feature, including an integration test asserting\nthe broken math against a real series.read dispatch. Both layers are re-based onto\nrealistic epoch-ms clocks whose magnitude makes the unit unmistakable. Written up\nin docs/debugging/viz/panel-time-override-was-1000x-off.md.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: Align gaining a tz. Filed downstream as additive and\npre-designed, it is neither — a real IANA zone makes the grid VARIABLE-WIDTH (a\nDST day is 23 or 25 hours), which is not align.rs''s origin + k*width model, and\nit would put a timezone database into a crate that is dependency-light on\npurpose. That is what the module''s own \"DST is deliberately NOT here\" section\nalready argues. Needs its own scope; $__timeGroup with a tz follows from it.\n\nSTILL OPEN: prefs.catalog returns i18n message strings, not the axis vocabulary,\nso a client cannot discover that unit_system is metric|imperial and every one\nhardcodes it.\n\nTests: 5 new files (39 tests), each verified red-then-green by disabling the\nmechanism and confirming the right tests fail. cargo fmt clean; every file under\nthe 400-line rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(flows): a retained inject poisoned flow_input and killed node_state workspace-wide\n\n`flows/triggers.rs` wrote the retained `flow_input` record with bare `lb_store::write`\ninstead of `write_locked`. Under the durable `kv-surrealkv` engine two writers on the\nsame row open optimistic transactions over one snapshot; one aborts with a write\nconflict, or — worse — a half-applied `rev` reads back as `Invalid revision ''…'' for\ntype ''Value''`. This is exactly the race `write_locked`''s own module docs describe on\nthe run-store; `run_store.rs` and `trigger_store.rs` were hardened for it and the\ninject path was missed.\n\nThe blast radius is the surprising part. `flows.node_state` folds retained inputs in\nby draining the WHOLE `flow_input` table (`scan_all`, deliberately no silent backstop),\nso ONE undeserializable row aborts the read for **every flow in the workspace** —\nincluding flows never written to — and it survives restarts. `flows.list` keeps\nworking throughout, so the flows page looks healthy while every dashboard control''s\nread-back is dead. Observed downstream as \"a slider writes once and every flow read is\nbroken from then on\": the write SUCCEEDS and the value is stored, it just can never be\nread again.\n\nFound by running a dashboard write-back end to end against a real store in rubix-ai.\nThe existing 19 cases here all boot `mem://`, where the race does not reproduce — they\nstayed green across the whole bug. The new test pins the invariant instead of the\ntiming: 16 concurrent retained injects must all land, and a subsequent `node_state`\nread of a DIFFERENT, never-injected flow must still deserialize. It fails on the bare\n`write` (`Transaction conflict`) and passes on `write_locked`.\n\nTwo adjacent findings, not fixed here: `flows.delete` does not clear a flow''s retained\n`flow_input` rows (a poisoned one has no route back through the API), and a fresh store\ngrants no `flows.*` caps at all, so every flow verb answers a bare `denied` that reads\nlike a capability bug in the caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b62a62eac2305ebd999e785f50c091253d582649","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/b62a62eac2305ebd999e785f50c091253d582649"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc","verification":{"payload":"tree b62a62eac2305ebd999e785f50c091253d582649\nparent f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789111485 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789111485 +1000\n\nfeat(agent,pack): reach agent.invoke over MCP, vary a scheduled job_id, and let a pack declare its schedule (#198)\n\n* fix(agent): dispatch `agent.invoke` over MCP, and vary a scheduled run''s job_id\n\n`agent.invoke` was built on every layer except the MCP dispatch table: the cap\n(`mcp:agent.invoke:call`), the `tools.catalog` descriptor and the routed edge→hub\npath (`invoke_remote`) all existed, but `call_agent_tool` had no `invoke` match, so\na call over `/mcp/call` fell through to `NotFound` and answered an opaque\n`403 \"no such tool\"` to a caller HOLDING the grant — a missing `match` arm that\nreads as a permissions bug.\n\nIt survived because every shipped caller uses another transport (browser →\n`POST /agent/invoke`, palette → `postAgent`, channel worker → the host fn). The\nfirst caller that needs the bridge is a REMINDER: an `Action::McpTool` fires\nthrough the `call_tool` chokepoint and has no browser, so \"run the agent on a\nschedule\" was unbuildable.\n\nThe new arm mirrors the HTTP route exactly (`reachable_tools` menu,\n`invoke_via_runtime` with `runtime = None`, same persona precedence, same opaque\nerror mapping) and adds no authority — `authorize_invoke` still runs inside and\nthe run stays bounded by `agent ∩ caller`. Two deliberate differences: `job_id` is\nrequired (a derived id would be identical on every firing), and `agent.invoke` is\nfiltered out of the menu handed to the model, since the dispatcher applies no\ndepth ceiling to host-native verbs.\n\nThe same work exposed a second defect: reminder args are static and the loop is\nidempotent on `job_id`, so a scheduled run would replay its first answer forever,\nnever calling the model. Fixed generically in `reminder/range.rs` — the module\nthat already owns fire-time payload substitution — with a `{{fire_ts}}` token\nreplaced by the fire clock''s epoch seconds in any string value. Action-agnostic\nper rule 10, and additive: a payload without the token is unchanged.\n\nProven live, not only unit-tested: `agent.invoke` over `/mcp/call` answered\n`{\"answer\":\"ok\",\"jobId\":\"mcp-real-1\"}` in 9.6s driven by a local offline Qwen3,\nand a per-minute probe reminder wrote memory slug `probe-1788764348`.\nRED-then-green verified: disabling the arm fails the guard with its message.\n\nTests: agent_invoke_mcp_test (6, incl. the regression guard and capability-deny),\n4 new range.rs unit tests. agent_suite 173/173, lb-host lib 534/534, all five\nreminder suites green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(pack): a pack declares its own schedule — the `reminders:` block\n\nEvery other pack section seeds a thing that sits inert until something drives\nit: a rule does nothing until `rules.run`, a channel until someone posts. A pack\nthat ships seven FDD rules and no schedule ships a product that detects nothing\nuntil an operator wires the cron by hand — off-manifest, undocumented, outside\nthe receipt, and not reproducible on a fresh box. The schedule IS the driver,\nand it was the one part of a domain pack that could not be declared.\n\nAdded on the `retention:` model, the closest precedent:\n\n- `lb_packs::manifest_reminder` — a dependency-free MIRROR of the\n  `reminder.create` args. `lb-reminders` depends on `lb-store` and `lb-packs` is\n  the pure half, so reusing `Action` would invert the layering. The cost is that\n  `action_kind` is a `String`, which is what makes the lint necessary.\n- `lb_packs::validate_reminder` — the lint the mirror pays for: an unknown\n  `action_kind` against the closed set, a kind missing the fields it needs, a\n  6-field quartz cron, and — as a WARNING — a static `job_id`.\n- `pack/reminder_action.rs` + a `Kind::Reminder` arm driving the same\n  `reminder_create` the public verb calls.\n\n**No cap smuggling.** The reminder is stored with the applying principal, and\nevery firing re-resolves that principal''s caps at the `call_tool` chokepoint. A\n`pack.apply` without `mcp:reminder.create:call` gets `denied` on the reminder\nobjects and an honest partial receipt; a pack-seeded schedule carries no\nauthority its admin lacks, and revoking that admin''s grant stops it.\n\nThe `job_id` warning is a warning rather than an error because the manifest\ncannot know which verbs are idempotent on which field. `job_id` without\n`{{fire_ts}}` is the one shape where the intent is unmistakable — and it is the\nexact replay trap the previous commit fixed: a static id returns the first run''s\nanswer forever, a frozen agent that looks perfectly alive.\n\nReminders plan LAST: a schedule firing `rules.run` is only meaningful once the\nrule it names exists.\n\nTests: `pack_reminders_test` (a pack seeds a schedule that will really fire —\nlistable AND with a `nextAttemptTs` past the apply clock, and `{{fire_ts}}`\nstored UNSUBSTITUTED, since baking the apply clock in would freeze every\nfiring; the per-object caps wall; LWW on re-apply; both lints) plus unit tests\nin `validate_reminder` and `manifest`. lb-packs 90/90, lb-host --lib 534/534,\nevery pack + reminder suite green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(rules): grid.size() spoke SurrealQL to DataFusion — and ai.classify was collateral\n\nThree bugs, one root: the FEDERATION seam''s shape was handled in `records()` and\nnowhere else. Found while testing whether a rule can call the model (the\n\"rule decides, model renders\" pattern, rubix-ai rules-calling-ai-scope.md).\n\n1. `size()` emitted `SELECT count() AS v FROM (…) GROUP ALL` — SurrealQL — on\n   EVERY source. Correct on the store, a hard parse error on any federation\n   source, and the error names a column position past the end of the author''s own\n   SQL so it reads as a bug in their query.\n\n   No spelling works on both: the obvious portable one-liner (`COUNT(*)`, no\n   `GROUP ALL`) fixed federation and BROKE the store (\"Unexpected token `*`\").\n   Verified both directions, then dispatched on `SourceKind` — which is already on\n   the grid; the module doc even says \"the grid carries its SourceKind so a…\".\n\n2. With the SQL fixed, `size()` still returned 0 on federation: it read the count\n   with `r.get(\"v\")`, but the federation seam returns column-aligned ARRAYS\n   (`[3]`, not `{\"v\":3}`). A wrong answer, not an error — and 0 rows reads as\n   \"always under the cap\", which defeats `ai.classify`''s over-large-grid guard\n   entirely.\n\n3. `ai.classify` then built its output with `as_object_mut()`, which is None on an\n   array row, so the label was silently dropped and it returned the UNLABELLED\n   rows. Fixed by normalizing with `row_to_map` first — the same normalizer\n   `records()` uses.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: `ai.classify` had never once worked on a federation source, i.e. on\nany time-series data — the only data the BAS/FDD path has. It is also the verb best\nsuited to a small local model (bounded rows in, one label each out, no set logic),\nso this was blocking the most promising direction, not a corner.\n\nVerified live on one node, same rule shape:\n  size() platform            5     (unchanged)\n  size() federation LIMIT 3  parse error -> 3\n  size() federation all      parse error -> 1152 (matches SELECT COUNT(*))\n  ai.classify on federation  parse error -> labels land, and are CORRECT:\n                             meter-020-zt (flatlined) -> frozen-sensor\n                             meter-021-zt (healthy)   -> healthy-sensor\n                             one metered call, 8 tokens\n\nWHY THE SUITE MISSED IT: tests/support/mod.rs''s seam keys its count branch on\n`query.contains(\"count()\")` — the SurrealQL spelling — and returns object rows for\nBOTH kinds. It bakes in the very platform assumption it is meant to test. The new\n`grid_dialect_test.rs` models each backend faithfully instead: right dialect, right\nrow shape, and each rejects the other''s. RED-then-green verified — 2 of 3 fail on\nthe original code, and the platform test passes throughout (no regression).\n\nThe lesson, and it is about which failures are cheap: the dialect bug threw and got\nfound. The two row-shape bugs returned plausible wrong values and would have sat\nthere indefinitely. A wrong answer costs more than a crash.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(prefs,ingest,viz,dashboard): unit provenance, a BAS vocabulary, 55 catalog views, a prefs tz default — and a 1000x epoch bug\n\nFive gaps found by measuring a real bench (44 dashboards / 269 cells) against\nwhat a mobile client would need to render dashboards and honour timezone/unit\npreferences. Written up downstream in rubix-ai''s\napp/docs/scope/dashboards/api-gaps-review.md.\n\n1. A SERIES NOW CARRIES ITS UNIT.\n\n`series_meta` gains a `unit` column and the viz `Field` an optional `unit`,\nstamped onto a frame''s numeric columns by host/viz/unit_attach.rs. This was the\nmissing link that made a *correct* converter unreachable: format.quantity needs\na from_unit and nothing on the data path had one — series_meta had two columns,\nseries.list returns bare strings, and Field had none while its own header\npromised \"SI/base units\".\n\nA producer declares it as a `unit` LABEL, validated against the closed enum:\n\"degrees celsius\" is REFUSED rather than recorded, because provenance that looks\nreal but converts to nothing is worse than an absent unit. Absent stays legal and\nmeans unknown. The frame never CONVERTS — that would bake one viewer''s prefs into\nshared, cacheable data.\n\n2. THE VOCABULARY LEARNED WHAT A BUILDING SPEAKS.\n\n8 dimensions / 29 units -> 18 / 58: energy, power, electric potential/current,\napparent power, volume, volume flow, illuminance, concentration, frequency, plus\nkPa which was missing from pressure all along. The measurement that forced it:\n85 of 143 unit-bearing panels (59%) declared a unit lb could not convert.\n\nVA is its OWN dimension, not power, and energy is not power: uom cannot tell VA\nfrom W, so VA->W and kWh->kW are structural CrossDimension refusals rather than\nplausible wrong numbers (the corpus contains a `custom:kW/kWh` unit).\n\n3. THE WIDGET CATALOG CARRIES 55 VIEWS, WAS 22.\n\nThe downstream shell kept a 31-entry WIRE_ALIASES table persisting a `geomap` as\nview:\"table\", so 45 of 269 cells stored a view that LIES and any client trusting\ncell.view drew ~20% of the corpus as a table, silently.\n\nThat table is not Grafana compatibility — it is a workaround for THIS catalog\nrejecting the ids on dashboard.save, and nearly every entry says \"retire once the\nlb catalog carries the id\". So the ids land here rather than adding a\n`view_resolved` field for clients to undo the lie with.\n\nNote: existing tests used \"heatmap\" as their canonical UNKNOWN view, which is now\na real one; they move to \"sunburst\".\n\n4. time.range.resolve DEFAULTS tz FROM THE CALLER''S PREFS.\n\nDST-correct calendar snapping already existed and already took a tz — it just\nnever learned who was asking, so \"today\" meant \"today in Greenwich\" for everyone,\nwhile prefs.timezone was read in exactly ONE place (format.datetime). An explicit\ntz still wins; no preference still means UTC. The verb is no longer purely\nstore-free; that one prefs read is the only touch.\n\n5. A LATENT 1000x WINDOW BUG.\n\nviz/time_override.rs claimed epoch SECONDS twice in its own header and subtracted\na seconds-valued duration from an ms clock, so timeFrom:\"6h\" asked for a\n21.6-SECOND window: silently empty, never an error. Every consumer on the path\n(series.read buckets, viz::resolution, $__timeFrom) is ms, and the client sends\nms.\n\nIt survived because no cell in the measured corpus had ever set timeFrom — and\nbecause its own tests were written from the same wrong premise and were green\nover the bug for the life of the feature, including an integration test asserting\nthe broken math against a real series.read dispatch. Both layers are re-based onto\nrealistic epoch-ms clocks whose magnitude makes the unit unmistakable. Written up\nin docs/debugging/viz/panel-time-override-was-1000x-off.md.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: Align gaining a tz. Filed downstream as additive and\npre-designed, it is neither — a real IANA zone makes the grid VARIABLE-WIDTH (a\nDST day is 23 or 25 hours), which is not align.rs''s origin + k*width model, and\nit would put a timezone database into a crate that is dependency-light on\npurpose. That is what the module''s own \"DST is deliberately NOT here\" section\nalready argues. Needs its own scope; $__timeGroup with a tz follows from it.\n\nSTILL OPEN: prefs.catalog returns i18n message strings, not the axis vocabulary,\nso a client cannot discover that unit_system is metric|imperial and every one\nhardcodes it.\n\nTests: 5 new files (39 tests), each verified red-then-green by disabling the\nmechanism and confirming the right tests fail. cargo fmt clean; every file under\nthe 400-line rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(flows): a retained inject poisoned flow_input and killed node_state workspace-wide\n\n`flows/triggers.rs` wrote the retained `flow_input` record with bare `lb_store::write`\ninstead of `write_locked`. Under the durable `kv-surrealkv` engine two writers on the\nsame row open optimistic transactions over one snapshot; one aborts with a write\nconflict, or — worse — a half-applied `rev` reads back as `Invalid revision ''…'' for\ntype ''Value''`. This is exactly the race `write_locked`''s own module docs describe on\nthe run-store; `run_store.rs` and `trigger_store.rs` were hardened for it and the\ninject path was missed.\n\nThe blast radius is the surprising part. `flows.node_state` folds retained inputs in\nby draining the WHOLE `flow_input` table (`scan_all`, deliberately no silent backstop),\nso ONE undeserializable row aborts the read for **every flow in the workspace** —\nincluding flows never written to — and it survives restarts. `flows.list` keeps\nworking throughout, so the flows page looks healthy while every dashboard control''s\nread-back is dead. Observed downstream as \"a slider writes once and every flow read is\nbroken from then on\": the write SUCCEEDS and the value is stored, it just can never be\nread again.\n\nFound by running a dashboard write-back end to end against a real store in rubix-ai.\nThe existing 19 cases here all boot `mem://`, where the race does not reproduce — they\nstayed green across the whole bug. The new test pins the invariant instead of the\ntiming: 16 concurrent retained injects must all land, and a subsequent `node_state`\nread of a DIFFERENT, never-injected flow must still deserialize. It fails on the bare\n`write` (`Transaction conflict`) and passes on `write_locked`.\n\nTwo adjacent findings, not fixed here: `flows.delete` does not clear a flow''s retained\n`flow_input` rows (a poisoned one has no route back through the API), and a fresh store\ngrants no `flows.*` caps at all, so every flow verb answers a bare `denied` that reads\nlike a capability bug in the caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqo6y9CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZmMQAHbOZBKW016k4mMNjM2qyuBC\n56FFoYKVcILuWxe6LIUOJ+D3+4rQL5gZaAM+rwq0gUy2OraQsiPIwZ+Qlnqabt3f\nX1fS526DevJvKTEHx7qwmO/defzSLDmtr4OMimwnwWZ3TqTzVnMsNx/2xkziX32L\n3YB1liC9nkYWMQD9MeS7Z8IGKAnvqbMLBzq34vDA1k+JLzF+NYrTUThYZWZpz51y\nuqJ0SgDC792LVRZh/lhHIY+YWFQipwapy2LIkDp1EFtDFgfkm09z4auo93trM1pm\nO/0ZTb6jjH1MilBFaSqAnlm+oeGYtFCjdlGb7ll08sO2+125D1DQ9Iiy9FkqyQrN\nJ+iJ+rq4DjGfdr6+qprro20wixsehwGvLgAme8dTYnNrxHWts/ATY06OkjpRwnYV\nwa7GJ6yDSssoSXHV8sR7ZIZ24E7BPWOH+2lH9ZytEEdpIdMFzQo3XwNLJPpVGToL\nemz3bENBa6crRUWKbKJ1woC2Z9oOaqFKQrv7clqL+pdLHm7Mdm0LCMDBxpCRhIBz\nWqXYlbaBZTBzE0QorkkcB4ds0e93f8L6GgSh29NZUiShBuFFlNaM6KwLwFDVnpcq\nVSzDH0tlHs15tJyoXylWLNozeEaPj7A8jbXOUoNl9KicOK/POE2qAI7VfBqsEHKI\nc855djuOoC5XjZ+Lzthh\n=H4Mz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-11T07:24:45Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDI5ZWNhOTMxODYyMTA1MGMzMjZjNzZjYWQyODEyODUxYWIyYmY1ZmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a","sha":"f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a"}],"sha":"29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/29eca9318621050c326c76cad2812851ab2bf5fc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-11T06:59:05Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-11T06:59:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"The Case plane — lb-cases, the case/SLA/party verbs, the reactors and the contractor token surface (#202)\n\n* docs(insights): the lb-side Case plane scope + session doc\n\nDerives the upstream half from the rubix-ai product scope: the lb-cases records,\nthe case.* verb surface with its cap tiers and gate aliases, the case-group /\nhold-down / sla-clock reactors, the GET /r/{token} token principal, and the\nchanges the insight raise hot path takes (the tags.* dispatcher door, the\nHuman > Producer fold, evidence.subjects[], the data-quality caveat stamp,\ncategory validated against a workspace-declared vocabulary, month_hist +\npattern, the case_id echo).\n\nIndexed in docs/scope/insights/README.md.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): pin the verdict seam precisely in the case-plane scope\n\nRead the shipped producer rather than assuming its shape. `body.explains[]`\nholds dedup_key STRINGS (not insight ids) and `body.root_cause` names the\nupstream finding, so the reactor resolves through dedup_lookup with a\nget-by-id fallback and sets `primary_insight` to the ROOT fault, not the\nrecord that names it. Both orderings (verdict-first, verdict-last) are\nspecified, and a human-placed member is never moved in either direction.\n\nStill two generic body keys and nothing else out of `body` — rule 10 intact.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cases): service_policy, the business-hours calendar and the SLA deadline arithmetic\n\nThe `service_policy` half of the case plane (docs/scope/insights/case-plane-scope.md):\nthe contract record, the calendar its hours are counted in, the pure deadline maths the\nsla-clock reactor will call, and the most-specific-wins policy resolution.\n\n- `policy.rs` — `ServicePolicy` + `PolicyMatch` (three OPAQUE strings; rule 10 holds:\n  nothing here names a pack, rule, site, extension or category value). `hold_down_days`\n  defaults to 14 per the scope: a rule''s own window measures detection cadence, not how\n  long a repair takes to prove itself. `respond_h`/`resolve_h` deliberately carry NO\n  serde default — a row without a deadline must fail to decode loudly rather than\n  quietly govern work as \"0 hours\" (instantly breached).\n- `calendar.rs` — `Calendar::{Always, Business}` + `DayHours`. **hours[0] is MONDAY**,\n  documented loudly: an off-by-one here does not crash, it silently bills a Sunday as a\n  Monday. `validate()` is the write door, so the arithmetic downstream stays total.\n- `deadline.rs` — `add_business_hours` / `respond_by` / `due_at`. Pure: injected `now`,\n  no store, no wall clock. Terminates on a never-opening calendar via a bounded scan and\n  the documented `NEVER` sentinel — the fail-safe direction, since the alternative\n  failure breaches every case in the workspace at once.\n- `policy_match.rs` — most specific wins; a policy with a pinned axis that CONTRADICTS\n  the query is disqualified outright, never scored as a partial fit. Ties by `id` asc.\n- `policy_list.rs` — the list order IS the resolution ladder, so an admin can read\n  precedence off the settings page instead of out of the code.\n\nTimezones are IANA names, not fixed offsets. `chrono-tz` is already a first-class\nworkspace dep (lb-schedules, lb-prefs, lb-host), so the honest option costs no new\ndependency — and `a_daylight_saving_transition_shortens_the_wall_gap` pins the hour a\nfixed offset would have got wrong twice a year.\n\n32 tests, all pure. The scope''s named case is `respond_by_crosses_a_weekend`: Mon-Fri\n09:00-17:00, opened Friday 16:00, respond_h 2 -> Monday 10:00.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): record the captured baseline — all 11 touched suites green at f8633b6d\n\nlb master is red on some jobs, so ''not my failure'' has to be provable. Every\nsuite this session touches (lb-insights + 11 named lb-host test binaries\ncovering insight, tags, outbox and reminders) is GREEN on untouched\norigin/master, which means none of the usual excuses apply to these eleven.\n\nStates plainly what is NOT baselined (the other ~100 lb-host binaries and the\ngateway) rather than implying a comparison that was never run.\n\nAlso records the disk incident: a second worktree target/ took the box to 100%\nof 916GB and surfaced as a zigcc SIGABRT on an unrelated crate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(insights): evidence.subjects, caveats, case_id, month_hist + pattern, and the workspace category vocabulary\n\nThe `lb-insights` half of the case plane (docs/scope/insights/case-plane-scope.md).\n\n- `evidence.subjects[]` — opaque point refs the finding was derived from. lb never\n  resolves one; it set-intersects them, which is what lets an open data-quality\n  finding gate everything derived from the same points.\n- `caveats[]` + `RaiseOutcome.caveated` — stamped at raise from the OPEN findings in\n  the workspace''s declared gating category whose subjects intersect this one''s.\n  REFRESHED (not merged) on every raise, so resolving the gating finding clears it.\n- **A caveated insight is never a breakthrough** — `Intent.caveated` suppresses the\n  breakthrough check AND the L0 immediate post in `ladder.rs`. Suppressing only the\n  breakthrough would leave the first raise of a caveated key posting anyway.\n  The accounting still runs, so it rides the digest: suppressed, not silenced.\n- `vocab.rs` — the `tag_vocab:{key}` record + `validate_category`. Rule 10: lb ships\n  NO default value list. An unseeded workspace validates anything; a seeded one\n  rejects an undeclared value naming the declared set. The gating category is a\n  parameter read from that row, never a literal in the crate.\n- `pattern.rs` — non-evicting `month_hist[12]` (calendar month of `ts`, UTC) and a\n  PURE `derive_pattern` → new | flapping | seasonal | chronic, every threshold\n  documented with why. Pure so a record classifies the same way on every read.\n- `case_ref.rs` — `set_case_id`, mirroring `tags_echo.rs` (skip the write when\n  unchanged; a missing record is Ok, not an error a reactor pass dies on).\n\nEvery new field is `#[serde(default)]` + skipped when empty, so a record written\nbefore it landed decodes and round-trips without gaining a key. Tests: the four\npattern branches, the calendar anchors, the size guard counting `subjects`, the\nvocab fallback, the caveat delivery pair (control + caveated) in ladder_test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(host/insight): the Human > Producer tag fold, the echo backfill job, and the tags-door tests\n\n- `insight/facets.rs` (new) — the precedence FOLD, extracted out of `raise.rs`.\n  For one tag key: Human > Producer > Inferred > System; newest `Provenance.at`\n  within a source; a remaining tie breaks on the value string so insertion order can\n  never change the answer. Before this, `materialize_facets` collected into a map and\n  kept whichever edge `tags.of` returned last — an unspecified order, so an\n  operator''s correction silently reverted on the next producer raise.\n  (docs/scope/insights/insight-tag-precedence-scope.md)\n- `insight/backfill.rs` (new) — `backfill_insight_facets`, the one-shot boot pass that\n  re-folds every insight''s echo. The fold self-heals on the next FIRING; this covers\n  the finding that never fires again, which is exactly where a correction stays\n  reverted for ever. Idempotent by construction (a second run returns 0).\n  Modelled on `heal_insight_timestamps`; wants the same call in node/src/reactors.rs.\n- `insight/raise.rs` — minimal: calls into `facets::materialize_facets` and passes\n  `outcome.caveated` into the matcher''s `InsightView`.\n- `tests/insight_tag_fold_test.rs` (new) — the fold through the real store, real tag\n  graph and real `insight.raise`, read back through BOTH `get` and `list`, plus\n  determinism and workspace isolation.\n- `tests/insight_caveat_test.rs` (new) — the caveat stamp and the workspace category\n  vocabulary end to end, including the delivery PAIR through a real subscription and\n  the real inbox: the caveated critical raise posts nothing, the control posts.\n- `tests/tags_door_test.rs` (new) — RED until the lead lands the registration, which\n  is the correct signal. It asserts positively (a missing alias is Denied, never\n  NotFound, so only a positive test catches it) that the four verbs dispatch, that\n  `tags.of` rides `mcp:tags.find:call`, and that `mcp:tags.remove:call` exists in\n  AUTHOR_CAPS.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style: cargo fmt --all over the insights + case-plane files\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(host): policy.sla.set / policy.sla.list — the admin SLA policy verbs\n\nThe host half of the service_policy slice (docs/scope/insights/case-plane-scope.md).\n\n- `host/src/case/policy_{set,list}.rs` — two admin verbs over `lb_cases`, gated by\n  `authorize_tool` before any validation so the deny stays opaque.\n- ADMIN tier for BOTH halves, in `authz/builtin_roles.rs`. The read is admin deliberately:\n  a policy row states the commercial contract (response/resolution hours per site), which\n  is not every member''s to read. A viewer sees the CONSEQUENCE — their case''s `due_at` —\n  never the terms.\n- Dispatched by EXACT name from `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT`, not a `policy.` prefix. Reserving a\n  whole namespace against a hypothetical extension whose id is `policy` is the mistake\n  `ext.list` and `update.*` already avoid (and that two colliding `ros.*` extensions once\n  demonstrated the hard way). Both gate on their own name, so neither needs a\n  `tool_gate.rs` alias.\n- `system/catalog/policy.rs` — the catalog rows. Without these the verbs dispatch but are\n  INVISIBLE to the console and the agent menu, and `host_catalog_covers_dispatch_prefixes`\n  goes red.\n\nTests (10, real `Node::boot()` + `mem://` store, no mocks): capability-deny per verb, the\nreal-vs-fictional-id oracle check, workspace isolation across a SHARED policy id, the\nladder ordering, upsert-by-id, the write-door rejects — plus the two POSITIVE gate tests\nthe scope demands, minting the REAL `workspace_admin_role_caps()` bundle. A negative test\ncannot catch a cap that exists in no bundle: a missing cap denies exactly like a present\none refusing. Only the positive test does.\n\nAlso fixes a bug in `policy_list` that only a real store could show: `lb_store::scan`\nreturns the whole `{ data, rev }` record while `read`/`list` return the inner value, so\ndecoding the wrapper yielded `None` for every row — and the deliberate \"skip a row that\nwill not decode\" tolerance swallowed it into an empty, green list. Now unwrapped, with\n`tests/policy_store_test.rs` (real `mem://` store) standing guard over the round trip.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(host): open the tags.* MCP door — dispatcher, gate alias, author cap, backfill boot job\n\nThe tag graph has had a full host service (`call_tags_tool`) and per-verb caps\nsince the tags scope, and NO dispatcher entry — so nothing could reach any of the\nfour verbs over MCP. That is what made `insight-tag-precedence-scope.md`\nunreachable in production: with no wire door to write a human-sourced edge, the\ncorrected classification the `Human > Producer` fold exists to protect could\nnever be authored in the first place.\n\n- `tool_call.rs`: the four verbs in `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT` by EXACT name (not a\n  `tags.` prefix — the `ext.list` precedent: never reserve a whole namespace\n  against a hypothetical extension whose id is `tags`), plus the dispatch arm.\n- `tool_gate.rs`: `tags.of` -> `tags.find`. Reading ONE entity''s tags is\n  `tags.find` narrowed to a single entity, not a second privilege.\n- `tags/verbs.rs`: the INNER gate aliased to match. This is the half that\n  actually bit. The outer alias alone left `tags.of` denied, because `tags_of`\n  re-checks `mcp:tags.of:call` inside itself: the two gates disagreed and the\n  strictest won — the exact shape `media.list` (gated on `media.get`) already\n  records in tool_gate.rs''s own notes. An alias whose inner gate disagrees is\n  decorative.\n- `builtin_roles.rs`: `mcp:tags.remove:call` joins AUTHOR beside `tags.add`. It\n  was in NO bundle, so remove would have been Denied for every caller, admins\n  included, the moment the door opened.\n- `lib.rs` + `node/src/reactors.rs`: export and boot the one-shot echo backfill.\n  The fold self-heals on the next firing, so the backfill exists for the finding\n  that never fires again — where an operator''s correction would otherwise stay\n  silently reverted for ever.\n\nBEHAVIOUR CHANGE, stated loudly: `tags_test::denies_each_verb_without_its_grant`\ngranted `mcp:tags.of:call` and asserted `tags.of` succeeded under it. That cap\nexists in no role bundle, so the test asserted a contract only a hand-built test\nprincipal could ever satisfy. It now grants `mcp:tags.find:call`. The read/write\nsplit the test exists to prove is unchanged.\n\nEvidence: tags_door_test 5/5 (2/5 before this commit — red-before-green).\ntags_test 3/3 and tags_isolation_test 1/1, both matching the master baseline\ncaptured at f8633b6d.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cases): the case plane — lb-cases, the host verbs, and the two grouping reactors\n\nAn insight is a detection. A case is a piece of work. They are different\nrecords, because work is many-to-one with detections: one gateway drop is 400\ndetections and one job; one chiller that short-cycles every summer is one\ndetection key and three jobs over three years. Putting workflow on the detection\nrow breaks both — so the raise hot path never learns what a purchase order is.\n\n- `lb-cases` (new crate, `rust/crates/cases/`): the `case`, `case_member` and\n  `case_event` records plus one pure verb per file over the store seam. No\n  authorization and no wall clock — `lb-insights`'' altitude exactly.\n- `host/src/case/`: the capability chokepoint. Eleven verbs, four of which gate\n  on a cap that is not their name (members/events -> case.get, merge/split ->\n  case.open, assign/snooze/comment -> case.workflow), each with the\n  `tool_gate.rs` arm a missing alias would otherwise turn into a bare `Denied`\n  for every caller including admins.\n\nTwo invariants carry the design, and both are enforced in one place:\n\n  1. `resolved` REQUIRES a `resolution`, and nothing else may carry one\n     (`workflow.rs`). Closed-with-no-reason cannot be told from a false positive\n     six months later, and the hold-down reactor has nothing to judge.\n  2. Every open insight is in exactly ONE open case (`member_add.rs` refuses to\n     break it; `merge`/`split` are the verbs that legitimately move members).\n     Without it the queue double-counts and two technicians work one fault from\n     two rows.\n\nThe reactors:\n\n- **case-group** runs INLINE at the end of `insight_raise`, not only on a loop:\n  a scan-only reactor makes the invariant merely EVENTUALLY true, so a UI that\n  raises then lists sees a case-less row for one tick. A verdict record — one\n  whose body carries a non-empty `explains[]` — makes the case about the ROOT it\n  names (`body.root_cause`), with the findings it explains and the verdict record\n  itself as `explained` members. Citations are `dedup_key` strings resolved\n  through `dedup_lookup` (id fallback); an unresolvable one is warned and\n  skipped, never an error. Both real orderings work: verdict-LAST merges the\n  existing `single` cases in, verdict-FIRST opens over what resolves and folds\n  each straggler in as it arrives. A `human_placed` member is never moved.\n- **hold-down** reaches the raise path through the same call site: a re-fire\n  inside the policy''s `hold_down_days` of a `fixed` close REOPENS that case with\n  a `reopened` event saying the repair did not hold. Any other resolution lets a\n  new case open, because only `fixed` was a repair.\n- **reconcile** is the restart-safe backstop AND the triage backfill: it carries\n  `insight.assigned_to` onto the case and replays the comment thread with the\n  ORIGINAL author and timestamp. Idempotent — a second pass returns 0.\n\n`insight.assign` / `insight.comment` become delegates (resolved decision 5). The\nverb names, the caps, the bulk semantics and the return shapes are untouched;\nwhat moved is where the fact LIVES. The case is the writer and the insight keeps\nan echo, so the roster''s owner column, the subscription matcher''s `assignee`\nfilter and `insight.list`''s owner axis all still answer from the insight. All 17\nshipped triage tests and all 22 insight tests pass unchanged. The one intended\nbehaviour change: assigning one detection assigns every detection its case\ncites, because three symptoms of one fault are one job with one owner.\n\nRule 10 throughout. Nothing here names a pack, a rule, a category value or an\nextension; `category`/`site`/`scope` are opaque workspace strings, and the only\nthing read out of `body` is the generic citation grammar — in ONE file\n(`case/verdict.rs`) so no other file can quietly learn a second key.\n\nTests (30 new, all green): capability-deny and workspace-isolation on every\nverb; a POSITIVE gate test per aliased verb (a missing alias is `Denied`, not\n`NotFound`, so only a call that must succeed catches it); the resolution and\nexclusivity invariants; 50 re-raises moving nothing on the human plane; a\nseverity escalation puncturing a snooze; the Mine lane resolving through TEAMS;\nboth verdict orderings; hold-down reopen vs new case. Every reactor case asserts\nRED with the gating cap removed — and that no case was created — before it\nasserts green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(host): prove policy.sla.* is visible in tools.catalog, not just dispatchable\n\nThe console and the agent''s menu are both built from `tools.catalog`, so a verb absent\nfrom the host catalog can only be called by someone who already knows its name — the\nshape in which `datasource.`/`viz.`/`flows.` once went missing. Asserted from my own test\nfile so the check does not depend on the crate-wide\n`host_catalog_covers_dispatch_prefixes` unit test, which is currently red on the `case.`\nprefix (cases-core''s catalog family, not this slice).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(host/catalog): the tags.* family the dispatcher door left unadvertised\n\nOpening the `tags.*` MCP door added four verbs to `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT` without\ncatalog rows, and `host_catalog_covers_dispatch_prefixes` asserts every\ndispatched verb has one — so `cargo test -p lb-host --lib` was red for the whole\nwave on `host catalog has no entry for dispatched verb ''tags.add''`. Two agents\nreported it independently, which is the guard doing exactly its job: a verb that\ndispatches but is invisible in `tools.catalog` is reachable by anyone who\nalready knows its name and undiscoverable by everyone else.\n\n`tags.of` is advertised under the `tags.find` read cap it actually gates on. The\ncatalog''s cardinal rule — advertise a tool only if the call would allow it,\nnever hide one that would pass — holds only if the catalog asks the gate''s own\nquestion, and `tags.of` is aliased in both `tool_gate.rs` and\n`tags/authorize.rs`.\n\nlb-host --lib: 563 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): correct the scope against the built records\n\nThree prose details did not survive contact with the code, and the UI mirrored\nthe prose before anyone noticed: case_event.seq is `eseq` on the wire (a serde\nrename, so a reader of `seq` silently gets nothing), CaseRow is Case FLATTENED\nplus member_count rather than a nested { case, member_count }, and external_ref\nis a plain opaque string rather than the {system,id,url} struct described.\n\nRecorded as a table rather than silently edited: a downstream consumer mirroring\nthe scope is precisely how these bite, so the correction is worth more than the\ntidy version. Mirror the Rust, not the scope — a scope is the ask, the record is\nthe contract.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): split the contractor page URL from its data endpoints\n\nThe scope said `GET /r/{token}` mints the token principal. But that path is also\nwhat a human opens in a browser, and the browser must be served the SPA''s HTML\nthere — one path cannot return HTML to a browser and JSON to that page''s own\nfetch. Corrected: /r/{token} is a PAGE url served by the SPA fallback, and the\ndata surface is three public gateway routes beside /public/invite/accept.\n\nThe attachment upload is a gateway route, NOT a third MCP verb: the token\nprincipal''s surface stays exactly two caps.\n\nAlso pins two things the build got right that the scope had left implicit. The\nview payload carries the plotted rows itself — a two-cap principal is Denied on\nevery query-plane verb, so the page physically cannot fetch a chart, and\nshipping the rows is what keeps its whole surface equal to one verb''s response.\nAnd the 410 body states the possibilities as a disjunction, because \"this token\nexpired\" tells a prober the string was real.\n\nFound by the UI half building against the scope and hitting the contradiction.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cases): rule.scorecard — precision per origin.ref per site, read-only\n\nThe feedback loop from resolution back to detection (case-plane scope §7).\n`origin.ref` names the producer; `case.resolution` names what a human concluded\nwhen the work finished. This joins the two so \"that rule cries wolf\" becomes a\nnumber instead of a mood.\n\nThree files:\n\n- `lb-cases/scorecard.rs` — the PURE arithmetic over already-loaded rows: no\n  store, no clock, no I/O, so the formula that will be argued about in review is\n  unit-testable in microseconds. `precision = fixed / (fixed + false_positive +\n  self_cleared)`; `accepted_risk` and `duplicate` are counted and reported but\n  deliberately OUT of the denominator (accepting a budget-blocked risk is not the\n  detector being wrong, and a duplicate is a statement about the GROUPING). A zero\n  denominator is `None` — never `0.0`, which would defame a detector that has\n  simply never been judged. Median time-to-resolve is over the `fixed` cases only\n  (a false positive''s \"resolve time\" measures how long somebody took to notice it\n  was noise); even counts take the mean of the two middle values.\n- `lb-cases/resolved.rs` — the unpaged read of the workspace''s resolved cases.\n  Not `list` with `include_closed`: that verb pages at 200, and a precision\n  computed over the first 200 rows and presented as the rule''s precision is the\n  exact shape of lie this scope exists to remove.\n- `host/case/scorecard.rs` — the gated verb. VIEWER: a scorecard is an aggregate\n  of outcomes a viewer can already read one at a time, and the people who most\n  need to know a detector is crying wolf hold only read caps.\n\nA resolved case whose primary insight has since been DELETED is counted under\n`unknown` with a warn, never dropped — dropping it would shrink a denominator and\nmake every surviving precision a lie about a smaller population than the reader\nbelieves they are seeing.\n\nThe verb IS an N+1 (one scan, then one insight read per resolved case); this is\nsaid plainly in the doc comment. The fix is an `origin_ref` echo written onto the\ncase at open — the discipline `case_id` and the owner echo already use — which is\na wave-1 record change, so it is named there rather than done here.\n\n`rule.` (SINGULAR) is dispatched by EXACT name in `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT`, not as a\nprefix: the host already owns `rules.` (plural, the rules engine), and reserving a\nsecond one-letter-different namespace against a hypothetical extension named\n`rule` is the mistake `ext.list` already avoids. The two cannot shadow each other\n(`\"rule.scorecard\".starts_with(\"rules.\")` is false).\n\nNo `rule_policy` demotion: §7 makes it an explicit fast-follow, and a number that\nsilently changes how a detector fires is the \"silent code\" that section rules out.\n\nTests: 14 unit tests on the arithmetic (the formula, the excluded outcomes, the\nzero denominator, odd/even medians, the `(rule_ref, site)` pair, the surviving\n`site: None` group) and 10 integration tests on a real booted node — capability\ndeny, workspace isolation, the deleted primary insight, the filters, and a\nPOSITIVE gate test proving a principal holding only `mcp:rule.scorecard:call`\nreaches the verb, plus an assertion that the cap ships in the viewer bundle.\nVerified RED: dropping the `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT` entry turns 8 of the 10 red.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(case): the sla-clock reactor — deadlines, the breach alarm, and the breach write\n\nWave 2''s sla-clock slice. A case''s place in the queue is `due_at` ascending —\na wall-clock fact a client can be held to — never a composite score, and the\nclock never pauses.\n\nlb-cases (pure, no wall clock):\n  - `deadline_set::set_deadlines` — persist `policy_id`/`respond_by`/`due_at`\n    and append an `sla` event naming BOTH ends of every axis. Idempotent: an\n    unchanged answer writes nothing. The one exception is the first pass over a\n    case that matches NO policy — every field is `None` either way, so without\n    an explicit statement a reader cannot tell \"no contract governs this work\"\n    from \"the clock never ran\".\n  - `breach_mark::mark_breached` — set-once `breached_ts` + `breach_waiting_on`\n    (the holder AT that instant, which is what makes a breach attributable).\n    Nothing here reads `snooze_until`, and `waiting_on` never stops time.\n  - `EventKind::Sla` — a deadline is not a state transition, so it does not\n    ride the `workflow` stream.\n\nhost/src/case:\n  - `sla_clock::apply_sla` — resolve the policy through `match_policy`, compute\n    through wave 1''s PURE `respond_by()`/`due_at()`, write, arm the alarm. Both\n    deadlines are a pure function of `(policy, opened_ts)`, so \"a snooze does\n    not move the clock\" is structural rather than a rule to remember, and a\n    severity escalation recomputes because severity is a policy-match axis.\n    `NEVER` folds to absent; no matching policy leaves both absent.\n  - `breach_reminder` — a durable ONE-SHOT reminder armed to the exact deadline\n    second (`next_attempt_ts` set directly; the cron would round to the minute\n    and fire EARLY). The `schedule` is still a real cron because a denied firing\n    reschedules through it, and a `BadCron` there aborts the whole workspace''s\n    reminder pass. Arms itself with exactly one grant: `mcp:case.breach:call`\n    for `system:sla-clock`, because `fire_reminder` re-resolves caps from the\n    durable grant store and a system actor holds none by default.\n  - `breach` — the `case.breach` verb the alarm fires. Gates on its OWN name\n    (no `tool_gate.rs` arm) and its cap is in NO role bundle: a person does not\n    declare a breach.\n  - `breach_notify` — escalates to the assignee''s own channel, BYPASSING the\n    ladder for the reason `insight/assign_notify.rs` bypasses it.\n\nWired at two call sites: `case_open`, and the tail of `group_insight` (whose\nexit 1 is the severity-escalation path, so open and escalation cannot drift).\n\nTests (`sla_clock_test.rs`, real store/policies/reminder reactor, no mocks):\nFriday 16:00 + 2 business hours = Monday 10:00; the same across a holiday; an\nescalation recomputes and punctures the snooze; a snooze moves neither deadline;\nthe breach records the holder at that instant and is never rewritten; no policy\nleaves the deadlines absent and says so; the alarm ACTUALLY FIRES when\n`react_to_reminders` is driven. RED-first throughout — the raise cap removed for\nthe clock''s own path, the breach grant revoked for the alarm''s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): wave 2''s decisions — the self-grant, the alarm, the N+1\n\nSeven decisions with their rejected alternatives, and one caveat stated rather\nthan hidden.\n\nThe one worth reading twice: fire_reminder re-resolves the stored principal''s\ncaps from the durable grant store, so a system actor holding none would have\nmade the breach alarm a permanent silent denied=1. Widening reactor_caps()\nwould have looked like a fix and changed nothing — that list feeds the flow\nloop, which the reminder fire path never reads.\n\nAlso records that rule.scorecard ships a known N+1, what the fix is, and why\nit was not done in this wave.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* test(case): the five reactor cases whose RED half was missing\n\nThe module doc claimed \"Every reactor case asserts RED first\". Four of nine\ndid. A doc that overstates its own tests is worse than the gap, because it\nstops the next reader looking.\n\nThree grouping cases now run their identical FIRST raise with\nmcp:insight.raise:call removed and assert both the refusal and that nothing\nwas grouped — the half that makes the green meaningful. Without it, a grouping\nfunction that returned a hard-coded id, or never ran at all, would pass.\n\nThe two reconcile_* cases are left alone and the doc now says why:\nreconcile_cases is a node-level backfill invoked as a Rust function, not a verb\nbehind the caps wall, so there is no cap to remove and a RED half there would\nbe theatre. Their honest equivalent already exists — the workspace is asserted\nto hold zero cases BEFORE the pass, which is what makes \"the pass created it\"\na claim rather than an assumption.\n\n9 passed, 0 failed (--test-threads=1).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* feat(cases): the contractor round trip — party, case_request, the token principal\n\nWave 2''s largest slice: an external party can be asked something about a case,\nopen a tokenised page with no account, and answer — end to end, over the same\ncaps wall as every other caller.\n\n**lb-cases** (the records + pure verbs, no clock, no authorization):\n`party` (kind/contact/sites/trades/window; scorecard fields derived by query,\nnever stored) with upsert + list; `case_request` (the ask, the token HASH, the\nwindow, the reply, `delivery`) with open/status/reply/delivery verbs; the\nsix-row reply transition table; `set_waiting_on` / `set_cost_to_fix`.\n\n**host/src/case/**: `case.request.send` mints a ≥256-bit token, stores only its\nSHA-256, resolves the window (party → policy → default), stages the row AND the\nlink email in ONE outbox transaction, schedules the 50 %/80 %/breach ladder, sets\n`waiting_on` and events it. `withdraw` / `nudge` alias onto the same cap;\n`view` / `reply` are the token principal''s two verbs and gate on caps that exist\nin NO bundle, deliberately. `party.upsert` / `party.list` are admin.\n\n**delivery mirrors the provider kind** (resolved decision 7): `EmailProvider`\nnow states a `Disposition`, the delivered ledger records it, and a dev node''s\n`LoggingEmailProvider` yields `logged` — never `sent`. The drawer can no longer\nclaim an email went out that did not.\n\n**The gateway**: three public routes beside `/public/invite/accept`, outside the\nsession layer, per-IP rate limited by the same fixed-window limiter.\n`GET /r/{token}` stays a PAGE url served by the SPA fallback. A dead link is a\n410 whose body is a disjunction — completed, withdrawn, or timed out, never\nwhich.\n\n`Principal::constraint` is a capability set and cannot narrow to a record id, so\nthe constraint carries the request id as a marker and the two verbs enforce the\nnarrowing themselves.\n\nRegistration: `tool_call.rs` (party.* exact + dispatch), `tool_gate.rs` (two\naliases), `builtin_roles.rs` (send → AUTHOR, party.* → ADMIN), the catalog rows\nfor every dispatched verb, and the three routes in `server.rs`.\n\nTests: 18 host + 5 gateway, all real (real store, real relay, real reminder\nreactor). The token they present is read out of the mail that was actually sent,\nbecause the store keeps only a hash.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(outbox): a public origin, so an emailed link is one a stranger can open\n\nFound by reading the real message a contractor receives, captured off an SMTP\ncatcher:\n\n  <a href=\"/r/lbr_nube.GSBFFPMHC27960B7ARQQBHG6GGH9AEFWZJHCT5HQ0PAJ8SN9K9NG\">\n\nA relative path. A mail client has no origin to resolve it against, so the\ncontractor''s only route into the product did not work. The token is correct,\nthe delivery is correct, and the link is dead.\n\nThis is NOT new: `invite.email.*` has always shipped `/accept?token={token}`\nthe same way. lb simply had no notion of what it is publicly called, and the\ncase plane is the first feature where that is fatal — you can tell a colleague\nthe URL of an invite; a contractor is a stranger whose only way in is the link.\n\n  BootConfig.public_base_url (LB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL)\n    -> reactors::spawn -> EmailTarget::with_base_url\n    -> content_for''s `{base_url}` catalog argument\n\nDeliberately NOT derived from gateway_addr: a node bound on 127.0.0.1 behind a\nproxy is publicly something else, and only the operator knows what. It lives on\nthe TARGET, not in the effect payload — it is a property of the deployment, not\nof the ask, and a producer that had to pass it is one that could pass the wrong\none, with a credential in the link.\n\n`with_base_url` is a builder rather than a fourth `new` argument so every\nexisting caller keeps compiling with the old behaviour. Unset renders EMPTY,\nnever the `{base_url}` literal, and the trailing `/` is trimmed so an empty\norigin can never produce `//r/token` — which a browser reads as a\nprotocol-relative URL to a host called `r`.\n\nOnly `request_link` copy is changed here. `invite.email.*` has the same defect\nand the same fix available, but it is a shipped surface with its own\ntranslations; changing it belongs to whoever owns invites, not to this PR.\n\nVerified RED then green: reverting the catalog line alone fails\n`a_public_origin_makes_the_request_link_absolute` and nothing else. Confirmed\nend to end on a live node — the captured mail now carries\nhttps://insights.nube-io.example/r/lbr_nube.XK29M7GXR3V2Q7T2SY6MG\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* chore(clippy): clean the branch''s own lints, and the caller I missed\n\nboot_wiring_test still called reactors::spawn with seven arguments — a real\ncompile error in a test binary, which `cargo clippy --all-targets` surfaced and\na `cargo test` of the case suites alone never would have. That is the argument\nfor --all-targets.\n\npattern.rs: `i + 1 <= MAX` is `i < MAX`.\n\nrequest_send / request_attach take an allow with the reason stated rather than a\nbag named `Args` — every argument is a distinct fact, and grouping them would be\nthe same arity with one more indirection between a caller and a mistake.\n\nZero clippy errors and no remaining warning in any file this branch introduced.\nThe warnings left in lb-host/lb-ingest/lb-mcp/federation/report/nav are\npre-existing and untouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* fix(case): the caveat echo is live, not a photograph taken at open\n\nFound driving the bas pack on a live node: an insight carried\ncaveats: [\"01M25RG7KW...\"] while its case still read caveated=false, so the\noperator''s *Send to contractor* button stayed live over a number nobody should\ntrust.\n\nCase.caveated echoes the PRIMARY insight''s caveat state and was written once, at\nopen, and never revisited. lb_insights::raise recomputes Insight.caveats on\nEVERY firing, deliberately, and says why in its own comment: \"a merge would make\na caveat permanent, which is the failure mode that teaches operators to ignore\nit\". A frozen echo reintroduces exactly that, in both directions:\n\n  - a case opened BEFORE its sensor broke never shows the soft-block — a\n    contractor dispatched against untrusted data, the one outcome the caveat\n    exists to prevent;\n  - a case opened WHILE caveated keeps the badge after the sensor is fixed — the\n    permanent caveat the insight layer refuses to create.\n\nThe refresh runs in the arm that already handles a re-raise of an\nalready-grouped insight, beside the snooze puncture. Only the PRIMARY decides:\nCase.caveated is the primary''s state, so a caveated member must not switch the\nwhole case''s contractor button off.\n\nNew EventKind::Caveat, additive, separate from `workflow` for the same reason\n`Sla` is — it is a fact about the evidence, not a transition of the work.\n\"Why did the contractor button switch off?\" has no other place to be answered.\nIdempotent: unchanged means no write and no event; a closed case is left alone,\nso what the client was told at resolution is not rewritten afterwards.\n\nVerified RED then green — with the refresh disabled the new test fails on the\nappear half, and the clear half is one a snapshot cannot do at all. Suites:\nlb-cases 34, case_plane 15, case_reactor 9, case_delegation 6, sla_clock 9,\nscorecard 10, case_request 18, insight_caveat 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* docs(insights): the branch''s full re-verification, and what is not done\n\nTest evidence for the whole branch at its tip rather than per wave: fmt clean,\nzero clippy errors, and the 15 files carrying a clippy warning set-intersected\nagainst the branch''s 146 changed .rs files to show the overlap is empty.\n\nAll eleven case-plane suites, the ten insight/tag suites, the four reminder\nsuites, the three mail suites, `lb-host --lib` at 582, and the whole gateway role\nat 62 suites / 388 tests / 0 failures — with the two fixtures that must be built\nonce or they read as failures rather than as a missing build.\n\nRecords two things a reviewer would otherwise have to discover:\n\n  * one `cargo test -p lb-role-gateway` run died with `linking with cc failed`\n    and passed completely on an immediate re-run with nothing changed. Target-dir\n    contention, not a code fault, and worth naming before it sends somebody after\n    a linker.\n  * the two behaviour changes on shipped surfaces — the `tags.of` → `tags.find`\n    gate alias (a shipped test had encoded a contract no real caller could\n    satisfy, and was changed on purpose) and `BootConfig.public_base_url` (new,\n    defaulted None, so no embedder changes behaviour by upgrading). And that\n    `invite.email.*` still ships a relative link and was deliberately left alone,\n    because fixing it here would bundle an unrelated surface into a PR whose\n    reviewable claim is the case plane.\n\n\"What is not done\" is now explicit: not merged and not tagged; the scorecard N+1\nand its `origin_ref` fix; storm folding unbuilt though its seed exists; the four\nunstarted fast-follows; and `case.request.withdraw`/`nudge` implemented and\nunit-tested but never driven on the wire.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(outbox,insights): stop this branch pushing two files over the FILE-LAYOUT limit\n\nThe `file-layout` CI job is red on master and has been for a while, which is\nexactly the failure the ratchet in `check-file-size.sh` was built to prevent —\n\"a check that is always red is a check that is off\". So its output has to be read\nper-file, and read that way this branch had pushed **two previously-compliant\nfiles** over the 400-line limit. Those are new violations I introduced, and the\nratchet is right to call them.\n\n`outbox/email_target.rs` 389 → 463. It gained `Disposition` (what a provider''s\nack MEANS) and the `base_url` origin. Split the SHAPES out to a new\n`outbox/email_message.rs` — `EmailMessage`, `EmailMeta`, `Disposition` and the\n`sanitize_message_id` rule that keeps the `Message-ID` field legal. The target\nkeeps the behaviour between an effect row and the wire (fan-out, per-recipient\ndedup); the shapes are what every provider impl names in its signatures and they\nchange for different reasons. That is the split the outbox folder-of-verbs\nalready makes for `email_payload.rs` / `email_content.rs` / `email_attachment.rs`.\n397 lines now, and the three sibling providers import from the new module —\neverything outside `outbox/` goes through `mod.rs`, so the public path is\nunchanged.\n\n`insights/tests/ladder_test.rs` 278 → 401, one line over, from a 123-line block\nasserting \"a caveated insight is never a breakthrough\". Moved to\n`caveat_ladder_test.rs`: `ladder_test.rs` is the ladder''s OWN behaviour and this\nis a different question asked of the same function. The 400-line limit doing its\njob — two concerns had accumulated in one place. Fixtures are a local copy rather\nthan a shared `common` module: an integration test is its own crate, and thirty\nlines of obvious fixture is a cheaper dependency than a helper two suites can\nsilently drift apart over. 13 tests still, now 10 + 3.\n\nVerified: fmt clean; clippy 0 errors and no warning in any touched file;\n`lb-host --lib` 582; `mail_suite` 11, `email_transport_test` 6,\n`invite_email_relay_test` 1, `case_request_test` 18, `insight_caveat_test` 8;\n`ladder_test` 10 + `caveat_ladder_test` 3.\n\nWhat this does NOT fix, stated plainly: nine of the branch''s new TEST files are\nover the limit (case_request_test 1286 down to case_token_test 404), and six\nbaseline files grew — `tool_call.rs` +86, `config.rs` +59, `server.rs` +58,\n`builder.rs` +24, `lib.rs` +20, `builtin_roles.rs` +8. The six are\nregistry/dispatch files where growth is structural: a new verb IS a dispatch arm,\na new BootConfig field IS a line in `config.rs`, a new route IS a registration in\n`server.rs`. The nine test files are a real backlog contribution and a maintainer\ncall — split them, or re-baseline once — so they are called out in the PR rather\nthan left to be found.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(cases): split the nine oversized case-plane test files\n\n`check-file-size.sh` is a RATCHET — the backlog may only shrink — and read\nper-file it showed this branch adding nine new TEST files over the 400-line\nFILE-LAYOUT limit, from `case_token_test` at 404 up to `case_request_test` at\n1286. All nine are now split by concern.\n\nTwo precedents already exist in this repo and both are followed rather than a\nthird being invented:\n\n  * `crates/host/tests/` — `agent_suite.rs` declares 29 files in `tests/agent/`\n    as modules of ONE harness, because Cargo links the whole dependency graph\n    (SurrealDB, Zenoh, wasmtime) into every top-level `tests/*.rs` at ~1 GB each.\n    The eight case suites follow it exactly: `case_suite.rs` aggregates 30 files\n    under `tests/case/` via `#[path]`, each suite being a `*_support.rs` fixture\n    module plus the focused files that use it. Eight test binaries became one,\n    which is ~7 GB less `target/` as a side effect.\n  * `role/gateway/tests/` — `common/` is the idiom there, and its own header says\n    it exists \"to stay under the FILE-LAYOUT 400-line limit\". `case_token_test.rs`\n    splits into `case_token_test.rs` (the happy path: a token views and replies\n    with no session, and `/r/{token}` stays a page) and\n    `case_token_denied_test.rs` (the refusals: dead, unknown, withdrawn, rate\n    limited) over a new `common/case_request.rs`.\n\nThe test COUNT is identical either side of the split — 86 in `case_suite`\n(plane 15, request 18, reactor 9, sla_clock 9, sla_policy 11, scorecard 10,\ncaveat 8, delegation 6) and 5 across the two gateway files. No test body was\nedited; they were moved, and each file gained a doc line saying what it covers.\nLargest new file: 307 lines.\n\nRejected: ONE shared `case/support.rs` for all eight suites. Tempting, since\n`principal()` is byte-identical in all eight — but `call`, `seed_roster`,\n`raise_input`, `case_of` and `seed_insight` each have a different variant per\nsuite (different caps, different seeds, different signatures). Merging them would\nhave been a behaviour-carrying rewrite of eight green suites to satisfy a line\ncount. Each suite keeps its own fixtures, moved verbatim.\n\nRejected: re-baselining with `--update`. The script says the list \"may only\nSHRINK\", and adding nine entries to the backlog to turn a job green is the move\nthat made it red on master in the first place.\n\nThis branch now adds ZERO new FILE-LAYOUT violations. Six baseline files still\ngrow — `tool_call.rs` +86, `config.rs` +59, `server.rs` +58, `builder.rs` +24,\n`lib.rs` +20, `builtin_roles.rs` +8 — and that is left alone deliberately: growth\nis structural in all six (a new verb IS a dispatch arm, a new `BootConfig` field\nIS a line in `config.rs`, a new route IS a registration in `server.rs`), and\nsplitting them is a core refactor with a blast radius far wider than this branch.\n\nVerified: `cargo fmt --all --check` clean; clippy 0 errors and no warning in any\nfile this branch touches; `case_suite` 86; `lb-host --lib` 582; `lb-cases`\n34/17/7; the whole gateway role 64 suites / 393 tests / 0 failures (one more\nbinary than before, same test total).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(prefs): the Spanish builtins were missing the six contractor-email keys\n\nCI''s `build-and-test (rest)` shard caught what my own verification did not:\n`lb-prefs --test generated_catalog_test` fails on this branch.\n\nWave 2 added six keys to the English builtin catalog — `request_link.email.{subject,body,body_html}`\nand `request_nudge.email.{subject,body,body_html}` — and none to `es.mf`. The test asserts the two\nbuiltins are key-for-key aligned, and its header says exactly why: \"a key present in one builtin but\nnot the other renders as a bare key for some locale\". So a Spanish-locale workspace would have\nemailed a contractor a literal `request_link.email.subject` as the subject line of the one message\nthe whole round trip depends on them opening.\n\nAdded the six es counterparts, with the same placeholders (`{base_url}`, `{token}`) and the same two\ncomments the en file carries — including the one explaining why the nudge deliberately has no link\n(the raw token exists once, in the original mail, and minting a second would kill the live one).\n\nWHY I MISSED IT, since the guard already existed and works: I verified per-crate over the crates this\nbranch changes — lb-cases, lb-host, lb-role-gateway, lb-insights — and lb-prefs is not one of them.\nA catalog in an untouched crate is exactly the kind of coupling that shard exists to catch. The\nbranch''s verification now runs what CI runs:\n\n    cargo test --workspace --exclude lb-host --exclude lb-role-gateway --no-fail-fast\n\n`lb-prefs` 13/9/15/1/8/1/3 green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): the `rest` shard, the third fixture, and the timing-sensitive suite\n\nRecords what the last round of verification actually taught, so the next person\ndoes not rediscover it:\n\n  * the branch''s verification is now `cargo test --workspace --exclude lb-host\n    --exclude lb-role-gateway --no-fail-fast` — what CI''s `rest` shard runs — and\n    it is in the evidence list because it caught the missing Spanish catalog keys\n    that eight per-crate green suites could not;\n  * a THIRD fixture (`extensions/hello`) must be built once, or `lb-role-acp` and\n    `lb-role-registry-host` read as failures. Both panics say \"Build it first\",\n    which is the good case; naming it here costs nothing;\n  * `federation --test result_cache_test` is TTL-timing sensitive — 2 failures\n    under default parallelism, 50/50 with `--test-threads=1`. Neither it nor the\n    fixture gap is a code fault, and CI hits neither.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b62a62eac2305ebd999e785f50c091253d582649","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/b62a62eac2305ebd999e785f50c091253d582649"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a","verification":{"payload":"tree b62a62eac2305ebd999e785f50c091253d582649\nparent 12ba78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789109945 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789109945 +1000\n\nThe Case plane — lb-cases, the case/SLA/party verbs, the reactors and the contractor token surface (#202)\n\n* docs(insights): the lb-side Case plane scope + session doc\n\nDerives the upstream half from the rubix-ai product scope: the lb-cases records,\nthe case.* verb surface with its cap tiers and gate aliases, the case-group /\nhold-down / sla-clock reactors, the GET /r/{token} token principal, and the\nchanges the insight raise hot path takes (the tags.* dispatcher door, the\nHuman > Producer fold, evidence.subjects[], the data-quality caveat stamp,\ncategory validated against a workspace-declared vocabulary, month_hist +\npattern, the case_id echo).\n\nIndexed in docs/scope/insights/README.md.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): pin the verdict seam precisely in the case-plane scope\n\nRead the shipped producer rather than assuming its shape. `body.explains[]`\nholds dedup_key STRINGS (not insight ids) and `body.root_cause` names the\nupstream finding, so the reactor resolves through dedup_lookup with a\nget-by-id fallback and sets `primary_insight` to the ROOT fault, not the\nrecord that names it. Both orderings (verdict-first, verdict-last) are\nspecified, and a human-placed member is never moved in either direction.\n\nStill two generic body keys and nothing else out of `body` — rule 10 intact.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cases): service_policy, the business-hours calendar and the SLA deadline arithmetic\n\nThe `service_policy` half of the case plane (docs/scope/insights/case-plane-scope.md):\nthe contract record, the calendar its hours are counted in, the pure deadline maths the\nsla-clock reactor will call, and the most-specific-wins policy resolution.\n\n- `policy.rs` — `ServicePolicy` + `PolicyMatch` (three OPAQUE strings; rule 10 holds:\n  nothing here names a pack, rule, site, extension or category value). `hold_down_days`\n  defaults to 14 per the scope: a rule''s own window measures detection cadence, not how\n  long a repair takes to prove itself. `respond_h`/`resolve_h` deliberately carry NO\n  serde default — a row without a deadline must fail to decode loudly rather than\n  quietly govern work as \"0 hours\" (instantly breached).\n- `calendar.rs` — `Calendar::{Always, Business}` + `DayHours`. **hours[0] is MONDAY**,\n  documented loudly: an off-by-one here does not crash, it silently bills a Sunday as a\n  Monday. `validate()` is the write door, so the arithmetic downstream stays total.\n- `deadline.rs` — `add_business_hours` / `respond_by` / `due_at`. Pure: injected `now`,\n  no store, no wall clock. Terminates on a never-opening calendar via a bounded scan and\n  the documented `NEVER` sentinel — the fail-safe direction, since the alternative\n  failure breaches every case in the workspace at once.\n- `policy_match.rs` — most specific wins; a policy with a pinned axis that CONTRADICTS\n  the query is disqualified outright, never scored as a partial fit. Ties by `id` asc.\n- `policy_list.rs` — the list order IS the resolution ladder, so an admin can read\n  precedence off the settings page instead of out of the code.\n\nTimezones are IANA names, not fixed offsets. `chrono-tz` is already a first-class\nworkspace dep (lb-schedules, lb-prefs, lb-host), so the honest option costs no new\ndependency — and `a_daylight_saving_transition_shortens_the_wall_gap` pins the hour a\nfixed offset would have got wrong twice a year.\n\n32 tests, all pure. The scope''s named case is `respond_by_crosses_a_weekend`: Mon-Fri\n09:00-17:00, opened Friday 16:00, respond_h 2 -> Monday 10:00.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): record the captured baseline — all 11 touched suites green at f8633b6d\n\nlb master is red on some jobs, so ''not my failure'' has to be provable. Every\nsuite this session touches (lb-insights + 11 named lb-host test binaries\ncovering insight, tags, outbox and reminders) is GREEN on untouched\norigin/master, which means none of the usual excuses apply to these eleven.\n\nStates plainly what is NOT baselined (the other ~100 lb-host binaries and the\ngateway) rather than implying a comparison that was never run.\n\nAlso records the disk incident: a second worktree target/ took the box to 100%\nof 916GB and surfaced as a zigcc SIGABRT on an unrelated crate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(insights): evidence.subjects, caveats, case_id, month_hist + pattern, and the workspace category vocabulary\n\nThe `lb-insights` half of the case plane (docs/scope/insights/case-plane-scope.md).\n\n- `evidence.subjects[]` — opaque point refs the finding was derived from. lb never\n  resolves one; it set-intersects them, which is what lets an open data-quality\n  finding gate everything derived from the same points.\n- `caveats[]` + `RaiseOutcome.caveated` — stamped at raise from the OPEN findings in\n  the workspace''s declared gating category whose subjects intersect this one''s.\n  REFRESHED (not merged) on every raise, so resolving the gating finding clears it.\n- **A caveated insight is never a breakthrough** — `Intent.caveated` suppresses the\n  breakthrough check AND the L0 immediate post in `ladder.rs`. Suppressing only the\n  breakthrough would leave the first raise of a caveated key posting anyway.\n  The accounting still runs, so it rides the digest: suppressed, not silenced.\n- `vocab.rs` — the `tag_vocab:{key}` record + `validate_category`. Rule 10: lb ships\n  NO default value list. An unseeded workspace validates anything; a seeded one\n  rejects an undeclared value naming the declared set. The gating category is a\n  parameter read from that row, never a literal in the crate.\n- `pattern.rs` — non-evicting `month_hist[12]` (calendar month of `ts`, UTC) and a\n  PURE `derive_pattern` → new | flapping | seasonal | chronic, every threshold\n  documented with why. Pure so a record classifies the same way on every read.\n- `case_ref.rs` — `set_case_id`, mirroring `tags_echo.rs` (skip the write when\n  unchanged; a missing record is Ok, not an error a reactor pass dies on).\n\nEvery new field is `#[serde(default)]` + skipped when empty, so a record written\nbefore it landed decodes and round-trips without gaining a key. Tests: the four\npattern branches, the calendar anchors, the size guard counting `subjects`, the\nvocab fallback, the caveat delivery pair (control + caveated) in ladder_test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(host/insight): the Human > Producer tag fold, the echo backfill job, and the tags-door tests\n\n- `insight/facets.rs` (new) — the precedence FOLD, extracted out of `raise.rs`.\n  For one tag key: Human > Producer > Inferred > System; newest `Provenance.at`\n  within a source; a remaining tie breaks on the value string so insertion order can\n  never change the answer. Before this, `materialize_facets` collected into a map and\n  kept whichever edge `tags.of` returned last — an unspecified order, so an\n  operator''s correction silently reverted on the next producer raise.\n  (docs/scope/insights/insight-tag-precedence-scope.md)\n- `insight/backfill.rs` (new) — `backfill_insight_facets`, the one-shot boot pass that\n  re-folds every insight''s echo. The fold self-heals on the next FIRING; this covers\n  the finding that never fires again, which is exactly where a correction stays\n  reverted for ever. Idempotent by construction (a second run returns 0).\n  Modelled on `heal_insight_timestamps`; wants the same call in node/src/reactors.rs.\n- `insight/raise.rs` — minimal: calls into `facets::materialize_facets` and passes\n  `outcome.caveated` into the matcher''s `InsightView`.\n- `tests/insight_tag_fold_test.rs` (new) — the fold through the real store, real tag\n  graph and real `insight.raise`, read back through BOTH `get` and `list`, plus\n  determinism and workspace isolation.\n- `tests/insight_caveat_test.rs` (new) — the caveat stamp and the workspace category\n  vocabulary end to end, including the delivery PAIR through a real subscription and\n  the real inbox: the caveated critical raise posts nothing, the control posts.\n- `tests/tags_door_test.rs` (new) — RED until the lead lands the registration, which\n  is the correct signal. It asserts positively (a missing alias is Denied, never\n  NotFound, so only a positive test catches it) that the four verbs dispatch, that\n  `tags.of` rides `mcp:tags.find:call`, and that `mcp:tags.remove:call` exists in\n  AUTHOR_CAPS.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style: cargo fmt --all over the insights + case-plane files\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(host): policy.sla.set / policy.sla.list — the admin SLA policy verbs\n\nThe host half of the service_policy slice (docs/scope/insights/case-plane-scope.md).\n\n- `host/src/case/policy_{set,list}.rs` — two admin verbs over `lb_cases`, gated by\n  `authorize_tool` before any validation so the deny stays opaque.\n- ADMIN tier for BOTH halves, in `authz/builtin_roles.rs`. The read is admin deliberately:\n  a policy row states the commercial contract (response/resolution hours per site), which\n  is not every member''s to read. A viewer sees the CONSEQUENCE — their case''s `due_at` —\n  never the terms.\n- Dispatched by EXACT name from `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT`, not a `policy.` prefix. Reserving a\n  whole namespace against a hypothetical extension whose id is `policy` is the mistake\n  `ext.list` and `update.*` already avoid (and that two colliding `ros.*` extensions once\n  demonstrated the hard way). Both gate on their own name, so neither needs a\n  `tool_gate.rs` alias.\n- `system/catalog/policy.rs` — the catalog rows. Without these the verbs dispatch but are\n  INVISIBLE to the console and the agent menu, and `host_catalog_covers_dispatch_prefixes`\n  goes red.\n\nTests (10, real `Node::boot()` + `mem://` store, no mocks): capability-deny per verb, the\nreal-vs-fictional-id oracle check, workspace isolation across a SHARED policy id, the\nladder ordering, upsert-by-id, the write-door rejects — plus the two POSITIVE gate tests\nthe scope demands, minting the REAL `workspace_admin_role_caps()` bundle. A negative test\ncannot catch a cap that exists in no bundle: a missing cap denies exactly like a present\none refusing. Only the positive test does.\n\nAlso fixes a bug in `policy_list` that only a real store could show: `lb_store::scan`\nreturns the whole `{ data, rev }` record while `read`/`list` return the inner value, so\ndecoding the wrapper yielded `None` for every row — and the deliberate \"skip a row that\nwill not decode\" tolerance swallowed it into an empty, green list. Now unwrapped, with\n`tests/policy_store_test.rs` (real `mem://` store) standing guard over the round trip.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(host): open the tags.* MCP door — dispatcher, gate alias, author cap, backfill boot job\n\nThe tag graph has had a full host service (`call_tags_tool`) and per-verb caps\nsince the tags scope, and NO dispatcher entry — so nothing could reach any of the\nfour verbs over MCP. That is what made `insight-tag-precedence-scope.md`\nunreachable in production: with no wire door to write a human-sourced edge, the\ncorrected classification the `Human > Producer` fold exists to protect could\nnever be authored in the first place.\n\n- `tool_call.rs`: the four verbs in `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT` by EXACT name (not a\n  `tags.` prefix — the `ext.list` precedent: never reserve a whole namespace\n  against a hypothetical extension whose id is `tags`), plus the dispatch arm.\n- `tool_gate.rs`: `tags.of` -> `tags.find`. Reading ONE entity''s tags is\n  `tags.find` narrowed to a single entity, not a second privilege.\n- `tags/verbs.rs`: the INNER gate aliased to match. This is the half that\n  actually bit. The outer alias alone left `tags.of` denied, because `tags_of`\n  re-checks `mcp:tags.of:call` inside itself: the two gates disagreed and the\n  strictest won — the exact shape `media.list` (gated on `media.get`) already\n  records in tool_gate.rs''s own notes. An alias whose inner gate disagrees is\n  decorative.\n- `builtin_roles.rs`: `mcp:tags.remove:call` joins AUTHOR beside `tags.add`. It\n  was in NO bundle, so remove would have been Denied for every caller, admins\n  included, the moment the door opened.\n- `lib.rs` + `node/src/reactors.rs`: export and boot the one-shot echo backfill.\n  The fold self-heals on the next firing, so the backfill exists for the finding\n  that never fires again — where an operator''s correction would otherwise stay\n  silently reverted for ever.\n\nBEHAVIOUR CHANGE, stated loudly: `tags_test::denies_each_verb_without_its_grant`\ngranted `mcp:tags.of:call` and asserted `tags.of` succeeded under it. That cap\nexists in no role bundle, so the test asserted a contract only a hand-built test\nprincipal could ever satisfy. It now grants `mcp:tags.find:call`. The read/write\nsplit the test exists to prove is unchanged.\n\nEvidence: tags_door_test 5/5 (2/5 before this commit — red-before-green).\ntags_test 3/3 and tags_isolation_test 1/1, both matching the master baseline\ncaptured at f8633b6d.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cases): the case plane — lb-cases, the host verbs, and the two grouping reactors\n\nAn insight is a detection. A case is a piece of work. They are different\nrecords, because work is many-to-one with detections: one gateway drop is 400\ndetections and one job; one chiller that short-cycles every summer is one\ndetection key and three jobs over three years. Putting workflow on the detection\nrow breaks both — so the raise hot path never learns what a purchase order is.\n\n- `lb-cases` (new crate, `rust/crates/cases/`): the `case`, `case_member` and\n  `case_event` records plus one pure verb per file over the store seam. No\n  authorization and no wall clock — `lb-insights`'' altitude exactly.\n- `host/src/case/`: the capability chokepoint. Eleven verbs, four of which gate\n  on a cap that is not their name (members/events -> case.get, merge/split ->\n  case.open, assign/snooze/comment -> case.workflow), each with the\n  `tool_gate.rs` arm a missing alias would otherwise turn into a bare `Denied`\n  for every caller including admins.\n\nTwo invariants carry the design, and both are enforced in one place:\n\n  1. `resolved` REQUIRES a `resolution`, and nothing else may carry one\n     (`workflow.rs`). Closed-with-no-reason cannot be told from a false positive\n     six months later, and the hold-down reactor has nothing to judge.\n  2. Every open insight is in exactly ONE open case (`member_add.rs` refuses to\n     break it; `merge`/`split` are the verbs that legitimately move members).\n     Without it the queue double-counts and two technicians work one fault from\n     two rows.\n\nThe reactors:\n\n- **case-group** runs INLINE at the end of `insight_raise`, not only on a loop:\n  a scan-only reactor makes the invariant merely EVENTUALLY true, so a UI that\n  raises then lists sees a case-less row for one tick. A verdict record — one\n  whose body carries a non-empty `explains[]` — makes the case about the ROOT it\n  names (`body.root_cause`), with the findings it explains and the verdict record\n  itself as `explained` members. Citations are `dedup_key` strings resolved\n  through `dedup_lookup` (id fallback); an unresolvable one is warned and\n  skipped, never an error. Both real orderings work: verdict-LAST merges the\n  existing `single` cases in, verdict-FIRST opens over what resolves and folds\n  each straggler in as it arrives. A `human_placed` member is never moved.\n- **hold-down** reaches the raise path through the same call site: a re-fire\n  inside the policy''s `hold_down_days` of a `fixed` close REOPENS that case with\n  a `reopened` event saying the repair did not hold. Any other resolution lets a\n  new case open, because only `fixed` was a repair.\n- **reconcile** is the restart-safe backstop AND the triage backfill: it carries\n  `insight.assigned_to` onto the case and replays the comment thread with the\n  ORIGINAL author and timestamp. Idempotent — a second pass returns 0.\n\n`insight.assign` / `insight.comment` become delegates (resolved decision 5). The\nverb names, the caps, the bulk semantics and the return shapes are untouched;\nwhat moved is where the fact LIVES. The case is the writer and the insight keeps\nan echo, so the roster''s owner column, the subscription matcher''s `assignee`\nfilter and `insight.list`''s owner axis all still answer from the insight. All 17\nshipped triage tests and all 22 insight tests pass unchanged. The one intended\nbehaviour change: assigning one detection assigns every detection its case\ncites, because three symptoms of one fault are one job with one owner.\n\nRule 10 throughout. Nothing here names a pack, a rule, a category value or an\nextension; `category`/`site`/`scope` are opaque workspace strings, and the only\nthing read out of `body` is the generic citation grammar — in ONE file\n(`case/verdict.rs`) so no other file can quietly learn a second key.\n\nTests (30 new, all green): capability-deny and workspace-isolation on every\nverb; a POSITIVE gate test per aliased verb (a missing alias is `Denied`, not\n`NotFound`, so only a call that must succeed catches it); the resolution and\nexclusivity invariants; 50 re-raises moving nothing on the human plane; a\nseverity escalation puncturing a snooze; the Mine lane resolving through TEAMS;\nboth verdict orderings; hold-down reopen vs new case. Every reactor case asserts\nRED with the gating cap removed — and that no case was created — before it\nasserts green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(host): prove policy.sla.* is visible in tools.catalog, not just dispatchable\n\nThe console and the agent''s menu are both built from `tools.catalog`, so a verb absent\nfrom the host catalog can only be called by someone who already knows its name — the\nshape in which `datasource.`/`viz.`/`flows.` once went missing. Asserted from my own test\nfile so the check does not depend on the crate-wide\n`host_catalog_covers_dispatch_prefixes` unit test, which is currently red on the `case.`\nprefix (cases-core''s catalog family, not this slice).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(host/catalog): the tags.* family the dispatcher door left unadvertised\n\nOpening the `tags.*` MCP door added four verbs to `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT` without\ncatalog rows, and `host_catalog_covers_dispatch_prefixes` asserts every\ndispatched verb has one — so `cargo test -p lb-host --lib` was red for the whole\nwave on `host catalog has no entry for dispatched verb ''tags.add''`. Two agents\nreported it independently, which is the guard doing exactly its job: a verb that\ndispatches but is invisible in `tools.catalog` is reachable by anyone who\nalready knows its name and undiscoverable by everyone else.\n\n`tags.of` is advertised under the `tags.find` read cap it actually gates on. The\ncatalog''s cardinal rule — advertise a tool only if the call would allow it,\nnever hide one that would pass — holds only if the catalog asks the gate''s own\nquestion, and `tags.of` is aliased in both `tool_gate.rs` and\n`tags/authorize.rs`.\n\nlb-host --lib: 563 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): correct the scope against the built records\n\nThree prose details did not survive contact with the code, and the UI mirrored\nthe prose before anyone noticed: case_event.seq is `eseq` on the wire (a serde\nrename, so a reader of `seq` silently gets nothing), CaseRow is Case FLATTENED\nplus member_count rather than a nested { case, member_count }, and external_ref\nis a plain opaque string rather than the {system,id,url} struct described.\n\nRecorded as a table rather than silently edited: a downstream consumer mirroring\nthe scope is precisely how these bite, so the correction is worth more than the\ntidy version. Mirror the Rust, not the scope — a scope is the ask, the record is\nthe contract.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): split the contractor page URL from its data endpoints\n\nThe scope said `GET /r/{token}` mints the token principal. But that path is also\nwhat a human opens in a browser, and the browser must be served the SPA''s HTML\nthere — one path cannot return HTML to a browser and JSON to that page''s own\nfetch. Corrected: /r/{token} is a PAGE url served by the SPA fallback, and the\ndata surface is three public gateway routes beside /public/invite/accept.\n\nThe attachment upload is a gateway route, NOT a third MCP verb: the token\nprincipal''s surface stays exactly two caps.\n\nAlso pins two things the build got right that the scope had left implicit. The\nview payload carries the plotted rows itself — a two-cap principal is Denied on\nevery query-plane verb, so the page physically cannot fetch a chart, and\nshipping the rows is what keeps its whole surface equal to one verb''s response.\nAnd the 410 body states the possibilities as a disjunction, because \"this token\nexpired\" tells a prober the string was real.\n\nFound by the UI half building against the scope and hitting the contradiction.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cases): rule.scorecard — precision per origin.ref per site, read-only\n\nThe feedback loop from resolution back to detection (case-plane scope §7).\n`origin.ref` names the producer; `case.resolution` names what a human concluded\nwhen the work finished. This joins the two so \"that rule cries wolf\" becomes a\nnumber instead of a mood.\n\nThree files:\n\n- `lb-cases/scorecard.rs` — the PURE arithmetic over already-loaded rows: no\n  store, no clock, no I/O, so the formula that will be argued about in review is\n  unit-testable in microseconds. `precision = fixed / (fixed + false_positive +\n  self_cleared)`; `accepted_risk` and `duplicate` are counted and reported but\n  deliberately OUT of the denominator (accepting a budget-blocked risk is not the\n  detector being wrong, and a duplicate is a statement about the GROUPING). A zero\n  denominator is `None` — never `0.0`, which would defame a detector that has\n  simply never been judged. Median time-to-resolve is over the `fixed` cases only\n  (a false positive''s \"resolve time\" measures how long somebody took to notice it\n  was noise); even counts take the mean of the two middle values.\n- `lb-cases/resolved.rs` — the unpaged read of the workspace''s resolved cases.\n  Not `list` with `include_closed`: that verb pages at 200, and a precision\n  computed over the first 200 rows and presented as the rule''s precision is the\n  exact shape of lie this scope exists to remove.\n- `host/case/scorecard.rs` — the gated verb. VIEWER: a scorecard is an aggregate\n  of outcomes a viewer can already read one at a time, and the people who most\n  need to know a detector is crying wolf hold only read caps.\n\nA resolved case whose primary insight has since been DELETED is counted under\n`unknown` with a warn, never dropped — dropping it would shrink a denominator and\nmake every surviving precision a lie about a smaller population than the reader\nbelieves they are seeing.\n\nThe verb IS an N+1 (one scan, then one insight read per resolved case); this is\nsaid plainly in the doc comment. The fix is an `origin_ref` echo written onto the\ncase at open — the discipline `case_id` and the owner echo already use — which is\na wave-1 record change, so it is named there rather than done here.\n\n`rule.` (SINGULAR) is dispatched by EXACT name in `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT`, not as a\nprefix: the host already owns `rules.` (plural, the rules engine), and reserving a\nsecond one-letter-different namespace against a hypothetical extension named\n`rule` is the mistake `ext.list` already avoids. The two cannot shadow each other\n(`\"rule.scorecard\".starts_with(\"rules.\")` is false).\n\nNo `rule_policy` demotion: §7 makes it an explicit fast-follow, and a number that\nsilently changes how a detector fires is the \"silent code\" that section rules out.\n\nTests: 14 unit tests on the arithmetic (the formula, the excluded outcomes, the\nzero denominator, odd/even medians, the `(rule_ref, site)` pair, the surviving\n`site: None` group) and 10 integration tests on a real booted node — capability\ndeny, workspace isolation, the deleted primary insight, the filters, and a\nPOSITIVE gate test proving a principal holding only `mcp:rule.scorecard:call`\nreaches the verb, plus an assertion that the cap ships in the viewer bundle.\nVerified RED: dropping the `HOST_NATIVE_EXACT` entry turns 8 of the 10 red.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(case): the sla-clock reactor — deadlines, the breach alarm, and the breach write\n\nWave 2''s sla-clock slice. A case''s place in the queue is `due_at` ascending —\na wall-clock fact a client can be held to — never a composite score, and the\nclock never pauses.\n\nlb-cases (pure, no wall clock):\n  - `deadline_set::set_deadlines` — persist `policy_id`/`respond_by`/`due_at`\n    and append an `sla` event naming BOTH ends of every axis. Idempotent: an\n    unchanged answer writes nothing. The one exception is the first pass over a\n    case that matches NO policy — every field is `None` either way, so without\n    an explicit statement a reader cannot tell \"no contract governs this work\"\n    from \"the clock never ran\".\n  - `breach_mark::mark_breached` — set-once `breached_ts` + `breach_waiting_on`\n    (the holder AT that instant, which is what makes a breach attributable).\n    Nothing here reads `snooze_until`, and `waiting_on` never stops time.\n  - `EventKind::Sla` — a deadline is not a state transition, so it does not\n    ride the `workflow` stream.\n\nhost/src/case:\n  - `sla_clock::apply_sla` — resolve the policy through `match_policy`, compute\n    through wave 1''s PURE `respond_by()`/`due_at()`, write, arm the alarm. Both\n    deadlines are a pure function of `(policy, opened_ts)`, so \"a snooze does\n    not move the clock\" is structural rather than a rule to remember, and a\n    severity escalation recomputes because severity is a policy-match axis.\n    `NEVER` folds to absent; no matching policy leaves both absent.\n  - `breach_reminder` — a durable ONE-SHOT reminder armed to the exact deadline\n    second (`next_attempt_ts` set directly; the cron would round to the minute\n    and fire EARLY). The `schedule` is still a real cron because a denied firing\n    reschedules through it, and a `BadCron` there aborts the whole workspace''s\n    reminder pass. Arms itself with exactly one grant: `mcp:case.breach:call`\n    for `system:sla-clock`, because `fire_reminder` re-resolves caps from the\n    durable grant store and a system actor holds none by default.\n  - `breach` — the `case.breach` verb the alarm fires. Gates on its OWN name\n    (no `tool_gate.rs` arm) and its cap is in NO role bundle: a person does not\n    declare a breach.\n  - `breach_notify` — escalates to the assignee''s own channel, BYPASSING the\n    ladder for the reason `insight/assign_notify.rs` bypasses it.\n\nWired at two call sites: `case_open`, and the tail of `group_insight` (whose\nexit 1 is the severity-escalation path, so open and escalation cannot drift).\n\nTests (`sla_clock_test.rs`, real store/policies/reminder reactor, no mocks):\nFriday 16:00 + 2 business hours = Monday 10:00; the same across a holiday; an\nescalation recomputes and punctures the snooze; a snooze moves neither deadline;\nthe breach records the holder at that instant and is never rewritten; no policy\nleaves the deadlines absent and says so; the alarm ACTUALLY FIRES when\n`react_to_reminders` is driven. RED-first throughout — the raise cap removed for\nthe clock''s own path, the breach grant revoked for the alarm''s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): wave 2''s decisions — the self-grant, the alarm, the N+1\n\nSeven decisions with their rejected alternatives, and one caveat stated rather\nthan hidden.\n\nThe one worth reading twice: fire_reminder re-resolves the stored principal''s\ncaps from the durable grant store, so a system actor holding none would have\nmade the breach alarm a permanent silent denied=1. Widening reactor_caps()\nwould have looked like a fix and changed nothing — that list feeds the flow\nloop, which the reminder fire path never reads.\n\nAlso records that rule.scorecard ships a known N+1, what the fix is, and why\nit was not done in this wave.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* test(case): the five reactor cases whose RED half was missing\n\nThe module doc claimed \"Every reactor case asserts RED first\". Four of nine\ndid. A doc that overstates its own tests is worse than the gap, because it\nstops the next reader looking.\n\nThree grouping cases now run their identical FIRST raise with\nmcp:insight.raise:call removed and assert both the refusal and that nothing\nwas grouped — the half that makes the green meaningful. Without it, a grouping\nfunction that returned a hard-coded id, or never ran at all, would pass.\n\nThe two reconcile_* cases are left alone and the doc now says why:\nreconcile_cases is a node-level backfill invoked as a Rust function, not a verb\nbehind the caps wall, so there is no cap to remove and a RED half there would\nbe theatre. Their honest equivalent already exists — the workspace is asserted\nto hold zero cases BEFORE the pass, which is what makes \"the pass created it\"\na claim rather than an assumption.\n\n9 passed, 0 failed (--test-threads=1).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* feat(cases): the contractor round trip — party, case_request, the token principal\n\nWave 2''s largest slice: an external party can be asked something about a case,\nopen a tokenised page with no account, and answer — end to end, over the same\ncaps wall as every other caller.\n\n**lb-cases** (the records + pure verbs, no clock, no authorization):\n`party` (kind/contact/sites/trades/window; scorecard fields derived by query,\nnever stored) with upsert + list; `case_request` (the ask, the token HASH, the\nwindow, the reply, `delivery`) with open/status/reply/delivery verbs; the\nsix-row reply transition table; `set_waiting_on` / `set_cost_to_fix`.\n\n**host/src/case/**: `case.request.send` mints a ≥256-bit token, stores only its\nSHA-256, resolves the window (party → policy → default), stages the row AND the\nlink email in ONE outbox transaction, schedules the 50 %/80 %/breach ladder, sets\n`waiting_on` and events it. `withdraw` / `nudge` alias onto the same cap;\n`view` / `reply` are the token principal''s two verbs and gate on caps that exist\nin NO bundle, deliberately. `party.upsert` / `party.list` are admin.\n\n**delivery mirrors the provider kind** (resolved decision 7): `EmailProvider`\nnow states a `Disposition`, the delivered ledger records it, and a dev node''s\n`LoggingEmailProvider` yields `logged` — never `sent`. The drawer can no longer\nclaim an email went out that did not.\n\n**The gateway**: three public routes beside `/public/invite/accept`, outside the\nsession layer, per-IP rate limited by the same fixed-window limiter.\n`GET /r/{token}` stays a PAGE url served by the SPA fallback. A dead link is a\n410 whose body is a disjunction — completed, withdrawn, or timed out, never\nwhich.\n\n`Principal::constraint` is a capability set and cannot narrow to a record id, so\nthe constraint carries the request id as a marker and the two verbs enforce the\nnarrowing themselves.\n\nRegistration: `tool_call.rs` (party.* exact + dispatch), `tool_gate.rs` (two\naliases), `builtin_roles.rs` (send → AUTHOR, party.* → ADMIN), the catalog rows\nfor every dispatched verb, and the three routes in `server.rs`.\n\nTests: 18 host + 5 gateway, all real (real store, real relay, real reminder\nreactor). The token they present is read out of the mail that was actually sent,\nbecause the store keeps only a hash.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(outbox): a public origin, so an emailed link is one a stranger can open\n\nFound by reading the real message a contractor receives, captured off an SMTP\ncatcher:\n\n  <a href=\"/r/lbr_nube.GSBFFPMHC27960B7ARQQBHG6GGH9AEFWZJHCT5HQ0PAJ8SN9K9NG\">\n\nA relative path. A mail client has no origin to resolve it against, so the\ncontractor''s only route into the product did not work. The token is correct,\nthe delivery is correct, and the link is dead.\n\nThis is NOT new: `invite.email.*` has always shipped `/accept?token={token}`\nthe same way. lb simply had no notion of what it is publicly called, and the\ncase plane is the first feature where that is fatal — you can tell a colleague\nthe URL of an invite; a contractor is a stranger whose only way in is the link.\n\n  BootConfig.public_base_url (LB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL)\n    -> reactors::spawn -> EmailTarget::with_base_url\n    -> content_for''s `{base_url}` catalog argument\n\nDeliberately NOT derived from gateway_addr: a node bound on 127.0.0.1 behind a\nproxy is publicly something else, and only the operator knows what. It lives on\nthe TARGET, not in the effect payload — it is a property of the deployment, not\nof the ask, and a producer that had to pass it is one that could pass the wrong\none, with a credential in the link.\n\n`with_base_url` is a builder rather than a fourth `new` argument so every\nexisting caller keeps compiling with the old behaviour. Unset renders EMPTY,\nnever the `{base_url}` literal, and the trailing `/` is trimmed so an empty\norigin can never produce `//r/token` — which a browser reads as a\nprotocol-relative URL to a host called `r`.\n\nOnly `request_link` copy is changed here. `invite.email.*` has the same defect\nand the same fix available, but it is a shipped surface with its own\ntranslations; changing it belongs to whoever owns invites, not to this PR.\n\nVerified RED then green: reverting the catalog line alone fails\n`a_public_origin_makes_the_request_link_absolute` and nothing else. Confirmed\nend to end on a live node — the captured mail now carries\nhttps://insights.nube-io.example/r/lbr_nube.XK29M7GXR3V2Q7T2SY6MG\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* chore(clippy): clean the branch''s own lints, and the caller I missed\n\nboot_wiring_test still called reactors::spawn with seven arguments — a real\ncompile error in a test binary, which `cargo clippy --all-targets` surfaced and\na `cargo test` of the case suites alone never would have. That is the argument\nfor --all-targets.\n\npattern.rs: `i + 1 <= MAX` is `i < MAX`.\n\nrequest_send / request_attach take an allow with the reason stated rather than a\nbag named `Args` — every argument is a distinct fact, and grouping them would be\nthe same arity with one more indirection between a caller and a mistake.\n\nZero clippy errors and no remaining warning in any file this branch introduced.\nThe warnings left in lb-host/lb-ingest/lb-mcp/federation/report/nav are\npre-existing and untouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* fix(case): the caveat echo is live, not a photograph taken at open\n\nFound driving the bas pack on a live node: an insight carried\ncaveats: [\"01M25RG7KW...\"] while its case still read caveated=false, so the\noperator''s *Send to contractor* button stayed live over a number nobody should\ntrust.\n\nCase.caveated echoes the PRIMARY insight''s caveat state and was written once, at\nopen, and never revisited. lb_insights::raise recomputes Insight.caveats on\nEVERY firing, deliberately, and says why in its own comment: \"a merge would make\na caveat permanent, which is the failure mode that teaches operators to ignore\nit\". A frozen echo reintroduces exactly that, in both directions:\n\n  - a case opened BEFORE its sensor broke never shows the soft-block — a\n    contractor dispatched against untrusted data, the one outcome the caveat\n    exists to prevent;\n  - a case opened WHILE caveated keeps the badge after the sensor is fixed — the\n    permanent caveat the insight layer refuses to create.\n\nThe refresh runs in the arm that already handles a re-raise of an\nalready-grouped insight, beside the snooze puncture. Only the PRIMARY decides:\nCase.caveated is the primary''s state, so a caveated member must not switch the\nwhole case''s contractor button off.\n\nNew EventKind::Caveat, additive, separate from `workflow` for the same reason\n`Sla` is — it is a fact about the evidence, not a transition of the work.\n\"Why did the contractor button switch off?\" has no other place to be answered.\nIdempotent: unchanged means no write and no event; a closed case is left alone,\nso what the client was told at resolution is not rewritten afterwards.\n\nVerified RED then green — with the refresh disabled the new test fails on the\nappear half, and the clear half is one a snapshot cannot do at all. Suites:\nlb-cases 34, case_plane 15, case_reactor 9, case_delegation 6, sla_clock 9,\nscorecard 10, case_request 18, insight_caveat 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016NYQrKLrcy6HF9Y7eDokfg\n\n* docs(insights): the branch''s full re-verification, and what is not done\n\nTest evidence for the whole branch at its tip rather than per wave: fmt clean,\nzero clippy errors, and the 15 files carrying a clippy warning set-intersected\nagainst the branch''s 146 changed .rs files to show the overlap is empty.\n\nAll eleven case-plane suites, the ten insight/tag suites, the four reminder\nsuites, the three mail suites, `lb-host --lib` at 582, and the whole gateway role\nat 62 suites / 388 tests / 0 failures — with the two fixtures that must be built\nonce or they read as failures rather than as a missing build.\n\nRecords two things a reviewer would otherwise have to discover:\n\n  * one `cargo test -p lb-role-gateway` run died with `linking with cc failed`\n    and passed completely on an immediate re-run with nothing changed. Target-dir\n    contention, not a code fault, and worth naming before it sends somebody after\n    a linker.\n  * the two behaviour changes on shipped surfaces — the `tags.of` → `tags.find`\n    gate alias (a shipped test had encoded a contract no real caller could\n    satisfy, and was changed on purpose) and `BootConfig.public_base_url` (new,\n    defaulted None, so no embedder changes behaviour by upgrading). And that\n    `invite.email.*` still ships a relative link and was deliberately left alone,\n    because fixing it here would bundle an unrelated surface into a PR whose\n    reviewable claim is the case plane.\n\n\"What is not done\" is now explicit: not merged and not tagged; the scorecard N+1\nand its `origin_ref` fix; storm folding unbuilt though its seed exists; the four\nunstarted fast-follows; and `case.request.withdraw`/`nudge` implemented and\nunit-tested but never driven on the wire.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(outbox,insights): stop this branch pushing two files over the FILE-LAYOUT limit\n\nThe `file-layout` CI job is red on master and has been for a while, which is\nexactly the failure the ratchet in `check-file-size.sh` was built to prevent —\n\"a check that is always red is a check that is off\". So its output has to be read\nper-file, and read that way this branch had pushed **two previously-compliant\nfiles** over the 400-line limit. Those are new violations I introduced, and the\nratchet is right to call them.\n\n`outbox/email_target.rs` 389 → 463. It gained `Disposition` (what a provider''s\nack MEANS) and the `base_url` origin. Split the SHAPES out to a new\n`outbox/email_message.rs` — `EmailMessage`, `EmailMeta`, `Disposition` and the\n`sanitize_message_id` rule that keeps the `Message-ID` field legal. The target\nkeeps the behaviour between an effect row and the wire (fan-out, per-recipient\ndedup); the shapes are what every provider impl names in its signatures and they\nchange for different reasons. That is the split the outbox folder-of-verbs\nalready makes for `email_payload.rs` / `email_content.rs` / `email_attachment.rs`.\n397 lines now, and the three sibling providers import from the new module —\neverything outside `outbox/` goes through `mod.rs`, so the public path is\nunchanged.\n\n`insights/tests/ladder_test.rs` 278 → 401, one line over, from a 123-line block\nasserting \"a caveated insight is never a breakthrough\". Moved to\n`caveat_ladder_test.rs`: `ladder_test.rs` is the ladder''s OWN behaviour and this\nis a different question asked of the same function. The 400-line limit doing its\njob — two concerns had accumulated in one place. Fixtures are a local copy rather\nthan a shared `common` module: an integration test is its own crate, and thirty\nlines of obvious fixture is a cheaper dependency than a helper two suites can\nsilently drift apart over. 13 tests still, now 10 + 3.\n\nVerified: fmt clean; clippy 0 errors and no warning in any touched file;\n`lb-host --lib` 582; `mail_suite` 11, `email_transport_test` 6,\n`invite_email_relay_test` 1, `case_request_test` 18, `insight_caveat_test` 8;\n`ladder_test` 10 + `caveat_ladder_test` 3.\n\nWhat this does NOT fix, stated plainly: nine of the branch''s new TEST files are\nover the limit (case_request_test 1286 down to case_token_test 404), and six\nbaseline files grew — `tool_call.rs` +86, `config.rs` +59, `server.rs` +58,\n`builder.rs` +24, `lib.rs` +20, `builtin_roles.rs` +8. The six are\nregistry/dispatch files where growth is structural: a new verb IS a dispatch arm,\na new BootConfig field IS a line in `config.rs`, a new route IS a registration in\n`server.rs`. The nine test files are a real backlog contribution and a maintainer\ncall — split them, or re-baseline once — so they are called out in the PR rather\nthan left to be found.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(cases): split the nine oversized case-plane test files\n\n`check-file-size.sh` is a RATCHET — the backlog may only shrink — and read\nper-file it showed this branch adding nine new TEST files over the 400-line\nFILE-LAYOUT limit, from `case_token_test` at 404 up to `case_request_test` at\n1286. All nine are now split by concern.\n\nTwo precedents already exist in this repo and both are followed rather than a\nthird being invented:\n\n  * `crates/host/tests/` — `agent_suite.rs` declares 29 files in `tests/agent/`\n    as modules of ONE harness, because Cargo links the whole dependency graph\n    (SurrealDB, Zenoh, wasmtime) into every top-level `tests/*.rs` at ~1 GB each.\n    The eight case suites follow it exactly: `case_suite.rs` aggregates 30 files\n    under `tests/case/` via `#[path]`, each suite being a `*_support.rs` fixture\n    module plus the focused files that use it. Eight test binaries became one,\n    which is ~7 GB less `target/` as a side effect.\n  * `role/gateway/tests/` — `common/` is the idiom there, and its own header says\n    it exists \"to stay under the FILE-LAYOUT 400-line limit\". `case_token_test.rs`\n    splits into `case_token_test.rs` (the happy path: a token views and replies\n    with no session, and `/r/{token}` stays a page) and\n    `case_token_denied_test.rs` (the refusals: dead, unknown, withdrawn, rate\n    limited) over a new `common/case_request.rs`.\n\nThe test COUNT is identical either side of the split — 86 in `case_suite`\n(plane 15, request 18, reactor 9, sla_clock 9, sla_policy 11, scorecard 10,\ncaveat 8, delegation 6) and 5 across the two gateway files. No test body was\nedited; they were moved, and each file gained a doc line saying what it covers.\nLargest new file: 307 lines.\n\nRejected: ONE shared `case/support.rs` for all eight suites. Tempting, since\n`principal()` is byte-identical in all eight — but `call`, `seed_roster`,\n`raise_input`, `case_of` and `seed_insight` each have a different variant per\nsuite (different caps, different seeds, different signatures). Merging them would\nhave been a behaviour-carrying rewrite of eight green suites to satisfy a line\ncount. Each suite keeps its own fixtures, moved verbatim.\n\nRejected: re-baselining with `--update`. The script says the list \"may only\nSHRINK\", and adding nine entries to the backlog to turn a job green is the move\nthat made it red on master in the first place.\n\nThis branch now adds ZERO new FILE-LAYOUT violations. Six baseline files still\ngrow — `tool_call.rs` +86, `config.rs` +59, `server.rs` +58, `builder.rs` +24,\n`lib.rs` +20, `builtin_roles.rs` +8 — and that is left alone deliberately: growth\nis structural in all six (a new verb IS a dispatch arm, a new `BootConfig` field\nIS a line in `config.rs`, a new route IS a registration in `server.rs`), and\nsplitting them is a core refactor with a blast radius far wider than this branch.\n\nVerified: `cargo fmt --all --check` clean; clippy 0 errors and no warning in any\nfile this branch touches; `case_suite` 86; `lb-host --lib` 582; `lb-cases`\n34/17/7; the whole gateway role 64 suites / 393 tests / 0 failures (one more\nbinary than before, same test total).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(prefs): the Spanish builtins were missing the six contractor-email keys\n\nCI''s `build-and-test (rest)` shard caught what my own verification did not:\n`lb-prefs --test generated_catalog_test` fails on this branch.\n\nWave 2 added six keys to the English builtin catalog — `request_link.email.{subject,body,body_html}`\nand `request_nudge.email.{subject,body,body_html}` — and none to `es.mf`. The test asserts the two\nbuiltins are key-for-key aligned, and its header says exactly why: \"a key present in one builtin but\nnot the other renders as a bare key for some locale\". So a Spanish-locale workspace would have\nemailed a contractor a literal `request_link.email.subject` as the subject line of the one message\nthe whole round trip depends on them opening.\n\nAdded the six es counterparts, with the same placeholders (`{base_url}`, `{token}`) and the same two\ncomments the en file carries — including the one explaining why the nudge deliberately has no link\n(the raw token exists once, in the original mail, and minting a second would kill the live one).\n\nWHY I MISSED IT, since the guard already existed and works: I verified per-crate over the crates this\nbranch changes — lb-cases, lb-host, lb-role-gateway, lb-insights — and lb-prefs is not one of them.\nA catalog in an untouched crate is exactly the kind of coupling that shard exists to catch. The\nbranch''s verification now runs what CI runs:\n\n    cargo test --workspace --exclude lb-host --exclude lb-role-gateway --no-fail-fast\n\n`lb-prefs` 13/9/15/1/8/1/3 green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(insights): the `rest` shard, the third fixture, and the timing-sensitive suite\n\nRecords what the last round of verification actually taught, so the next person\ndoes not rediscover it:\n\n  * the branch''s verification is now `cargo test --workspace --exclude lb-host\n    --exclude lb-role-gateway --no-fail-fast` — what CI''s `rest` shard runs — and\n    it is in the evidence list because it caught the missing Spanish catalog keys\n    that eight per-crate green suites could not;\n  * a THIRD fixture (`extensions/hello`) must be built once, or `lb-role-acp` and\n    `lb-role-registry-host` read as failures. Both panics say \"Build it first\",\n    which is the good case; naming it here costs nothing;\n  * `federation --test result_cache_test` is TTL-timing sensitive — 2 failures\n    under default parallelism, 50/50 with `--test-threads=1`. Neither it nor the\n    fixture gap is a code fault, and CI hits neither.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqo6a5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAB9cQABMn0iGdYvOANAv7ASBk5tY7\nXt6w60MminT7Sh6Yb9Hlj55aFRvNG6WwLwHxx+7ZPNOyfSZIYem9NqHZ1JaYI9E7\nNsPP/z8A7Pc3IrWaM7dDppZdnQYrguzvgMAqVISrGfSIuTWl9OhOa5EtECPLnhkm\nrJgRr8eirAncNa4J68sb6HgSaTQNdY6XpDK/hpZUjj8xPmm0yv7SryI1NrKp2MU6\n8NkDfnEFcSdnbtx/w1euIQLAZmAm8EU3wu3NskZg9s9OsKaZTGNttuQyrJfMf7Oh\nZSJh0goJCJD9uMuEohKNMPbHBG+Y1uHhjrP7bSWbmCKKCT4fHaTm4iPdzG5zWRqh\namI4eaLuK92aCAj9bd5lWadVzxcKGyMySj4PtgZes3WEoSgg+lFuZ7Nllqw2OnIO\ngjxETTs0vnM15ecHxwox6G+02OUZrU1uFHTpD1Uwl+WAY4SULafLxmZdS1pwiX1s\nk4ub/0foMImA9XchWamEXi6NRToZOc27wvyPRrCfTpkScQqDZIKXlFl3FAR9xg0h\n8UC4CAbWKuTwT2pM4f/LOTNnS70rj1qxe7MF1kx8gsZ7kx+16jLqoTQI7ZAoDb8J\nJYO/kmvUaAZS/tdRpelX64hea2EZtD9nUhgNmcM7868vDxMXIDweRv1immpDW6Kk\n0CxgutKZ0xRwBPlixQIH\n=lacj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-11T06:59:06Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKGYyZmFkZWUzZjA3NGI5NmMwOTUyMmM3MGIzODM4ODQ4MmYyZjhhN2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/12ba78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63","sha":"12ba78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/12ba78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63"}],"sha":"f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/f2fadee3f074b96c09522c70b38388482f2f8a7a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47450908?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JemyJenesh","id":47450908,"login":"JemyJenesh","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ3NDUwOTA4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/12ba78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T11:06:39Z","email":"47450908+JemyJenesh@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Jenesh"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T11:06:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(gateway): pre-auth workspace brand read — GET /public/branding (#196)\n\nThe sign-in screen of a branded deployment showed the compiled product default\non any browser that had never signed in. Every `prefs.*` verb derives its\nworkspace from the bearer token, so pre-auth there was nothing to read a brand\nfrom: the shell could only repaint what an earlier *authenticated* visit had\ncached in localStorage, and a new device, a new browser or a cleared profile has\nno such cache. Reported downstream as NubeIO/rubix-ai#306.\n\nBuild the read seam `workspace-branding-scope.md` named and deferred:\n`GET /public/branding?ws=<ws>`, unauthenticated, returning the workspace-default\n`ui_branding` + `ui_theme` blobs and nothing else. Zero change to prefs, the\nhost, capabilities or the schema — those axes already exist; this is a read of\ndata that was already there.\n\nThe fifth pre-auth route, and a deliberate read-only break in the workspace wall\n(§7), so four invariants keep it hairline — each with a test that fails if it\ngoes:\n\n1. The whitelist is CONSTRUCTION, not filtering. The handler destructures the\n   loaded `Prefs` into two named fields and never serializes the record, so a\n   future prefs axis cannot leak by merely existing.\n2. Not a workspace-existence oracle. Unknown workspace, unbranded workspace,\n   store-rejected slug and absent `ws` all return the byte-identical\n   `200 {\"ui_branding\":null,\"ui_theme\":null}`.\n3. `ws` is required and never inferred. A \"single-workspace node answers for\n   itself\" convenience would mean enumerating workspaces for an anonymous\n   caller, which is the thing this route must not do.\n4. Rate-limited per client from day one on its own budget (60/min), beside the\n   invite limiter''s 10/min. The clock/key/429 body both public middlewares\n   shared is now one `limit()` helper.\n\nA `?ws=` query rather than a `/{ws}` path: a path segment turns a missing\nworkspace into a router 404, a different answer from an unknown one — an oracle\nby routing table. Named `branding`, not `brand`, because `brand.*` / `GET\n/brands` is already report brand profiles.\n\nTests: role/gateway/tests/public_branding_route_test.rs, 5/5, real node and\nstore, no Authorization header anywhere in the file.","tree":{"sha":"843fa23641cdfce9c91ccd010ead4e65fc74e490","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/843fa23641cdfce9c91ccd010ead4e65fc74e490"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/12ba78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63","verification":{"payload":"tree 843fa23641cdfce9c91ccd010ead4e65fc74e490\nparent f8633b6d851e9bd501333b75a8ab90dc599079ad\nauthor Jenesh <47450908+JemyJenesh@users.noreply.github.com> 1789038399 +0545\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789038399 +0545\n\nfeat(gateway): pre-auth workspace brand read — GET /public/branding (#196)\n\nThe sign-in screen of a branded deployment showed the compiled product default\non any browser that had never signed in. Every `prefs.*` verb derives its\nworkspace from the bearer token, so pre-auth there was nothing to read a brand\nfrom: the shell could only repaint what an earlier *authenticated* visit had\ncached in localStorage, and a new device, a new browser or a cleared profile has\nno such cache. Reported downstream as NubeIO/rubix-ai#306.\n\nBuild the read seam `workspace-branding-scope.md` named and deferred:\n`GET /public/branding?ws=<ws>`, unauthenticated, returning the workspace-default\n`ui_branding` + `ui_theme` blobs and nothing else. Zero change to prefs, the\nhost, capabilities or the schema — those axes already exist; this is a read of\ndata that was already there.\n\nThe fifth pre-auth route, and a deliberate read-only break in the workspace wall\n(§7), so four invariants keep it hairline — each with a test that fails if it\ngoes:\n\n1. The whitelist is CONSTRUCTION, not filtering. The handler destructures the\n   loaded `Prefs` into two named fields and never serializes the record, so a\n   future prefs axis cannot leak by merely existing.\n2. Not a workspace-existence oracle. Unknown workspace, unbranded workspace,\n   store-rejected slug and absent `ws` all return the byte-identical\n   `200 {\"ui_branding\":null,\"ui_theme\":null}`.\n3. `ws` is required and never inferred. A \"single-workspace node answers for\n   itself\" convenience would mean enumerating workspaces for an anonymous\n   caller, which is the thing this route must not do.\n4. Rate-limited per client from day one on its own budget (60/min), beside the\n   invite limiter''s 10/min. The clock/key/429 body both public middlewares\n   shared is now one `limit()` helper.\n\nA `?ws=` query rather than a `/{ws}` path: a path segment turns a missing\nworkspace into a router 404, a different answer from an unknown one — an oracle\nby routing table. Named `branding`, not `brand`, because `brand.*` / `GET\n/brands` is already report brand profiles.\n\nTests: role/gateway/tests/public_branding_route_test.rs, 5/5, real node and\nstore, no Authorization header anywhere in the file.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoo8/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsPgQAEJytIxxqHu11b5O+KN7my4r\naML2mGcR0zIY03LrgRhcI2muqSylZVatJgr4VHXn/1icrXKGMHpjXARps1eOL3V5\nKI0kebXnOe/foBzk1bvRFDTdPb3eR83gy9/C84JPQvHDUBNQGT1Om1UA4GRYXanF\nsgulDet7yNUwV/FUk9R5G48mf+q1kNQurLzOhZwWxI6IZdbucssM9A8YaBe2xSp5\nm+q/kgOz3vEds2iu+ZdGhwBC7ZRMXxYC5N68YFcsgZmQVoC4g+S9Oc