a78c336835dd86791a009a5facbbd8081cc63"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/f8633b6d851e9bd501333b75a8ab90dc599079ad/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T06:17:11Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T06:17:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(debugging): the #200 file-layout claim used the wrong baseline (#201)\n\nThe written record of #200 said the merge kept \"the identical 22 violations #198\nalready has ... so the merge adds none\". That compared against the #198 BRANCH —\nthe merge''s source, where those 8 came from — instead of the master it lands on.\n\nMeasured by running rust/scripts/check-file-size.sh on both refs: pre-merge master\nd109b016 has 14, master head ac8701b4 has 22. The merge adds 8. Seven are growth\npast an existing baseline on files already far over the limit; one is genuinely\nnew, rules/src/grid.rs at 426.\n\nThe general lesson is why this is worth an entry: an always-red check reports no\ntransition, so its own colour cannot tell you whether you made it worse. For a\ncheck like this the count is the signal, and it must be taken on the branch the\nchange lands on.\n\nNot a reason to hold node-v0.28.0 — every one is inside the pre-existing backlog''s\nshape — but they should not be recorded as zero. grid.rs is worth splitting first,\nbeing a new violation rather than more of an old one.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"25c84ebb8e4fe5cc8ac85bebc51c38045acc33e9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/25c84ebb8e4fe5cc8ac85bebc51c38045acc33e9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/f8633b6d851e9bd501333b75a8ab90dc599079ad","verification":{"payload":"tree 25c84ebb8e4fe5cc8ac85bebc51c38045acc33e9\nparent ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789021031 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789021031 +1000\n\ndocs(debugging): the #200 file-layout claim used the wrong baseline (#201)\n\nThe written record of #200 said the merge kept \"the identical 22 violations #198\nalready has ... so the merge adds none\". That compared against the #198 BRANCH —\nthe merge''s source, where those 8 came from — instead of the master it lands on.\n\nMeasured by running rust/scripts/check-file-size.sh on both refs: pre-merge master\nd109b016 has 14, master head ac8701b4 has 22. The merge adds 8. Seven are growth\npast an existing baseline on files already far over the limit; one is genuinely\nnew, rules/src/grid.rs at 426.\n\nThe general lesson is why this is worth an entry: an always-red check reports no\ntransition, so its own colour cannot tell you whether you made it worse. For a\ncheck like this the count is the signal, and it must be taken on the branch the\nchange lands on.\n\nNot a reason to hold node-v0.28.0 — every one is inside the pre-existing backlog''s\nshape — but they should not be recorded as zero. grid.rs is worth splitting first,\nbeing a new violation rather than more of an old one.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoktnCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/+sQAHV/LMDPrZR8ta8gxkbrIZQK\nnh/lLLx14gLUG49Uqs6JYkyd1LHznwUyTgUX3tajYi6K+3/nn5X44rvZnLh9yhTt\nRGBJxbN1wPxibRmvPeFkV6khDyrNOld92VN1N09qsI3cT539vUP6aFPSDA84V3R4\n31xu2Et8AMvwjjxcs8VaH1ffHiV94pyZiXJNjfFWzHlFPrYT7BePx/yJlbaStX2w\nKdNls/H9K1fK6co4xVlmc7QwsPow9TJiJUDW0LzvveuNzAMsRn9Zg+O9ZEOPJXPi\nFrm61SzIPpqRHbXJ2pCi8BZ5gTle8eIt75/gI1MhamJkRrnav78sj6FZGO25L4Dp\nHevSdUyQpn5ATNAib2IkGLFUGRhyGME5xkJpy9hf2HlvKiyrGavK/Cybupj3K1fn\nXQIrBK3TLUldOyJi9FjBBJmJZY5LTFi+Ck87NtnkA3FkiSJ/l5ukdo6+hrXKFA8O\nxLTWw01IT9WgLHSleoEEBG7q/Cpc1dDX0Qy5DPZBBMwwNXnTLDhqIcRewVhYzBh1\nGjA9+gLIp6CVNIhwZwkJMysKNa5WpIuQKq2kIsXp/aGljzfFxIgXCUcL3ODYA2EP\nrQ2CcDP2YmQonjQuch1VRHZ/KD7lhqsT4VJB0p/+h3scxsQE8ueeAPnwCZQqXbC6\nvuIaF4keWv2HPD4+J3k9\n=6L9Z\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T06:17:11Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/f8633b6d851e9bd501333b75a8ab90dc599079ad","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKGY4NjMzYjZkODUxZTliZDUwMTMzM2I3NWE4YWI5MGRjNTk5MDc5YWQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652","sha":"ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652"}],"sha":"f8633b6d851e9bd501333b75a8ab90dc599079ad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/f8633b6d851e9bd501333b75a8ab90dc599079ad"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-10T01:14:03Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-10T01:14:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Merge master into feat/agent-invoke-mcp-arm — SurrealDB 3 + the staging removal (#200)\n\n* fix(agent): dispatch `agent.invoke` over MCP, and vary a scheduled run''s job_id\n\n`agent.invoke` was built on every layer except the MCP dispatch table: the cap\n(`mcp:agent.invoke:call`), the `tools.catalog` descriptor and the routed edge→hub\npath (`invoke_remote`) all existed, but `call_agent_tool` had no `invoke` match, so\na call over `/mcp/call` fell through to `NotFound` and answered an opaque\n`403 \"no such tool\"` to a caller HOLDING the grant — a missing `match` arm that\nreads as a permissions bug.\n\nIt survived because every shipped caller uses another transport (browser →\n`POST /agent/invoke`, palette → `postAgent`, channel worker → the host fn). The\nfirst caller that needs the bridge is a REMINDER: an `Action::McpTool` fires\nthrough the `call_tool` chokepoint and has no browser, so \"run the agent on a\nschedule\" was unbuildable.\n\nThe new arm mirrors the HTTP route exactly (`reachable_tools` menu,\n`invoke_via_runtime` with `runtime = None`, same persona precedence, same opaque\nerror mapping) and adds no authority — `authorize_invoke` still runs inside and\nthe run stays bounded by `agent ∩ caller`. Two deliberate differences: `job_id` is\nrequired (a derived id would be identical on every firing), and `agent.invoke` is\nfiltered out of the menu handed to the model, since the dispatcher applies no\ndepth ceiling to host-native verbs.\n\nThe same work exposed a second defect: reminder args are static and the loop is\nidempotent on `job_id`, so a scheduled run would replay its first answer forever,\nnever calling the model. Fixed generically in `reminder/range.rs` — the module\nthat already owns fire-time payload substitution — with a `{{fire_ts}}` token\nreplaced by the fire clock''s epoch seconds in any string value. Action-agnostic\nper rule 10, and additive: a payload without the token is unchanged.\n\nProven live, not only unit-tested: `agent.invoke` over `/mcp/call` answered\n`{\"answer\":\"ok\",\"jobId\":\"mcp-real-1\"}` in 9.6s driven by a local offline Qwen3,\nand a per-minute probe reminder wrote memory slug `probe-1788764348`.\nRED-then-green verified: disabling the arm fails the guard with its message.\n\nTests: agent_invoke_mcp_test (6, incl. the regression guard and capability-deny),\n4 new range.rs unit tests. agent_suite 173/173, lb-host lib 534/534, all five\nreminder suites green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(pack): a pack declares its own schedule — the `reminders:` block\n\nEvery other pack section seeds a thing that sits inert until something drives\nit: a rule does nothing until `rules.run`, a channel until someone posts. A pack\nthat ships seven FDD rules and no schedule ships a product that detects nothing\nuntil an operator wires the cron by hand — off-manifest, undocumented, outside\nthe receipt, and not reproducible on a fresh box. The schedule IS the driver,\nand it was the one part of a domain pack that could not be declared.\n\nAdded on the `retention:` model, the closest precedent:\n\n- `lb_packs::manifest_reminder` — a dependency-free MIRROR of the\n  `reminder.create` args. `lb-reminders` depends on `lb-store` and `lb-packs` is\n  the pure half, so reusing `Action` would invert the layering. The cost is that\n  `action_kind` is a `String`, which is what makes the lint necessary.\n- `lb_packs::validate_reminder` — the lint the mirror pays for: an unknown\n  `action_kind` against the closed set, a kind missing the fields it needs, a\n  6-field quartz cron, and — as a WARNING — a static `job_id`.\n- `pack/reminder_action.rs` + a `Kind::Reminder` arm driving the same\n  `reminder_create` the public verb calls.\n\n**No cap smuggling.** The reminder is stored with the applying principal, and\nevery firing re-resolves that principal''s caps at the `call_tool` chokepoint. A\n`pack.apply` without `mcp:reminder.create:call` gets `denied` on the reminder\nobjects and an honest partial receipt; a pack-seeded schedule carries no\nauthority its admin lacks, and revoking that admin''s grant stops it.\n\nThe `job_id` warning is a warning rather than an error because the manifest\ncannot know which verbs are idempotent on which field. `job_id` without\n`{{fire_ts}}` is the one shape where the intent is unmistakable — and it is the\nexact replay trap the previous commit fixed: a static id returns the first run''s\nanswer forever, a frozen agent that looks perfectly alive.\n\nReminders plan LAST: a schedule firing `rules.run` is only meaningful once the\nrule it names exists.\n\nTests: `pack_reminders_test` (a pack seeds a schedule that will really fire —\nlistable AND with a `nextAttemptTs` past the apply clock, and `{{fire_ts}}`\nstored UNSUBSTITUTED, since baking the apply clock in would freeze every\nfiring; the per-object caps wall; LWW on re-apply; both lints) plus unit tests\nin `validate_reminder` and `manifest`. lb-packs 90/90, lb-host --lib 534/534,\nevery pack + reminder suite green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(rules): grid.size() spoke SurrealQL to DataFusion — and ai.classify was collateral\n\nThree bugs, one root: the FEDERATION seam''s shape was handled in `records()` and\nnowhere else. Found while testing whether a rule can call the model (the\n\"rule decides, model renders\" pattern, rubix-ai rules-calling-ai-scope.md).\n\n1. `size()` emitted `SELECT count() AS v FROM (…) GROUP ALL` — SurrealQL — on\n   EVERY source. Correct on the store, a hard parse error on any federation\n   source, and the error names a column position past the end of the author''s own\n   SQL so it reads as a bug in their query.\n\n   No spelling works on both: the obvious portable one-liner (`COUNT(*)`, no\n   `GROUP ALL`) fixed federation and BROKE the store (\"Unexpected token `*`\").\n   Verified both directions, then dispatched on `SourceKind` — which is already on\n   the grid; the module doc even says \"the grid carries its SourceKind so a…\".\n\n2. With the SQL fixed, `size()` still returned 0 on federation: it read the count\n   with `r.get(\"v\")`, but the federation seam returns column-aligned ARRAYS\n   (`[3]`, not `{\"v\":3}`). A wrong answer, not an error — and 0 rows reads as\n   \"always under the cap\", which defeats `ai.classify`''s over-large-grid guard\n   entirely.\n\n3. `ai.classify` then built its output with `as_object_mut()`, which is None on an\n   array row, so the label was silently dropped and it returned the UNLABELLED\n   rows. Fixed by normalizing with `row_to_map` first — the same normalizer\n   `records()` uses.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: `ai.classify` had never once worked on a federation source, i.e. on\nany time-series data — the only data the BAS/FDD path has. It is also the verb best\nsuited to a small local model (bounded rows in, one label each out, no set logic),\nso this was blocking the most promising direction, not a corner.\n\nVerified live on one node, same rule shape:\n  size() platform            5     (unchanged)\n  size() federation LIMIT 3  parse error -> 3\n  size() federation all      parse error -> 1152 (matches SELECT COUNT(*))\n  ai.classify on federation  parse error -> labels land, and are CORRECT:\n                             meter-020-zt (flatlined) -> frozen-sensor\n                             meter-021-zt (healthy)   -> healthy-sensor\n                             one metered call, 8 tokens\n\nWHY THE SUITE MISSED IT: tests/support/mod.rs''s seam keys its count branch on\n`query.contains(\"count()\")` — the SurrealQL spelling — and returns object rows for\nBOTH kinds. It bakes in the very platform assumption it is meant to test. The new\n`grid_dialect_test.rs` models each backend faithfully instead: right dialect, right\nrow shape, and each rejects the other''s. RED-then-green verified — 2 of 3 fail on\nthe original code, and the platform test passes throughout (no regression).\n\nThe lesson, and it is about which failures are cheap: the dialect bug threw and got\nfound. The two row-shape bugs returned plausible wrong values and would have sat\nthere indefinitely. A wrong answer costs more than a crash.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(prefs,ingest,viz,dashboard): unit provenance, a BAS vocabulary, 55 catalog views, a prefs tz default — and a 1000x epoch bug\n\nFive gaps found by measuring a real bench (44 dashboards / 269 cells) against\nwhat a mobile client would need to render dashboards and honour timezone/unit\npreferences. Written up downstream in rubix-ai''s\napp/docs/scope/dashboards/api-gaps-review.md.\n\n1. A SERIES NOW CARRIES ITS UNIT.\n\n`series_meta` gains a `unit` column and the viz `Field` an optional `unit`,\nstamped onto a frame''s numeric columns by host/viz/unit_attach.rs. This was the\nmissing link that made a *correct* converter unreachable: format.quantity needs\na from_unit and nothing on the data path had one — series_meta had two columns,\nseries.list returns bare strings, and Field had none while its own header\npromised \"SI/base units\".\n\nA producer declares it as a `unit` LABEL, validated against the closed enum:\n\"degrees celsius\" is REFUSED rather than recorded, because provenance that looks\nreal but converts to nothing is worse than an absent unit. Absent stays legal and\nmeans unknown. The frame never CONVERTS — that would bake one viewer''s prefs into\nshared, cacheable data.\n\n2. THE VOCABULARY LEARNED WHAT A BUILDING SPEAKS.\n\n8 dimensions / 29 units -> 18 / 58: energy, power, electric potential/current,\napparent power, volume, volume flow, illuminance, concentration, frequency, plus\nkPa which was missing from pressure all along. The measurement that forced it:\n85 of 143 unit-bearing panels (59%) declared a unit lb could not convert.\n\nVA is its OWN dimension, not power, and energy is not power: uom cannot tell VA\nfrom W, so VA->W and kWh->kW are structural CrossDimension refusals rather than\nplausible wrong numbers (the corpus contains a `custom:kW/kWh` unit).\n\n3. THE WIDGET CATALOG CARRIES 55 VIEWS, WAS 22.\n\nThe downstream shell kept a 31-entry WIRE_ALIASES table persisting a `geomap` as\nview:\"table\", so 45 of 269 cells stored a view that LIES and any client trusting\ncell.view drew ~20% of the corpus as a table, silently.\n\nThat table is not Grafana compatibility — it is a workaround for THIS catalog\nrejecting the ids on dashboard.save, and nearly every entry says \"retire once the\nlb catalog carries the id\". So the ids land here rather than adding a\n`view_resolved` field for clients to undo the lie with.\n\nNote: existing tests used \"heatmap\" as their canonical UNKNOWN view, which is now\na real one; they move to \"sunburst\".\n\n4. time.range.resolve DEFAULTS tz FROM THE CALLER''S PREFS.\n\nDST-correct calendar snapping already existed and already took a tz — it just\nnever learned who was asking, so \"today\" meant \"today in Greenwich\" for everyone,\nwhile prefs.timezone was read in exactly ONE place (format.datetime). An explicit\ntz still wins; no preference still means UTC. The verb is no longer purely\nstore-free; that one prefs read is the only touch.\n\n5. A LATENT 1000x WINDOW BUG.\n\nviz/time_override.rs claimed epoch SECONDS twice in its own header and subtracted\na seconds-valued duration from an ms clock, so timeFrom:\"6h\" asked for a\n21.6-SECOND window: silently empty, never an error. Every consumer on the path\n(series.read buckets, viz::resolution, $__timeFrom) is ms, and the client sends\nms.\n\nIt survived because no cell in the measured corpus had ever set timeFrom — and\nbecause its own tests were written from the same wrong premise and were green\nover the bug for the life of the feature, including an integration test asserting\nthe broken math against a real series.read dispatch. Both layers are re-based onto\nrealistic epoch-ms clocks whose magnitude makes the unit unmistakable. Written up\nin docs/debugging/viz/panel-time-override-was-1000x-off.md.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: Align gaining a tz. Filed downstream as additive and\npre-designed, it is neither — a real IANA zone makes the grid VARIABLE-WIDTH (a\nDST day is 23 or 25 hours), which is not align.rs''s origin + k*width model, and\nit would put a timezone database into a crate that is dependency-light on\npurpose. That is what the module''s own \"DST is deliberately NOT here\" section\nalready argues. Needs its own scope; $__timeGroup with a tz follows from it.\n\nSTILL OPEN: prefs.catalog returns i18n message strings, not the axis vocabulary,\nso a client cannot discover that unit_system is metric|imperial and every one\nhardcodes it.\n\nTests: 5 new files (39 tests), each verified red-then-green by disabling the\nmechanism and confirming the right tests fail. cargo fmt clean; every file under\nthe 400-line rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(flows): a retained inject poisoned flow_input and killed node_state workspace-wide\n\n`flows/triggers.rs` wrote the retained `flow_input` record with bare `lb_store::write`\ninstead of `write_locked`. Under the durable `kv-surrealkv` engine two writers on the\nsame row open optimistic transactions over one snapshot; one aborts with a write\nconflict, or — worse — a half-applied `rev` reads back as `Invalid revision ''…'' for\ntype ''Value''`. This is exactly the race `write_locked`''s own module docs describe on\nthe run-store; `run_store.rs` and `trigger_store.rs` were hardened for it and the\ninject path was missed.\n\nThe blast radius is the surprising part. `flows.node_state` folds retained inputs in\nby draining the WHOLE `flow_input` table (`scan_all`, deliberately no silent backstop),\nso ONE undeserializable row aborts the read for **every flow in the workspace** —\nincluding flows never written to — and it survives restarts. `flows.list` keeps\nworking throughout, so the flows page looks healthy while every dashboard control''s\nread-back is dead. Observed downstream as \"a slider writes once and every flow read is\nbroken from then on\": the write SUCCEEDS and the value is stored, it just can never be\nread again.\n\nFound by running a dashboard write-back end to end against a real store in rubix-ai.\nThe existing 19 cases here all boot `mem://`, where the race does not reproduce — they\nstayed green across the whole bug. The new test pins the invariant instead of the\ntiming: 16 concurrent retained injects must all land, and a subsequent `node_state`\nread of a DIFFERENT, never-injected flow must still deserialize. It fails on the bare\n`write` (`Transaction conflict`) and passes on `write_locked`.\n\nTwo adjacent findings, not fixed here: `flows.delete` does not clear a flow''s retained\n`flow_input` rows (a poisoned one has no route back through the API), and a fresh store\ngrants no `flows.*` caps at all, so every flow verb answers a bare `denied` that reads\nlike a capability bug in the caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(status): record the master merge, and the two breaks only --all-targets could see\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(status): the merged ingest suite is 186/0 across all 26 files\n\nCloses the one number the merge commit left provisional — the slow >100k-sample\nretention and cap tests were still running when it was written.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(viz): satisfy `cargo fmt --all --check`, stably\n\n`fmt` was already red on this branch before the merge — the same file is\nunformatted on the untouched `feat/agent-invoke-mcp-arm`, and it is not in the\nmerge''s diff. Inherited, not introduced, but it is one command to clear so CI can\nreport on the merge itself.\n\nThe `assert_eq!` wrap is rustfmt''s own. The comment above `NOW_MS` needed a real\nfix rather than rustfmt''s: it followed a statement with a trailing comment, so\nrustfmt treated it as a continuation and indented it to column 72, trailing off\nthe right edge. A blank line separates them, which both reads correctly and makes\n`cargo fmt --all --check` STABLE — running fmt again is now a no-op instead of\nre-introducing the same diff.\n\n`cargo check -p lb-host --test viz_query_test` is clean.\n\nNot addressed, because it is not this PR''s: `file-layout` is also red, with **22\nviolations — the identical 22 on the untouched PR branch** (master has 15). The\nmerge adds none; the extra 7 are #198''s own pre-existing debt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4178d4aff474910d03f239925ecceb7c35280ba9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/4178d4aff474910d03f239925ecceb7c35280ba9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652","verification":{"payload":"tree 4178d4aff474910d03f239925ecceb7c35280ba9\nparent d109b016c81ea10be48ad79cf90675b8cae8d884\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1789002843 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789002843 +1000\n\nMerge master into feat/agent-invoke-mcp-arm — SurrealDB 3 + the staging removal (#200)\n\n* fix(agent): dispatch `agent.invoke` over MCP, and vary a scheduled run''s job_id\n\n`agent.invoke` was built on every layer except the MCP dispatch table: the cap\n(`mcp:agent.invoke:call`), the `tools.catalog` descriptor and the routed edge→hub\npath (`invoke_remote`) all existed, but `call_agent_tool` had no `invoke` match, so\na call over `/mcp/call` fell through to `NotFound` and answered an opaque\n`403 \"no such tool\"` to a caller HOLDING the grant — a missing `match` arm that\nreads as a permissions bug.\n\nIt survived because every shipped caller uses another transport (browser →\n`POST /agent/invoke`, palette → `postAgent`, channel worker → the host fn). The\nfirst caller that needs the bridge is a REMINDER: an `Action::McpTool` fires\nthrough the `call_tool` chokepoint and has no browser, so \"run the agent on a\nschedule\" was unbuildable.\n\nThe new arm mirrors the HTTP route exactly (`reachable_tools` menu,\n`invoke_via_runtime` with `runtime = None`, same persona precedence, same opaque\nerror mapping) and adds no authority — `authorize_invoke` still runs inside and\nthe run stays bounded by `agent ∩ caller`. Two deliberate differences: `job_id` is\nrequired (a derived id would be identical on every firing), and `agent.invoke` is\nfiltered out of the menu handed to the model, since the dispatcher applies no\ndepth ceiling to host-native verbs.\n\nThe same work exposed a second defect: reminder args are static and the loop is\nidempotent on `job_id`, so a scheduled run would replay its first answer forever,\nnever calling the model. Fixed generically in `reminder/range.rs` — the module\nthat already owns fire-time payload substitution — with a `{{fire_ts}}` token\nreplaced by the fire clock''s epoch seconds in any string value. Action-agnostic\nper rule 10, and additive: a payload without the token is unchanged.\n\nProven live, not only unit-tested: `agent.invoke` over `/mcp/call` answered\n`{\"answer\":\"ok\",\"jobId\":\"mcp-real-1\"}` in 9.6s driven by a local offline Qwen3,\nand a per-minute probe reminder wrote memory slug `probe-1788764348`.\nRED-then-green verified: disabling the arm fails the guard with its message.\n\nTests: agent_invoke_mcp_test (6, incl. the regression guard and capability-deny),\n4 new range.rs unit tests. agent_suite 173/173, lb-host lib 534/534, all five\nreminder suites green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(pack): a pack declares its own schedule — the `reminders:` block\n\nEvery other pack section seeds a thing that sits inert until something drives\nit: a rule does nothing until `rules.run`, a channel until someone posts. A pack\nthat ships seven FDD rules and no schedule ships a product that detects nothing\nuntil an operator wires the cron by hand — off-manifest, undocumented, outside\nthe receipt, and not reproducible on a fresh box. The schedule IS the driver,\nand it was the one part of a domain pack that could not be declared.\n\nAdded on the `retention:` model, the closest precedent:\n\n- `lb_packs::manifest_reminder` — a dependency-free MIRROR of the\n  `reminder.create` args. `lb-reminders` depends on `lb-store` and `lb-packs` is\n  the pure half, so reusing `Action` would invert the layering. The cost is that\n  `action_kind` is a `String`, which is what makes the lint necessary.\n- `lb_packs::validate_reminder` — the lint the mirror pays for: an unknown\n  `action_kind` against the closed set, a kind missing the fields it needs, a\n  6-field quartz cron, and — as a WARNING — a static `job_id`.\n- `pack/reminder_action.rs` + a `Kind::Reminder` arm driving the same\n  `reminder_create` the public verb calls.\n\n**No cap smuggling.** The reminder is stored with the applying principal, and\nevery firing re-resolves that principal''s caps at the `call_tool` chokepoint. A\n`pack.apply` without `mcp:reminder.create:call` gets `denied` on the reminder\nobjects and an honest partial receipt; a pack-seeded schedule carries no\nauthority its admin lacks, and revoking that admin''s grant stops it.\n\nThe `job_id` warning is a warning rather than an error because the manifest\ncannot know which verbs are idempotent on which field. `job_id` without\n`{{fire_ts}}` is the one shape where the intent is unmistakable — and it is the\nexact replay trap the previous commit fixed: a static id returns the first run''s\nanswer forever, a frozen agent that looks perfectly alive.\n\nReminders plan LAST: a schedule firing `rules.run` is only meaningful once the\nrule it names exists.\n\nTests: `pack_reminders_test` (a pack seeds a schedule that will really fire —\nlistable AND with a `nextAttemptTs` past the apply clock, and `{{fire_ts}}`\nstored UNSUBSTITUTED, since baking the apply clock in would freeze every\nfiring; the per-object caps wall; LWW on re-apply; both lints) plus unit tests\nin `validate_reminder` and `manifest`. lb-packs 90/90, lb-host --lib 534/534,\nevery pack + reminder suite green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(rules): grid.size() spoke SurrealQL to DataFusion — and ai.classify was collateral\n\nThree bugs, one root: the FEDERATION seam''s shape was handled in `records()` and\nnowhere else. Found while testing whether a rule can call the model (the\n\"rule decides, model renders\" pattern, rubix-ai rules-calling-ai-scope.md).\n\n1. `size()` emitted `SELECT count() AS v FROM (…) GROUP ALL` — SurrealQL — on\n   EVERY source. Correct on the store, a hard parse error on any federation\n   source, and the error names a column position past the end of the author''s own\n   SQL so it reads as a bug in their query.\n\n   No spelling works on both: the obvious portable one-liner (`COUNT(*)`, no\n   `GROUP ALL`) fixed federation and BROKE the store (\"Unexpected token `*`\").\n   Verified both directions, then dispatched on `SourceKind` — which is already on\n   the grid; the module doc even says \"the grid carries its SourceKind so a…\".\n\n2. With the SQL fixed, `size()` still returned 0 on federation: it read the count\n   with `r.get(\"v\")`, but the federation seam returns column-aligned ARRAYS\n   (`[3]`, not `{\"v\":3}`). A wrong answer, not an error — and 0 rows reads as\n   \"always under the cap\", which defeats `ai.classify`''s over-large-grid guard\n   entirely.\n\n3. `ai.classify` then built its output with `as_object_mut()`, which is None on an\n   array row, so the label was silently dropped and it returned the UNLABELLED\n   rows. Fixed by normalizing with `row_to_map` first — the same normalizer\n   `records()` uses.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: `ai.classify` had never once worked on a federation source, i.e. on\nany time-series data — the only data the BAS/FDD path has. It is also the verb best\nsuited to a small local model (bounded rows in, one label each out, no set logic),\nso this was blocking the most promising direction, not a corner.\n\nVerified live on one node, same rule shape:\n  size() platform            5     (unchanged)\n  size() federation LIMIT 3  parse error -> 3\n  size() federation all      parse error -> 1152 (matches SELECT COUNT(*))\n  ai.classify on federation  parse error -> labels land, and are CORRECT:\n                             meter-020-zt (flatlined) -> frozen-sensor\n                             meter-021-zt (healthy)   -> healthy-sensor\n                             one metered call, 8 tokens\n\nWHY THE SUITE MISSED IT: tests/support/mod.rs''s seam keys its count branch on\n`query.contains(\"count()\")` — the SurrealQL spelling — and returns object rows for\nBOTH kinds. It bakes in the very platform assumption it is meant to test. The new\n`grid_dialect_test.rs` models each backend faithfully instead: right dialect, right\nrow shape, and each rejects the other''s. RED-then-green verified — 2 of 3 fail on\nthe original code, and the platform test passes throughout (no regression).\n\nThe lesson, and it is about which failures are cheap: the dialect bug threw and got\nfound. The two row-shape bugs returned plausible wrong values and would have sat\nthere indefinitely. A wrong answer costs more than a crash.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(prefs,ingest,viz,dashboard): unit provenance, a BAS vocabulary, 55 catalog views, a prefs tz default — and a 1000x epoch bug\n\nFive gaps found by measuring a real bench (44 dashboards / 269 cells) against\nwhat a mobile client would need to render dashboards and honour timezone/unit\npreferences. Written up downstream in rubix-ai''s\napp/docs/scope/dashboards/api-gaps-review.md.\n\n1. A SERIES NOW CARRIES ITS UNIT.\n\n`series_meta` gains a `unit` column and the viz `Field` an optional `unit`,\nstamped onto a frame''s numeric columns by host/viz/unit_attach.rs. This was the\nmissing link that made a *correct* converter unreachable: format.quantity needs\na from_unit and nothing on the data path had one — series_meta had two columns,\nseries.list returns bare strings, and Field had none while its own header\npromised \"SI/base units\".\n\nA producer declares it as a `unit` LABEL, validated against the closed enum:\n\"degrees celsius\" is REFUSED rather than recorded, because provenance that looks\nreal but converts to nothing is worse than an absent unit. Absent stays legal and\nmeans unknown. The frame never CONVERTS — that would bake one viewer''s prefs into\nshared, cacheable data.\n\n2. THE VOCABULARY LEARNED WHAT A BUILDING SPEAKS.\n\n8 dimensions / 29 units -> 18 / 58: energy, power, electric potential/current,\napparent power, volume, volume flow, illuminance, concentration, frequency, plus\nkPa which was missing from pressure all along. The measurement that forced it:\n85 of 143 unit-bearing panels (59%) declared a unit lb could not convert.\n\nVA is its OWN dimension, not power, and energy is not power: uom cannot tell VA\nfrom W, so VA->W and kWh->kW are structural CrossDimension refusals rather than\nplausible wrong numbers (the corpus contains a `custom:kW/kWh` unit).\n\n3. THE WIDGET CATALOG CARRIES 55 VIEWS, WAS 22.\n\nThe downstream shell kept a 31-entry WIRE_ALIASES table persisting a `geomap` as\nview:\"table\", so 45 of 269 cells stored a view that LIES and any client trusting\ncell.view drew ~20% of the corpus as a table, silently.\n\nThat table is not Grafana compatibility — it is a workaround for THIS catalog\nrejecting the ids on dashboard.save, and nearly every entry says \"retire once the\nlb catalog carries the id\". So the ids land here rather than adding a\n`view_resolved` field for clients to undo the lie with.\n\nNote: existing tests used \"heatmap\" as their canonical UNKNOWN view, which is now\na real one; they move to \"sunburst\".\n\n4. time.range.resolve DEFAULTS tz FROM THE CALLER''S PREFS.\n\nDST-correct calendar snapping already existed and already took a tz — it just\nnever learned who was asking, so \"today\" meant \"today in Greenwich\" for everyone,\nwhile prefs.timezone was read in exactly ONE place (format.datetime). An explicit\ntz still wins; no preference still means UTC. The verb is no longer purely\nstore-free; that one prefs read is the only touch.\n\n5. A LATENT 1000x WINDOW BUG.\n\nviz/time_override.rs claimed epoch SECONDS twice in its own header and subtracted\na seconds-valued duration from an ms clock, so timeFrom:\"6h\" asked for a\n21.6-SECOND window: silently empty, never an error. Every consumer on the path\n(series.read buckets, viz::resolution, $__timeFrom) is ms, and the client sends\nms.\n\nIt survived because no cell in the measured corpus had ever set timeFrom — and\nbecause its own tests were written from the same wrong premise and were green\nover the bug for the life of the feature, including an integration test asserting\nthe broken math against a real series.read dispatch. Both layers are re-based onto\nrealistic epoch-ms clocks whose magnitude makes the unit unmistakable. Written up\nin docs/debugging/viz/panel-time-override-was-1000x-off.md.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: Align gaining a tz. Filed downstream as additive and\npre-designed, it is neither — a real IANA zone makes the grid VARIABLE-WIDTH (a\nDST day is 23 or 25 hours), which is not align.rs''s origin + k*width model, and\nit would put a timezone database into a crate that is dependency-light on\npurpose. That is what the module''s own \"DST is deliberately NOT here\" section\nalready argues. Needs its own scope; $__timeGroup with a tz follows from it.\n\nSTILL OPEN: prefs.catalog returns i18n message strings, not the axis vocabulary,\nso a client cannot discover that unit_system is metric|imperial and every one\nhardcodes it.\n\nTests: 5 new files (39 tests), each verified red-then-green by disabling the\nmechanism and confirming the right tests fail. cargo fmt clean; every file under\nthe 400-line rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(flows): a retained inject poisoned flow_input and killed node_state workspace-wide\n\n`flows/triggers.rs` wrote the retained `flow_input` record with bare `lb_store::write`\ninstead of `write_locked`. Under the durable `kv-surrealkv` engine two writers on the\nsame row open optimistic transactions over one snapshot; one aborts with a write\nconflict, or — worse — a half-applied `rev` reads back as `Invalid revision ''…'' for\ntype ''Value''`. This is exactly the race `write_locked`''s own module docs describe on\nthe run-store; `run_store.rs` and `trigger_store.rs` were hardened for it and the\ninject path was missed.\n\nThe blast radius is the surprising part. `flows.node_state` folds retained inputs in\nby draining the WHOLE `flow_input` table (`scan_all`, deliberately no silent backstop),\nso ONE undeserializable row aborts the read for **every flow in the workspace** —\nincluding flows never written to — and it survives restarts. `flows.list` keeps\nworking throughout, so the flows page looks healthy while every dashboard control''s\nread-back is dead. Observed downstream as \"a slider writes once and every flow read is\nbroken from then on\": the write SUCCEEDS and the value is stored, it just can never be\nread again.\n\nFound by running a dashboard write-back end to end against a real store in rubix-ai.\nThe existing 19 cases here all boot `mem://`, where the race does not reproduce — they\nstayed green across the whole bug. The new test pins the invariant instead of the\ntiming: 16 concurrent retained injects must all land, and a subsequent `node_state`\nread of a DIFFERENT, never-injected flow must still deserialize. It fails on the bare\n`write` (`Transaction conflict`) and passes on `write_locked`.\n\nTwo adjacent findings, not fixed here: `flows.delete` does not clear a flow''s retained\n`flow_input` rows (a poisoned one has no route back through the API), and a fresh store\ngrants no `flows.*` caps at all, so every flow verb answers a bare `denied` that reads\nlike a capability bug in the caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(status): record the master merge, and the two breaks only --all-targets could see\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(status): the merged ingest suite is 186/0 across all 26 files\n\nCloses the one number the merge commit left provisional — the slow >100k-sample\nretention and cap tests were still running when it was written.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(viz): satisfy `cargo fmt --all --check`, stably\n\n`fmt` was already red on this branch before the merge — the same file is\nunformatted on the untouched `feat/agent-invoke-mcp-arm`, and it is not in the\nmerge''s diff. Inherited, not introduced, but it is one command to clear so CI can\nreport on the merge itself.\n\nThe `assert_eq!` wrap is rustfmt''s own. The comment above `NOW_MS` needed a real\nfix rather than rustfmt''s: it followed a statement with a trailing comment, so\nrustfmt treated it as a continuation and indented it to column 72, trailing off\nthe right edge. A blank line separates them, which both reads correctly and makes\n`cargo fmt --all --check` STABLE — running fmt again is now a no-op instead of\nre-introducing the same diff.\n\n`cargo check -p lb-host --test viz_query_test` is clean.\n\nNot addressed, because it is not this PR''s: `file-layout` is also red, with **22\nviolations — the identical 22 on the untouched PR branch** (master has 15). The\nmerge adds none; the extra 7 are #198''s own pre-existing debt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqogRbCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1kEQADctCgJkNAmbIAAp+fPYenx+\nLvUQoRsTmi/uIIvI0rnvwnm5wY0DdhBHIrOsH9HbNnVj0TYvKlhrawKm2sGyRWyh\nokgRNBET78pN4Qgauiq3mo+BjK/PeMDSnprpR5J2qpXYabS+rGwg1o6C8bEjGK3j\nqMStJePJt5X068VUZXy3m+YJ+nBa4f5U5obSVfNY7ReDfNxrRfS4SQPwxtepqyDJ\ngyp8B98rUhJ2ZFKk7QKWJFbuZQMMHxsTenhHclCR6ZXnduh7aApRvzAivN7GWEAH\nmhlxRWwoxgaTMY17wD+WlXZT48gA7tudM/0rNuZ8Uf/pbPwW1Eg7lIWkM3KYX/7J\nQiCO61Zeu8w+h2mwH1Lwq9f57jFUSNluqXpmCc1U5erim1A6ugbHXy4m10Rfs2UM\n1SnH+TwYwKLOi/1hcofkVXrRj3IdToD6olHuq3mtBoxg4eFV28hZV93Wn6ZaFQuZ\nGPvwLWdccvs10dRU5wQyNHmPjMXQ8z5WElMM/1rTKMcxqKLJmF7x31NBTOaEz+WC\naiD9kbju26U+OcKiVe0A9EVv/XgZ9eQA50zshy+FuY/kI7p3tq6PTYBF56miSP35\nkyJxt4CxwrPuBmlV03xpQrWAPGe8WGS0QoS+DAVcpwxXvmOt9AQy8lNV9etLj6L3\nyrvNPDN2RxCKdogeFgZR\n=34C1\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/ac8701b4c13871d6274d92e0023b82b9d9966652","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKGFjODcwMWI0YzEzODcxZDYyNzRkOTJlMDAyM2I4MmI5ZDk5NjY2NTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/d109b016c81ea10be48ad79cf90675b8cae8d884","sha":"