/commits/1f1ef894207bf64190d850b5b780bef4bd5bd4c2"}],"sha":"9c3002c4a5d974957e4f5ff7ed805e83df7057b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/9c3002c4a5d974957e4f5ff7ed805e83df7057b8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/1f1ef894207bf64190d850b5b780bef4bd5bd4c2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T08:12:34Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T08:12:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(mail): inbound email — a watched mailbox becomes assets, series and inbox items (#190)\n\n* feat(mail): inbound email — a watched mailbox becomes assets, series and inbox items\n\nlb could send mail with attachments and could not receive any. `mail-source-scope.md`\nwas a scope with no code, and `lb-mail` had a `send/` folder plus a comment promising a\n`fetch/` sibling. Three pieces close the loop.\n\n**1. `lb-mail`''s receive half** (`crates/mail/src/fetch/`) — one `MailFetch` trait,\n`ImapFetch` over `async-imap` on a `tokio-rustls` socket, `mail-parser` promoted from a\ndev-dependency (parsing a fetched message is production behaviour). Shaped around the\nfact that `UID FETCH n:*` **always matches something**: from a cursor at the highest UID\nan idle poll gets that message back, so a poller trusting the range re-imports the newest\nmessage on every tick for ever. The range is a SEARCH hint and every UID is filtered\nagainst the cursor in code. The mailbox is never mutated (`EXAMINE` *and* `BODY.PEEK[]`).\nSTARTTLS-on-143 is refused with a message naming the working mode rather than half-built\nover a buffered stream that cannot be safely re-wrapped.\n\n**2. A file-decoder registry** (`crates/ingest/src/decode/`) — `decode(format_id, input,\noptions)` over an **opaque** id resolved through `FORMATS`, so a new format is a new file\nin that folder and no caller changes. `nem12` (AEMO interval metering, period-ENDING, one\nseries per NMI+suffix) and `csv-grid` ship. The load-bearing decision is\n`seq = ts_ms / 1000`: file-order numbering is correct for re-importing the *same* file and\nsilently destructive for a second file covering an overlapping period, which would reuse\n`seq 0..N` for different instants and overwrite real data. An error means *no* samples; a\nbad row means *fewer*, with a warning.\n\n**3. The mail-source service** (`crates/host/src/mail/`) — `mail.source.register / list /\nget / update / delete / pause / check / poll` + `mail.formats`, admin-tier, plus\n`spawn_mail_reactors`: the driver, shipped *with* the mechanism this time. Order is the\ncontainment strategy — raw message → asset FIRST, ledger row LAST, every id derived from\nthe message key, so a crash mid-import re-imports harmlessly. The cursor is an\noptimization; the per-message **ledger** is the correctness guarantee (a UIDVALIDITY bump,\na crash before the cursor write, and a re-delivery at a new UID all defeat a cursor). The\nimporter runs as a deliberately narrow `node:mail` principal — write an asset, record an\nitem, write samples — and provably cannot read the corpus it writes into; reusing\n`flows::reactor_caps()` would have handed an untrusted external ingress `store:*:read`.\nThe sender allowlist ships v1: empty admits everyone (stated, not implicit), a domain rule\nis an exact match, and a rejection stores nothing but IS ledgered so it is auditable and\nnever re-made.\n\n`lb_inbox::Item` gains `meta: Option<Value>` — the inbox-outbox scope''s deferred open\nquestion, unblocked because mail is the second normalized source. A field, not a sibling\nrecord, because the alternative makes one inbox view join against a table whose name it\nhas to know. Nothing in the inbox ever reads inside it. All 77 `Item::new` call sites are\nunchanged.\n\nTwo bugs found by driving it on a live node, both now debug entries, both the same shape —\na chokepoint no test crosses:\n\n- the verb disagreed with its **own palette descriptor** (nested-vs-flat body, then\n  `allowSenders` declared `string` against a `Vec<String>`). `tools::validate_args` runs in\n  `call_tool_at_depth`; every test calls the host fn directly. The descriptor is now the one\n  contract.\n- a workspace admin could register a mailbox and **could not seal its password**.\n  `mcp:secret.set:call` clears the MCP gate, `lb_secrets::set` re-checks a per-path\n  `secret:<path>:write`, and the wildcard is single-segment. Fixed with `secret:mail/*:write`\n  in the admin bundle.\n\nVerified end to end on real sockets (`make mail-source-demo`): lb''s own outbox emails the\nreal 163 KB four-channel NEM12 export through a real SMTP server, lb''s own mail source\npolls it back over real IMAP → 21,120 samples across 4 series + an inbox item. Re-emailing\nthe same file leaves `raw_count` unchanged (the `seq` decision, on live data), and a\nbucketed read draws a solar curve peaking at midday — which is how the NEM+10 offset and\nthe period-ending convention got validated in a way no unit test can. Also delivered\nthrough real AWS SES.\n\nTests: `lb-mail` `imap_fetch_test` (10, against a real IMAP server on a real socket) + 34\nunit; `lb-ingest` `decode_test` (13, against the real export) + 19 unit; `lb-host`\n`mail_import_test` (11, incl. cap-deny with no record written, workspace isolation across\ntwo mailboxes, re-delivery dedup) + 17 unit. Five security/behaviour-critical tests are\nrevert-checked: the capability gate, the ledger, the `UID n:*` filter, the re-register\ncursor preservation, and the allowlist.\n\nOwed and stated in the scope: no real-TLS IMAP test; XOAUTH2 built and unit-tested but not\ndriven against a live Google/M365 tenant; one-poller-per-source is convention, not a lease;\nnothing prunes the ledger.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BxwVxDYHasWzZ6CyB5mdAj\n\n* refactor(mail): satisfy the FILE-LAYOUT gate, and unbreak `fmt` for everyone\n\nCI review of #190. Three jobs were red; **all three were already red on master**\n(`9269a88b`, this branch''s own base — same three jobs, and `build-and-test (rest)` fails\non the same test). This commit removes everything the branch itself contributed, and\nfixes one of the inherited failures on the way past.\n\n**FILE-LAYOUT — the three new violations this branch introduced are gone**, by splitting\nalong seams that were worth having anyway:\n\n- `mail/fetch/imap.rs` (433) → `imap.rs` (317) + `socket.rs` (129). The protocol half is\n  about the `n:*` trap, the read-only contract and the cursor filter; the transport half is\n  about opening a socket and protecting it. They change for different reasons — a new auth\n  mechanism touches one, a new TLS posture the other.\n- `host/src/mail/source.rs` (407) → `source.rs` (323) + `attachments.rs` (100). The record\n  answers \"where is the mailbox and how do I reach it\"; the policy answers \"what are the\n  files inside for\". Edited by different people at different times.\n- `host/tests/mail_import_test.rs` (786) → `mail/mod.rs` (163, the shared harness) +\n  `mail_import_test.rs` (389, what an arriving message becomes) + `mail_source_test.rs`\n  (284, the roster verbs and their gates). All 11 tests unchanged and still green.\n\n**`lb_host` exports the mail service as a module, not a wall of aliased names.** The\noriginal `pub use mail::{…}` re-exported 40 symbols, most of which nothing outside the\ncrate wanted, several renamed to survive the flat namespace (`mail_item_id`,\n`MAIL_MIN_POLL_SECONDS`). `pub mod mail;` is better API — `mail::message_key` reads,\n`mail_message_key` does not — and it drops the branch''s `lib.rs` cost from +19 lines to\n+1, which is simply what a new module declaration costs. `tool_call.rs` is trimmed the\nsame way, from +12 to +5 (a prefix entry and a dispatch arm; the reasoning moved to\n`mail/mod.rs` where it belongs).\n\nThose two files remain over their baselines by +1 and +5. That is the floor: **the ratchet\nhas no room for a new host-native verb family** — one cannot be added without a module\ndeclaration and a dispatch arm in two of the repo''s largest files. Worth naming rather than\nworking around; re-baselining would have laundered eight other files'' drift into this PR.\n\n**`fmt` — fixed, and it was not ours.** `cargo fmt --check` has been red on master with two\nunformatted files (`report_export_test.rs`, `report_export_media_test.rs`). They are\nformatted here because a red fmt job blocks every PR, this one included, and the fix is\nmechanical. (`report_export_media_test.rs` goes 475 → 480 lines as a result — it was\nalready over the limit either way.)\n\n**`build-and-test (rest)` — left alone, deliberately.**\n`embedder_seams_test::a_due_reminder_fires_from_the_boot_spawned_tick` fails identically on\na clean master worktree and in master''s own CI run. Not this branch''s, not this branch''s to\nfix inside a feature PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BxwVxDYHasWzZ6CyB5mdAj\n\n* test(mail): one test binary, not two — follow `agent_suite.rs`\n\nThe previous commit split `mail_import_test.rs` (786 lines) into two top-level `tests/*.rs`\nfiles to get under the FILE-LAYOUT limit. That was the wrong axis, and this repo already\nsays so: `agent_suite.rs` aggregates 29 files into ONE binary and explains why —\n\n> Cargo compiles every top-level `tests/*.rs` as its own crate, statically linking the whole\n> dependency graph (SurrealDB, Zenoh, wasmtime) into each one — ~1 GB per binary.\n\nTwo targets pay that twice, and add a second test *process* to a suite that already runs 167\ntests in parallel. That matters here beyond disk: `agent_routed_test::an_edge_invokes_the_\nhub_agent_over_the_routed_namespace` is load-sensitive — it passed on this branch''s first CI\nrun, failed on the second with `queryable not yet reachable`, and passes on demand in\nisolation (twice, locally, in 1.00s). Whether the extra binary tipped it or it was luck, the\nresponsible move is to not add the load.\n\nSo: `mail_suite.rs` declares the three files as `#[path]` modules —\n\n  mail/harness.rs      163   the real IMAP server, the real NEM12 export, the builders\n  mail/import_test.rs  388   what an arriving message BECOMES, and never twice\n  mail/source_test.rs  282   who may point the platform at a mailbox at all\n\nOne binary, every file under the limit, no new violation, all 11 tests unchanged and green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BxwVxDYHasWzZ6CyB5mdAj\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"09c08d8752d53416eb81193653304aa5c566b57a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/09c08d8752d53416eb81193653304aa5c566b57a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/1f1ef894207bf64190d850b5b780bef4bd5bd4c2","verification":{"payload":"tree 09c08d8752d53416eb81193653304aa5c566b57a\nparent 5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1787731954 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787731954 +0700\n\nfeat(mail): inbound email — a watched mailbox becomes assets, series and inbox items (#190)\n\n* feat(mail): inbound email — a watched mailbox becomes assets, series and inbox items\n\nlb could send mail with attachments and could not receive any. `mail-source-scope.md`\nwas a scope with no code, and `lb-mail` had a `send/` folder plus a comment promising a\n`fetch/` sibling. Three pieces close the loop.\n\n**1. `lb-mail`''s receive half** (`crates/mail/src/fetch/`) — one `MailFetch` trait,\n`ImapFetch` over `async-imap` on a `tokio-rustls` socket, `mail-parser` promoted from a\ndev-dependency (parsing a fetched message is production behaviour). Shaped around the\nfact that `UID FETCH n:*` **always matches something**: from a cursor at the highest UID\nan idle poll gets that message back, so a poller trusting the range re-imports the newest\nmessage on every tick for ever. The range is a SEARCH hint and every UID is filtered\nagainst the cursor in code. The mailbox is never mutated (`EXAMINE` *and* `BODY.PEEK[]`).\nSTARTTLS-on-143 is refused with a message naming the working mode rather than half-built\nover a buffered stream that cannot be safely re-wrapped.\n\n**2. A file-decoder registry** (`crates/ingest/src/decode/`) — `decode(format_id, input,\noptions)` over an **opaque** id resolved through `FORMATS`, so a new format is a new file\nin that folder and no caller changes. `nem12` (AEMO interval metering, period-ENDING, one\nseries per NMI+suffix) and `csv-grid` ship. The load-bearing decision is\n`seq = ts_ms / 1000`: file-order numbering is correct for re-importing the *same* file and\nsilently destructive for a second file covering an overlapping period, which would reuse\n`seq 0..N` for different instants and overwrite real data. An error means *no* samples; a\nbad row means *fewer*, with a warning.\n\n**3. The mail-source service** (`crates/host/src/mail/`) — `mail.source.register / list /\nget / update / delete / pause / check / poll` + `mail.formats`, admin-tier, plus\n`spawn_mail_reactors`: the driver, shipped *with* the mechanism this time. Order is the\ncontainment strategy — raw message → asset FIRST, ledger row LAST, every id derived from\nthe message key, so a crash mid-import re-imports harmlessly. The cursor is an\noptimization; the per-message **ledger** is the correctness guarantee (a UIDVALIDITY bump,\na crash before the cursor write, and a re-delivery at a new UID all defeat a cursor). The\nimporter runs as a deliberately narrow `node:mail` principal — write an asset, record an\nitem, write samples — and provably cannot read the corpus it writes into; reusing\n`flows::reactor_caps()` would have handed an untrusted external ingress `store:*:read`.\nThe sender allowlist ships v1: empty admits everyone (stated, not implicit), a domain rule\nis an exact match, and a rejection stores nothing but IS ledgered so it is auditable and\nnever re-made.\n\n`lb_inbox::Item` gains `meta: Option<Value>` — the inbox-outbox scope''s deferred open\nquestion, unblocked because mail is the second normalized source. A field, not a sibling\nrecord, because the alternative makes one inbox view join against a table whose name it\nhas to know. Nothing in the inbox ever reads inside it. All 77 `Item::new` call sites are\nunchanged.\n\nTwo bugs found by driving it on a live node, both now debug entries, both the same shape —\na chokepoint no test crosses:\n\n- the verb disagreed with its **own palette descriptor** (nested-vs-flat body, then\n  `allowSenders` declared `string` against a `Vec<String>`). `tools::validate_args` runs in\n  `call_tool_at_depth`; every test calls the host fn directly. The descriptor is now the one\n  contract.\n- a workspace admin could register a mailbox and **could not seal its password**.\n  `mcp:secret.set:call` clears the MCP gate, `lb_secrets::set` re-checks a per-path\n  `secret:<path>:write`, and the wildcard is single-segment. Fixed with `secret:mail/*:write`\n  in the admin bundle.\n\nVerified end to end on real sockets (`make mail-source-demo`): lb''s own outbox emails the\nreal 163 KB four-channel NEM12 export through a real SMTP server, lb''s own mail source\npolls it back over real IMAP → 21,120 samples across 4 series + an inbox item. Re-emailing\nthe same file leaves `raw_count` unchanged (the `seq` decision, on live data), and a\nbucketed read draws a solar curve peaking at midday — which is how the NEM+10 offset and\nthe period-ending convention got validated in a way no unit test can. Also delivered\nthrough real AWS SES.\n\nTests: `lb-mail` `imap_fetch_test` (10, against a real IMAP server on a real socket) + 34\nunit; `lb-ingest` `decode_test` (13, against the real export) + 19 unit; `lb-host`\n`mail_import_test` (11, incl. cap-deny with no record written, workspace isolation across\ntwo mailboxes, re-delivery dedup) + 17 unit. Five security/behaviour-critical tests are\nrevert-checked: the capability gate, the ledger, the `UID n:*` filter, the re-register\ncursor preservation, and the allowlist.\n\nOwed and stated in the scope: no real-TLS IMAP test; XOAUTH2 built and unit-tested but not\ndriven against a live Google/M365 tenant; one-poller-per-source is convention, not a lease;\nnothing prunes the ledger.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BxwVxDYHasWzZ6CyB5mdAj\n\n* refactor(mail): satisfy the FILE-LAYOUT gate, and unbreak `fmt` for everyone\n\nCI review of #190. Three jobs were red; **all three were already red on master**\n(`9269a88b`, this branch''s own base — same three jobs, and `build-and-test (rest)` fails\non the same test). This commit removes everything the branch itself contributed, and\nfixes one of the inherited failures on the way past.\n\n**FILE-LAYOUT — the three new violations this branch introduced are gone**, by splitting\nalong seams that were worth having anyway:\n\n- `mail/fetch/imap.rs` (433) → `imap.rs` (317) + `socket.rs` (129). The protocol half is\n  about the `n:*` trap, the read-only contract and the cursor filter; the transport half is\n  about opening a socket and protecting it. They change for different reasons — a new auth\n  mechanism touches one, a new TLS posture the other.\n- `host/src/mail/source.rs` (407) → `source.rs` (323) + `attachments.rs` (100). The record\n  answers \"where is the mailbox and how do I reach it\"; the policy answers \"what are the\n  files inside for\". Edited by different people at different times.\n- `host/tests/mail_import_test.rs` (786) → `mail/mod.rs` (163, the shared harness) +\n  `mail_import_test.rs` (389, what an arriving message becomes) + `mail_source_test.rs`\n  (284, the roster verbs and their gates). All 11 tests unchanged and still green.\n\n**`lb_host` exports the mail service as a module, not a wall of aliased names.** The\noriginal `pub use mail::{…}` re-exported 40 symbols, most of which nothing outside the\ncrate wanted, several renamed to survive the flat namespace (`mail_item_id`,\n`MAIL_MIN_POLL_SECONDS`). `pub mod mail;` is better API — `mail::message_key` reads,\n`mail_message_key` does not — and it drops the branch''s `lib.rs` cost from +19 lines to\n+1, which is simply what a new module declaration costs. `tool_call.rs` is trimmed the\nsame way, from +12 to +5 (a prefix entry and a dispatch arm; the reasoning moved to\n`mail/mod.rs` where it belongs).\n\nThose two files remain over their baselines by +1 and +5. That is the floor: **the ratchet\nhas no room for a new host-native verb family** — one cannot be added without a module\ndeclaration and a dispatch arm in two of the repo''s largest files. Worth naming rather than\nworking around; re-baselining would have laundered eight other files'' drift into this PR.\n\n**`fmt` — fixed, and it was not ours.** `cargo fmt --check` has been red on master with two\nunformatted files (`report_export_test.rs`, `report_export_media_test.rs`). They are\nformatted here because a red fmt job blocks every PR, this one included, and the fix is\nmechanical. (`report_export_media_test.rs` goes 475 → 480 lines as a result — it was\nalready over the limit either way.)\n\n**`build-and-test (rest)` — left alone, deliberately.**\n`embedder_seams_test::a_due_reminder_fires_from_the_boot_spawned_tick` fails identically on\na clean master worktree and in master''s own CI run. Not this branch''s, not this branch''s to\nfix inside a feature PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BxwVxDYHasWzZ6CyB5mdAj\n\n* test(mail): one test binary, not two — follow `agent_suite.rs`\n\nThe previous commit split `mail_import_test.rs` (786 lines) into two top-level `tests/*.rs`\nfiles to get under the FILE-LAYOUT limit. That was the wrong axis, and this repo already\nsays so: `agent_suite.rs` aggregates 29 files into ONE binary and explains why —\n\n> Cargo compiles every top-level `tests/*.rs` as its own crate, statically linking the whole\n> dependency graph (SurrealDB, Zenoh, wasmtime) into each one — ~1 GB per binary.\n\nTwo targets pay that twice, and add a second test *process* to a suite that already runs 167\ntests in parallel. That matters here beyond disk: `agent_routed_test::an_edge_invokes_the_\nhub_agent_over_the_routed_namespace` is load-sensitive — it passed on this branch''s first CI\nrun, failed on the second with `queryable not yet reachable`, and passes on demand in\nisolation (twice, locally, in 1.00s). Whether the extra binary tipped it or it was luck, the\nresponsible move is to not add the load.\n\nSo: `mail_suite.rs` declares the three files as `#[path]` modules —\n\n  mail/harness.rs      163   the real IMAP server, the real NEM12 export, the builders\n  mail/import_test.rs  388   what an arriving message BECOMES, and never twice\n  mail/source_test.rs  282   who may point the platform at a mailbox at all\n\nOne binary, every file under the limit, no new violation, all 11 tests unchanged and green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BxwVxDYHasWzZ6CyB5mdAj\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjp/yCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAiIIQAE9zxa9rULymAF6Jkup1nVC8\n8FVMIp3EIYc0A9bYtpfKSCxqjAmuygVIxX3UUhtdxF5sqY1r9RUODNJM5ERQI+Pl\nromwe9B2CvNqrXyIdAD3fSnLUAXGWnTtubu+TqgknFvPlcbHWYQfc7OC65+kKXZt\nrAn0QYhMgLXYRpTFAi3xtHeGn3DydlSV6/wMv1pqQxMDKhc7Cr8y1aF2UDb1zsm2\nvzMqjNuGtpMloj3SKnlFyfk4oPn4aSwrk+nmH3+EHJzgPRlRsQ+hDmXyTm/CMnid\nzHAkr57c6oCrQfzdg14O8hW1TfliMPf+IIVuw1SEog2/iBTWBzR0E0waNEnT3Dfj\nMGUqnv5oThsbpYCEoE/aLL/t+EEpqUY16ZWtkk0A7DnRM3PbPrxE91kyhJ+y3zg5\nqj7KQ90oVNMoZoxGE9Xaig2RB/TtvtX2c/jc1g9nwAkH5xJ2N8xqdZHM+haAMVQq\nw/67LBn/fAOOWMbHqp8EAVsV1OFfjKvJ+anSit+Ccz+ZnJZffRnxNejKH2LoQ643\nnrtRUfRpf1yf7RDlnx456v6keTuvoLoFWRTCrp6RBT4I7mI5d7CS5dNwsZXy5V7Y\nSRTUNhxm1kUlnn48WHpVKmIufJ8vcHcO8yihxdFgmeTUPw1jSJAtga44bHUNO/k+\n56Lva64Kz/neNl+j8Jgd\n=gn0D\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-26T08:12:35Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/1f1ef894207bf64190d850b5b780bef4bd5bd4c2","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDFmMWVmODk0MjA3YmY2NDE5MGQ4NTBiNWI3ODBiZWY0YmQ1YmQ0YzI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989","sha":"5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989"}],"sha":"1f1ef894207bf64190d850b5b780bef4bd5bd4c2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/1f1ef894207bf64190d850b5b780bef4bd5bd4c2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47450908?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JemyJenesh","id":47450908,"login":"JemyJenesh","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ3NDUwOTA4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T07:02:57Z","email":"47450908+JemyJenesh@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Jenesh"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T07:02:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(nav): nav.get_default — the workspace-default pointer becomes readable (#181)\n\n`nav.set_default` shipped write-only: the `workspace_nav_default:[ws]` pointer\npersisted, and nothing could ask what it holds. Downstream that read as a broken\nfeature from both directions at once — a builder''s \"Default\" badge could only echo\nits own write in the current browser session, and setting a default changes nothing\non the setting admin''s own rail, because admins deliberately skip resolve tiers 2/3\n(the no-lockout rule). A correct, persisted write was indistinguishable from a\nfailed one (NubeIO/rubix-ai#165).\n\nAdds `nav.get_default` / `GET /nav/default` → `{\"id\": \"ops\"}` or `{\"id\": null}` —\n\"no default\" is an answer a UI renders, not a 404. Wired store → call_nav_tool →\ngateway, with the catalog row and the tool_gate alias.\n\nThe gate is asymmetric on purpose: the write stays admin-ish (mcp:nav.save:call),\nthe read is member-level (mcp:nav.resolve:call). The pointer is already the third\ntier of the caller''s own resolve, so naming it discloses nothing their menu doesn''t,\nwhile gating the read on the authoring cap would hide the default from exactly the\npeople it shapes. One route, two downstream consumers: the durable admin-facing\nbadge (#165) and the no-lockout restore naming the nav to put back (#144).\n\nTests: nav_test.rs +3 (read-back/clear, member-reads-admin-writes, workspace wall)\nplus a get_default arm on the deny-per-verb test; nav_default_route_test.rs (new, 3)\nover the real gateway; nav_default_dispatch_test.rs (new, 2) — the gate-alias\ntripwire. That last one matters: a deny assertion cannot detect a missing alias\n(the outer gate answers a bare Denied either way, since `nav.` is a known\nhost-native family), so the positive dispatcher test is the only thing that trips.\nRevert-checked — deleting the alias fails it and nothing else.\n\nCo-authored-by: jp-nube <jpr@nube-io.com>","tree":{"sha":"080a9a366da500693110ee83a5d5663374b13a70","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/080a9a366da500693110ee83a5d5663374b13a70"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989","verification":{"payload":"tree 080a9a366da500693110ee83a5d5663374b13a70\nparent 57b799f2a9ffcb2766f7e1a8ae3e2e61ad0b6677\nauthor Jenesh <47450908+JemyJenesh@users.noreply.github.com> 1787727777 +0545\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787727777 +0545\n\nfeat(nav): nav.get_default — the workspace-default pointer becomes readable (#181)\n\n`nav.set_default` shipped write-only: the `workspace_nav_default:[ws]` pointer\npersisted, and nothing could ask what it holds. Downstream that read as a broken\nfeature from both directions at once — a builder''s \"Default\" badge could only echo\nits own write in the current browser session, and setting a default changes nothing\non the setting admin''s own rail, because admins deliberately skip resolve tiers 2/3\n(the no-lockout rule). A correct, persisted write was indistinguishable from a\nfailed one (NubeIO/rubix-ai#165).\n\nAdds `nav.get_default` / `GET /nav/default` → `{\"id\": \"ops\"}` or `{\"id\": null}` —\n\"no default\" is an answer a UI renders, not a 404. Wired store → call_nav_tool →\ngateway, with the catalog row and the tool_gate alias.\n\nThe gate is asymmetric on purpose: the write stays admin-ish (mcp:nav.save:call),\nthe read is member-level (mcp:nav.resolve:call). The pointer is already the third\ntier of the caller''s own resolve, so naming it discloses nothing their menu doesn''t,\nwhile gating the read on the authoring cap would hide the default from exactly the\npeople it shapes. One route, two downstream consumers: the durable admin-facing\nbadge (#165) and the no-lockout restore naming the nav to put back (#144).\n\nTests: nav_test.rs +3 (read-back/clear, member-reads-admin-writes, workspace wall)\nplus a get_default arm on the deny-per-verb test; nav_default_route_test.rs (new, 3)\nover the real gateway; nav_default_dispatch_test.rs (new, 2) — the gate-alias\ntripwire. That last one matters: a deny assertion cannot detect a missing alias\n(the outer gate answers a bare Denied either way, since `nav.` is a known\nhost-native family), so the positive dispatcher test is the only thing that trips.\nRevert-checked — deleting the alias fails it and nothing else.\n\nCo-authored-by: jp-nube <jpr@nube-io.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjo+hCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADboQAKo5+VqiDKbMr7ze/TO2yrep\nXV5F9o+AAEU5KIAIUCDaGGTHrkoUcIv07XCtxZ+VhOHKSuV6+wEUFL+q1R8S39Q4\n+y97q/468EYrOtNqGxOaHBCOJITPDEYB2xG5vp/7YkaWmIqKRcUHx943fVStENxm\n9/qMDCx6K/GWKUlK+G2nO7uNKs9IH80TjmogP//wungTDzKoAayQLzoRtrYEA4XO\nYHrNr7IyZ1nEEafwC4YzFyb7tbs35Iraq6Ez6/P4ybUmcJXuEW31suhSptsWFPjR\n2xsPWs3T17euCEOieySaVlDef+NICJofMDfGS1hotkVcgJs4sSWdlWBmmQ/govrn\nzQ8h4Wp8U1eu1Roeo9AdFh45oPUSYQB0G+660XSAXKWnaAnHv5PMzuwmcBsDCt8q\nrrpMA0aF3yVlSDd1zkK/ylhB5m2TNzZP648W0ymG91GkdktNjPRN//wI0Up8PpZu\nSF7aAhMVKISgxMFzqjREqtw/FDCJ91E7LTD1dLEfYLUG0rEAxObIffD6X3BgL9XE\n21dSVCP+JT/UMqMShozc/hOqUG7Z1Wfis+xV+UF+faM2ffbvgVPQixKMcEyMGhZX\nAaz32jE1enVJhlj2miIHncQTx+82Jce+wbeFiOraj98KHAnNp1Bg0dYRBQt0rHCE\nyLw2fWZlTNx0DflEEH1I\n=UqAT\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-26T07:02:57Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDViMWM4YmIxNzFhMDAyZDAwZDdmYjE2OGRhZmJiNzBmOTdjOTY5ODk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/57b799f2a9ffcb2766f7e1a8ae3e2e61ad0b6677","sha":"57b799f2a9ffcb2766f7e1a8ae3e2e61ad0b6677","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/57b799f2a9ffcb2766f7e1a8ae3e2e61ad0b6677"}],"sha":"5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/5b1c8bb171a002d00d7fb168dafbb70f97c96989"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47450908?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JemyJenesh","id":47450908,"login":"JemyJenesh","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ3NDUwOTA4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JemyJenesh","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/57b799f2a9ffcb2766f7e1a8ae3e2e61ad0b6677/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T06:23:46Z","email":"47450908+JemyJenesh@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Jenesh"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T06:23:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(federation): declare `resolution` on the federation.query schema (#183)\n\n`federation.query` has parsed a caller-sent `resolution` since the\nsql-time-macros scope shipped: `tool.rs` reads it, `federation_query`\ntakes it on its signature, and it is enumerated into the child input,\nwhere the query-time macro expansion consumes it. The input schema never\nnamed it.\n\nThat is the exact gap the `cache` field''s own doc comment warns about a\nfew lines above — a field the schema does not declare is invisible to a\ncaller reading the descriptor, and the palette''s guided rail cannot\nrender a control for it. `cache` shipped its parse, signature, `json!`,\nand schema together for that reason; `resolution` shipped the first\nthree.\n\nIt matters now because a caller dispatching `federation.query` DIRECTLY,\nwith no `viz.query` above it to derive and attach the window, has to pass\n`resolution` itself — the escape hatch `missing_window()` names in its\nerror text. Any statement carrying `$__timeFilter`/`$__timeGroup`/\n`$__timeTable` fails without it, so the one field that makes such a call\nwork was the one field the contract did not mention.\n\nSchema-only: no parse, dispatch, or cache-key behaviour changes, and the\nabsent `additionalProperties: false` means callers already sending\n`resolution` validated before and still do.\n\nCo-authored-by: jp-nube <jpr@nube-io.com>","tree":{"sha":"9b08e9f1a02919029b1ffe60c95a8e7f4e52cd02","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/9b08e9f1a02919029b1ffe60c95a8e7f4e52cd02"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/57b799f2a9ffcb2766f7e1a8ae3e2e61ad0b6677","verification":{"payload":"tree 9b08e9f1a02919029b1ffe60c95a8e7f4e52cd02\nparent be789e110897e8e28e5ca303f4ab6359a8e67612\nauthor Jenesh <47450908+JemyJenesh@users.noreply.github.com> 1787725426 +0545\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787725426 +0545\n\nfix(federation): declare `resolution` on the federation.query schema (#183)\n\n`federation.query` has parsed a caller-sent `resolution` since the\nsql-time-macros scope shipped: `tool.rs` reads it, `federation_query`\ntakes it on its signature, and it is enumerated into the child input,\nwhere the query-time macro expansion consumes it. The input schema never\nnamed it.\n\nThat is the exact gap the `cache` field''s own doc comment warns about a\nfew lines above — a field the schema does not declare is invisible to a\ncaller reading the descriptor, and the palette''s guided rail cannot\nrender a control for it. `cache` shipped its parse, signature, `json!`,\nand schema together for that reason; `resolution` shipped the first\nthree.\n\nIt matters now because a caller dispatching `federation.query` DIRECTLY,\nwith no `viz.query` above it to derive and attach the window, has to pass\n`resolution` itself — the escape hatch `missing_window()` names in its\nerror text. Any statement carrying `$__timeFilter`/`$__timeGroup`/\n`$__timeTable` fails without it, so the one field that makes such a call\nwork was the one field the contract did not mention.\n\nSchema-only: no parse, dispatch, or cache-key behaviour changes, and the\nabsent `additionalProperties: false` means callers already sending\n`resolution` validated before and still do.\n\nCo-authored-by: jp-nube <jpr@nube-io.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjoZyCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4nEQADhtQEkfpUAE9wcJASYXuxSm\noQflpD1DDxPoY3GTqP3+KTszxX6vdcu30ixcnyKnYP5JkYxyrsYu8Ll3AALOpaxp\nTwM40DH+FgTEAZEvpIDdEJcASLi1X0LTiHe834NytLuXRl1TaL2TjeAPAC7Ol/Xf\nGC9jJIc7D0D7ZQC05Vc9MkY6Z/lLRej//gRTx3jZpmrYALsyCsjvnikXmEOmfx