---\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T06:43:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/032e70db49c8f3ccbba125f8c0a7049e4c6bf4f0","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDAzMmU3MGRiNDljOGYzY2NiYmExMjVmOGMwYTcwNDllNGM2YmY0ZjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52","sha":"9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52"}],"sha":"032e70db49c8f3ccbba125f8c0a7049e4c6bf4f0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/032e70db49c8f3ccbba125f8c0a7049e4c6bf4f0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T06:38:39Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T06:38:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(mcp): a native sidecar is no longer serialised by the per-instance mutex (#182)\n\n* fix(mcp): a native sidecar is no longer serialised by the per-instance mutex\n\n`dispatch` held `Arc<Mutex<dyn LocalDispatch>>` across the ENTIRE round-trip, so concurrency was 1\nper extension, node-wide — however well the child multiplexed underneath.\n\nThat mutex is correct for a wasm `Instance`, which genuinely cannot run two calls at once. It is a\nwasm-shaped lock applied to a native target: a sidecar is a separate process whose wire multiplexes\n(`lb-ext-native`''s serve spawns each `call`; `Conn` correlates replies by `id`), so the exclusive\nlock bought it nothing and cost it everything.\n\nThe lower layers were already careful about exactly this — `Conn` multiplexes, and\n`native/call.rs::attempt` clones the handle out and drops the guard before the round-trip, warning\nin a comment that inlining it \"silently restores the old bug\". This layer sat above both and\nre-imposed that bug.\n\nMeasured on a live node (ext-esr, `pg_stat_activity` confirmed idle throughout; `esr.site.list`\nreads the extension''s own SurrealDB table and touches no customer database):\n\n    esr.site.list, idle sidecar             0.02 s\n    esr.site.list, one slow verb in flight   8.96 s\n    esr.site.list, immediately after         0.02 s\n\nThree cheap calls issued in parallel behind one slow verb released at the SAME instant\n(9.78/9.78/9.79 s) — a mutex signature, not a queue. A host verb beside the same slow call was\nunimpeded (`store.query` 0.01 s), which pins it here rather than at the gateway.\n\nThe fix — two defaulted `LocalDispatch` methods, so the wasm tier is untouched:\n\n- `is_multiplexing()` -> bool, default false. `SidecarDispatch` overrides it to true.\n- `call_tool_detached(&self, …) -> Option<BoxFuture<''static, …>>` builds the round-trip as an OWNED\n  future under the lock, so `dispatch` drops the guard and awaits it unlocked.\n\nThe detached shape rather than a plain `&self` method is forced by wasm: an `Instance` is not\n`Sync` (it owns wasmtime state), so a `&self` future would demand a `Sync` bound the wasm tier\ncannot satisfy. An owned future keeps that bound local to the impls that opt in.\n\nThis is a property the TARGET declares, not a Tier branch at the call site, so the registry and\n`dispatch` stay Tier-agnostic (§3.1). `SidecarDispatch`''s two paths funnel into one\n`dispatch_to_sidecar` body so they cannot drift. The re-entrancy guard is preserved: `holding`\nstill wraps the detached call, because the guard tracks the call CHAIN, not the mutex.\n\nTests in `crates/mcp/src/call/dispatch.rs`:\n- `a_multiplexing_target_is_not_serialised_by_the_instance_lock` — FAILS before the fix (the cheap\n  call blocks behind the slow one until the timeout)\n- `a_non_multiplexing_target_is_still_serialised` — pins the wasm behaviour that must NOT change\n\nBoth pre-existing re-entrancy tests pass unchanged; lb-host''s 482 tests and lb-supervisor''s suite\nare green.\n\nSee ext-esr/docs/debugging/backend/sidecar-head-of-line-blocking.md for the full write-up — this\nwas the second of two independent serialisation points; the first was the SDK''s own serve loop\n(fixed in lb-ext-sdk sdk-v0.5.0).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(mcp): rustfmt, and split dispatch tests out to stay under the FILE-LAYOUT limit\n\nTwo CI fixes, no behaviour change:\n\n- `cargo fmt --all` on the dispatch branch.\n- `dispatch.rs` had grown to 477 lines (limit 400). Its `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` moves to\n  `dispatch_tests.rs`, reached via `#[path]`, so the module path and `super::*` are unchanged and\n  the tests still report as `call::dispatch::tests::*`. Leaves dispatch.rs at 191 lines.\n\nThe remaining `check-file-size.sh` failure (`scheduled_rules_test.rs`, 1284 lines) is pre-existing:\nit is the same 1284 lines on origin/master and is untouched here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"97af32cff194884a9979ae7b72992109bc57a50b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/97af32cff194884a9979ae7b72992109bc57a50b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52","verification":{"payload":"tree 97af32cff194884a9979ae7b72992109bc57a50b\nparent d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1787121519 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787121519 +1000\n\nfix(mcp): a native sidecar is no longer serialised by the per-instance mutex (#182)\n\n* fix(mcp): a native sidecar is no longer serialised by the per-instance mutex\n\n`dispatch` held `Arc<Mutex<dyn LocalDispatch>>` across the ENTIRE round-trip, so concurrency was 1\nper extension, node-wide — however well the child multiplexed underneath.\n\nThat mutex is correct for a wasm `Instance`, which genuinely cannot run two calls at once. It is a\nwasm-shaped lock applied to a native target: a sidecar is a separate process whose wire multiplexes\n(`lb-ext-native`''s serve spawns each `call`; `Conn` correlates replies by `id`), so the exclusive\nlock bought it nothing and cost it everything.\n\nThe lower layers were already careful about exactly this — `Conn` multiplexes, and\n`native/call.rs::attempt` clones the handle out and drops the guard before the round-trip, warning\nin a comment that inlining it \"silently restores the old bug\". This layer sat above both and\nre-imposed that bug.\n\nMeasured on a live node (ext-esr, `pg_stat_activity` confirmed idle throughout; `esr.site.list`\nreads the extension''s own SurrealDB table and touches no customer database):\n\n    esr.site.list, idle sidecar             0.02 s\n    esr.site.list, one slow verb in flight   8.96 s\n    esr.site.list, immediately after         0.02 s\n\nThree cheap calls issued in parallel behind one slow verb released at the SAME instant\n(9.78/9.78/9.79 s) — a mutex signature, not a queue. A host verb beside the same slow call was\nunimpeded (`store.query` 0.01 s), which pins it here rather than at the gateway.\n\nThe fix — two defaulted `LocalDispatch` methods, so the wasm tier is untouched:\n\n- `is_multiplexing()` -> bool, default false. `SidecarDispatch` overrides it to true.\n- `call_tool_detached(&self, …) -> Option<BoxFuture<''static, …>>` builds the round-trip as an OWNED\n  future under the lock, so `dispatch` drops the guard and awaits it unlocked.\n\nThe detached shape rather than a plain `&self` method is forced by wasm: an `Instance` is not\n`Sync` (it owns wasmtime state), so a `&self` future would demand a `Sync` bound the wasm tier\ncannot satisfy. An owned future keeps that bound local to the impls that opt in.\n\nThis is a property the TARGET declares, not a Tier branch at the call site, so the registry and\n`dispatch` stay Tier-agnostic (§3.1). `SidecarDispatch`''s two paths funnel into one\n`dispatch_to_sidecar` body so they cannot drift. The re-entrancy guard is preserved: `holding`\nstill wraps the detached call, because the guard tracks the call CHAIN, not the mutex.\n\nTests in `crates/mcp/src/call/dispatch.rs`:\n- `a_multiplexing_target_is_not_serialised_by_the_instance_lock` — FAILS before the fix (the cheap\n  call blocks behind the slow one until the timeout)\n- `a_non_multiplexing_target_is_still_serialised` — pins the wasm behaviour that must NOT change\n\nBoth pre-existing re-entrancy tests pass unchanged; lb-host''s 482 tests and lb-supervisor''s suite\nare green.\n\nSee ext-esr/docs/debugging/backend/sidecar-head-of-line-blocking.md for the full write-up — this\nwas the second of two independent serialisation points; the first was the SDK''s own serve loop\n(fixed in lb-ext-sdk sdk-v0.5.0).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(mcp): rustfmt, and split dispatch tests out to stay under the FILE-LAYOUT limit\n\nTwo CI fixes, no behaviour change:\n\n- `cargo fmt --all` on the dispatch branch.\n- `dispatch.rs` had grown to 477 lines (limit 400). Its `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` moves to\n  `dispatch_tests.rs`, reached via `#[path]`, so the module path and `super::*` are unchanged and\n  the tests still report as `call::dispatch::tests::*`. Leaves dispatch.rs at 191 lines.\n\nThe remaining `check-file-size.sh` failure (`scheduled_rules_test.rs`, 1284 lines) is pre-existing:\nit is the same 1284 lines on origin/master and is untouched here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhU9vCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmgwQAJf4Wyt25zXfbaJlOUefuQAe\nb1Sj2NUtQu9ZjSTKzR0i50YLsPePjk1M5sjs6uDQLuMUEUptlns+6rmcDIaffXCQ\nvu+foeTG+zY4eNnlEcw7A29Pm7CNG8IBf584hvhwWX6zIPFm/dMwpRHGUxPAoe4G\nVl58CkrKpmZt4Uy2D14+Qx+puwgO6+TDwojQri9RLR/XJraXd3aHSNZ5GUhZ/JTV\nzOZoSwd/0a0JTaBmp1MBX3WeKwyI+b/nKzcOZLw5VWIWBXBVzJYUzCbAMeaEhG4X\nf4uGidEkif5ps6c+G2G0PbJnkusIsBkWhzbIez/Kbn75We/hYK7TbkK0Uk/hn+qA\nudEUHsREdyKP/8AAk09GkijYjSyu5lnuuZXxHYi3j7Xp4B2MvLLPTvNXTJ9iT86a\nUl2dywOtgLHpIikL3PYJPqb/1F9RDImSOyKeXySgmZDW5i3XH1DLwcFMQjOJh93V\n93pQrMepFD69sssePtLaR8fClHbLPK22UvcF9Ymg9IS2ZtPekDd55JSZjx0O0I3F\n7Yp7I7VFjPZQCBjkvpLDydh7JQmHGwbO4q3M0lPFqtODIPAsqA1Y45k+7A9hCrIz\nKAJDQRkjfrCEj/VrwVsOLg3r55HlmIOIvvvAsl1heQjKH6UcSjh/g0XkxAY+J0B/\nD5hIRFGR43VgnvwwLmaI\n=phaO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T06:38:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKDlmODZmZDBiOTE3MzNkMDk3MTIxZjAxN2Y5YmQ5NTEyMmE5ZTRhNTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39","sha":"d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39"}],"sha":"9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/9f86fd0b91733d097121f017f9bd95122a9e4a52"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-18T11:04:34Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-18T11:04:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(rules): a scheduled rule runs as its OWNER, not as the reactor (#167) (#180)\n\nScheduled rules have never once run to completion in the estate. Every fire was a\n`partialFailure` whose `rule` step read `denied`, while the same rule run by hand\nfrom the Rules page succeeded — because a manual run carries the user''s token and\na scheduled fire carries the fixed system principal `node:reactor`.\n\n#169 fixed two layers of this by granting `reactor_caps()` the verbs a rule needs:\n`rules.eval`, then `inbox.record`/`outbox.enqueue` for a body ending in `alert()`.\nA third layer remained, and it is the one that matters in production: a body whose\nfirst statement is `query(\"<datasource>\", ...)` collects via `federation.query`,\nwhich `reactor_caps()` does not grant — and should not. Granting it would hand\nevery scheduled flow on the node blanket read access to every registered\ndatasource, which is the same blanket-third-party-reach argument that function\nalready refuses for `ext-call`. Three layers in, the list was being extended one\ndenial at a time toward exactly the omnipotent system principal it was written to\navoid.\n\nSo stop asking \"what may the reactor do\" and ask \"who is this run acting for\" —\nthe run-as-owner follow-up `ext-store-nodes-scope.md` §348 reserved.\n\n`rules.save` records `SavedRule::scheduled_by` (only when the body carries a\ndirective; a run-on-demand rule already runs as whoever calls `rules.run`). A\nHEADLESS `rule` node then dispatches as that owner, with caps re-resolved LIVE\nfrom the grant store on every fire. Properties that make this not an escalation:\n\n  - identity, not credential — nothing is frozen into the record but a name, so a\n    demoted/revoked/deleted author''s schedule loses exactly the reach they lost,\n    on the next tick;\n  - never wider than the owner, who could always run the rule by hand;\n  - workspace-pinned, so a ws-A schedule cannot read ws-B;\n  - fail-closed — no owner, an unresolvable owner, or a store error all degrade to\n    the reactor principal, i.e. to the old behaviour. A miss is a denial, never a\n    widening;\n  - manual runs untouched: the swap keys strictly off the `node:reactor` subject.\n\nMigration: rules saved before this carry no owner and stay broken until claimed.\n`rules.adopt_schedule {id}` records the caller as owner (re-saving does the same).\nDeliberately NO silent backfill — stamping a guessed subject onto every ownerless\nrule is a privilege grant performed by a migration rather than by a person, and\nnothing in the store records who authored a rule before the field existed. The\nverb is gate-aliased to `mcp:rules.save:call`: a bespoke\n`mcp:rules.adopt_schedule:call` exists in no role bundle and would be unreachable\nfor every caller including admins — the `outbox.enqueue_held` / `media.upload_*`\ntrap.\n\nTwo traps hit while building this, both near-invisible:\n\n  1. grants are stored under the BARE user name while `principal.sub()` is\n     `user:test`. Passing the prefixed form to `resolve_caps_live` returns zero\n     caps, which reads as \"revoked owner\" and degrades to the reactor — failing\n     exactly like the bug it fixes. Same prefix bug as #176''s reminder fire path.\n  2. the first regression test DID use the real reactor principal — the lesson\n     from #169 — and still passed with the substitution deleted, because its rule\n     body only called `insight.raise`, a verb the reactor already holds. Every\n     verb a rule can reach without a live datasource sidecar is one\n     `reactor_caps()` grants.\n\nThe shipped regression fires under a reactor principal with `rules.eval`\ndeliberately stripped, so the run can only succeed if the node really dispatched\nunder the owner. Verified RED before green.\n\nVerified end to end on a live node against a real Postgres datasource: a headless\n1-minute probe returned 33,454 rows through `federation.query`, and four estate\npdnsw rules flipped `partialFailure` -> `success` on the first tick after adoption,\nraising real findings (two Griffith IAQ boards frozen on all 5 channels).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3b9aa20f344de0fd54ee9b21e4ecfd249509ad42","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/3b9aa20f344de0fd54ee9b21e4ecfd249509ad42"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39","verification":{"payload":"tree 3b9aa20f344de0fd54ee9b21e4ecfd249509ad42\nparent c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1787051074 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787051074 +1000\n\nfix(rules): a scheduled rule runs as its OWNER, not as the reactor (#167) (#180)\n\nScheduled rules have never once run to completion in the estate. Every fire was a\n`partialFailure` whose `rule` step read `denied`, while the same rule run by hand\nfrom the Rules page succeeded — because a manual run carries the user''s token and\na scheduled fire carries the fixed system principal `node:reactor`.\n\n#169 fixed two layers of this by granting `reactor_caps()` the verbs a rule needs:\n`rules.eval`, then `inbox.record`/`outbox.enqueue` for a body ending in `alert()`.\nA third layer remained, and it is the one that matters in production: a body whose\nfirst statement is `query(\"<datasource>\", ...)` collects via `federation.query`,\nwhich `reactor_caps()` does not grant — and should not. Granting it would hand\nevery scheduled flow on the node blanket read access to every registered\ndatasource, which is the same blanket-third-party-reach argument that function\nalready refuses for `ext-call`. Three layers in, the list was being extended one\ndenial at a time toward exactly the omnipotent system principal it was written to\navoid.\n\nSo stop asking \"what may the reactor do\" and ask \"who is this run acting for\" —\nthe run-as-owner follow-up `ext-store-nodes-scope.md` §348 reserved.\n\n`rules.save` records `SavedRule::scheduled_by` (only when the body carries a\ndirective; a run-on-demand rule already runs as whoever calls `rules.run`). A\nHEADLESS `rule` node then dispatches as that owner, with caps re-resolved LIVE\nfrom the grant store on every fire. Properties that make this not an escalation:\n\n  - identity, not credential — nothing is frozen into the record but a name, so a\n    demoted/revoked/deleted author''s schedule loses exactly the reach they lost,\n    on the next tick;\n  - never wider than the owner, who could always run the rule by hand;\n  - workspace-pinned, so a ws-A schedule cannot read ws-B;\n  - fail-closed — no owner, an unresolvable owner, or a store error all degrade to\n    the reactor principal, i.e. to the old behaviour. A miss is a denial, never a\n    widening;\n  - manual runs untouched: the swap keys strictly off the `node:reactor` subject.\n\nMigration: rules saved before this carry no owner and stay broken until claimed.\n`rules.adopt_schedule {id}` records the caller as owner (re-saving does the same).\nDeliberately NO silent backfill — stamping a guessed subject onto every ownerless\nrule is a privilege grant performed by a migration rather than by a person, and\nnothing in the store records who authored a rule before the field existed. The\nverb is gate-aliased to `mcp:rules.save:call`: a bespoke\n`mcp:rules.adopt_schedule:call` exists in no role bundle and would be unreachable\nfor every caller including admins — the `outbox.enqueue_held` / `media.upload_*`\ntrap.\n\nTwo traps hit while building this, both near-invisible:\n\n  1. grants are stored under the BARE user name while `principal.sub()` is\n     `user:test`. Passing the prefixed form to `resolve_caps_live` returns zero\n     caps, which reads as \"revoked owner\" and degrades to the reactor — failing\n     exactly like the bug it fixes. Same prefix bug as #176''s reminder fire path.\n  2. the first regression test DID use the real reactor principal — the lesson\n     from #169 — and still passed with the substitution deleted, because its rule\n     body only called `insight.raise`, a verb the reactor already holds. Every\n     verb a rule can reach without a live datasource sidecar is one\n     `reactor_caps()` grants.\n\nThe shipped regression fires under a reactor principal with `rules.eval`\ndeliberately stripped, so the run can only succeed if the node really dispatched\nunder the owner. Verified RED before green.\n\nVerified end to end on a live node against a real Postgres datasource: a headless\n1-minute probe returned 33,454 rows through `federation.query`, and four estate\npdnsw rules flipped `partialFailure` -> `success` on the first tick after adoption,\nraising real findings (two Griffith IAQ boards frozen on all 5 channels).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhDxCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAo70QAJbd+4QqZ8p0p4/ZcewiMnZ7\n2xAlSj6tXksUJmycdWrSTV6pnJn0cp4xaerh3cGysAe85Dmq4Q7+vNsQVpT8+7In\nHGdHFDhwVPKVfN/JFj3M7VzbjVqbtVgdMS/FPSQC/GXnNH42Y8F1ur6GEESOGv+g\nKV2CaKC9b/xi43dNrNhGwhaAR9Rf5ustA+73gSyotOtWRqOTJabqCVsUAZeWT/LU\nqD5lO2ezED0kqTF/Z+sJbtSlnNS+BJ1ajN9ydRzYUdzy7F6kfnu+XJ8uC3v6MN4S\nRctfNpfXZlEXA6JJ/snsHPlYtw5/gNIp/saQuXat8rmOA3qiiPNyTvnXardqwACP\nAWfMeQURQWQr1WqjyoHJPY1sHA7KoXadmDt2meBDCVBLl3WeRilsCdEr3Y3BCswP\nYrE2lmfpg8VvWBm3lUGuvNDZh3bTN2DNaOHilkPr8Hp69FEWdm7OjjvIvbCOyK77\nfXrmT6+INTNHdpFTAVLzCP7NwLnGlBG5+oxa2UOqhCGWM++nGevyEQSC8eJsp8fR\noAlYgxiuZpzUvD3RA7YnKXq5kk+3UKVVon9OtKtlkiJ/nt0XyS8zpU86vv4bPpBo\nvS8yj6AJP1aZ3YO0zpZ3T5jXf+82fCjgNXamO+tZkFRTOvU2vuTfooUB5D+OLTe9\nL7IAoMAH9DetmvTPASnG\n=Cw9s\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-18T11:04:35Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKGQyYTY3ZWEwNjY3Mzc0MWVmOTE0NzljNjFjMDc1MTk3MmYwMDVlMzk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024","sha":"c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024"}],"sha":"d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/d2a67ea06673741ef91479c61c0751972f005e39"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T23:54:48Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T23:54:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(outbox): attachments, recipient fan-out, and payload-authored words on the email target (#176)\n\n* feat(outbox): attachments, recipient fan-out, and payload-authored words on the email target\n\nThe email transport shipped (#118) and works, but the TARGET in front of it could\nonly ever deliver one thing. `EmailTarget::deliver` read `payload.email` — exactly\none address — rendered subject and body from the `invite.email.*` catalog under a\nhardcoded `action == \"send_invite\"` arm, had no notion of an attachment, and fell\nthrough to the literal subject \"Notification\" with an EMPTY body for anything else.\n`EmailMessage` had no `attachments` field at all, even though `lb_mail::MailMessage`\none layer down has had one since the transport landed.\n\nSo the deferred half of the reports scope had nowhere to land: rubix-ai renders a\nreport to PDF on a schedule, stores it as a workspace asset, and enqueues\n`{target: \"email\", action: \"report\", payload: {assetId, recipients, subject, body}}`\n— a row this target could not read, address, or attach.\n\nAttaching a file and mailing several people are things ANY producer wants, so\nneither goes behind an `if action == \"report\"`:\n\n- fan out to every address in `recipients` (plus the legacy single `email`),\n  de-duplicated, ONE message each, so every recipient has its own delivery outcome\n  and its own delivered-ledger row. A retry after a partial failure re-sends only\n  to whoever missed out;\n- `payload.assetId` (or an `attachments[]` array) resolves at delivery time into\n  `EmailAttachment { filename, mime, bytes }`, carried on `EmailMessage` and passed\n  through by BOTH providers — SMTP into `MailMessage.attachments`, Postmark into its\n  base64 `Attachments` array (without which a Postmark host would silently receive a\n  report email containing no report);\n- a producer that authored a subject gets that subject; one that authored none falls\n  back to `{action}.email.subject|body|body_html` in the effect''s locale, so a new\n  emailed action ships translated copy by adding CATALOG KEYS, not a match arm.\n  `send_invite` keeps its legacy `invite.` prefix — those keys are in every shipped\n  translation and renaming them would break all of them.\n\nThe bytes travel by reference, never in the row: a multi-megabyte PDF inlined into a\ndurable queue row would be re-written on every retry and kept forever in a dead\nletter. The asset read is workspace-walled and principal-free, the same posture as\nthe SMTP credential read — the workspace comes from the payload and is never\ndefaulted (rule 6). A missing asset is a PERMANENT failure resolved BEFORE the first\nsend, so an effect with a bad reference mails nobody: a report email that arrives\nwith no report is worse than a visible dead letter, because it looks like it worked.\n\n`email_target.rs` was at 397 of its 400 allowed lines, so the payload, the words, the\nattachments and the dev providers each moved to their own file.\n\nAlso re-export `lb_telemetry::SinkConfig` from `lb-node` as `TelemetrySink`.\n`BootConfig.telemetry` was a field an embedder was REQUIRED to fill with a type it\ncould not name, so the only reachable value was the embed default `Off` — which\ninstalls no tracing subscriber, silently discarding every info!/warn! the core emits\nin an embedded node. A host that had dropped every email looked exactly like one that\nhad sent them, and the warning saying so was itself invisible. `Off` stays the\ndefault; opting in is now one line.\n\nTests: 27 lib unit (fan-out, per-recipient dedup, missing asset, authored-vs-catalog\nwords) plus `email_attachment_wire_test` — a real PDF asset through a real\n`relay_outbox` pass and a real `SmtpEmailProvider` into the sanctioned in-test SMTP\nserver, asserting the base64 of the PDF and its `filename=` appear in the bytes BOTH\nrecipients'' sessions actually put on the wire.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(outbox): split SmtpTransportConfig out of provider_smtp.rs\n\nThe attachment mapping pushed provider_smtp.rs to 448 lines, past both its\ngrandfathered baseline (442) and the 400-line FILE-LAYOUT limit — CI caught it.\nExtending a file that is already over is exactly what the rule exists to stop.\n\nSplit by responsibility, not by line count: `provider_smtp.rs` owns the SENDING\n(resolve a credential per send, mint an OAuth token, map a transport error onto the\noutbox''s retry classification), and `smtp_transport_config.rs` owns the DESCRIPTION\nof a relay — which has a different reader, an operator writing deployment config\nrather than a maintainer reading the send path. The \"names only, never values\" doc\nand the test asserting the struct stays Debug-safe travel with it, since that\nproperty is about the config, not the provider.\n\nprovider_smtp.rs 448 -> 345; the baseline is trimmed accordingly.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(inbox-outbox): cross-link the attachments/fan-out scope from the transport scope\n\nThe transport scope''s ''every email goes nowhere'' framing is now two levels stale:\nthe transport shipped, and the target in front of it grew fan-out and attachments.\nRecord which of its gaps closed, and the reason the boot warning it added was never\nseen on an embedded node.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(reminder): a firing resolves its author''s grants, and a denial no longer kills the schedule\n\nTwo bugs, found while proving the scheduled-report email path end to end. Together\nthey mean NO reminder authored by a real user has ever fired — any action kind,\nany workspace — and that the failure was invisible.\n\n1. THE GRANT LOOKUP USED THE WRONG KEY.\n\n`Principal::sub()` is `\"user:test\"`; the durable grant store keys on the BARE handle\n(`resolve_caps` re-wraps its argument as `Subject::User`). The session mint has always\nstripped the prefix and says so in a comment (`role/gateway/src/session/mint_session.rs`).\n`resolve_fire_principal` did not — so it looked up `Subject::User(\"user:test\")`, a key\nnothing in production ever writes, and got ZERO caps back. Every action''s own gate then\nrefused, every time.\n\nObserved live: an admin holding 339 resolved caps (including `mcp:outbox.enqueue:call`\nfrom BOTH `role:member` and `role:workspace-admin`) had a reminder that fired with\n`n=0` caps and was denied.\n\nIt went unnoticed because the tests seeded their grants under the prefixed key too —\n`Subject::User(\"user:test\")` — so the fixtures agreed with the bug. Those helpers now\nPARSE the subject exactly as the `grants.assign` verb does, which is what makes the\nnew `a_firing_resolves_the_authors_grants_under_the_bare_handle_the_store_keys_on`\nregression test meaningful (mutation-checked: it fails against the unfixed resolver).\n\n2. A DENIED FIRING WEDGED THE REMINDER PERMANENTLY.\n\nOn `Denied` the reactor left `next_attempt_ts` untouched, commented as \"the reminder\nwaits\" for the grant to come back. It could not wait: the idempotency job for that\ninstant had already been written, so every later scan re-selected the same instant,\nfound the job, and skipped. One denial killed the schedule for good — after a single\n`denied=1` line, the reactor said nothing ever again.\n\nA denied firing is now RESCHEDULED to the next slot. Deliberately `reschedule`, not\n`advance`: nothing ran, so `runs` must not move and a `max_runs` one-shot must not be\nburned by a denial it never executed. Skipping a missed instant is already this\nreactor''s documented policy (fire-once-then-skip, no backfill), so this is the\nbehaviour the original comment was reaching for. The job still blocks a re-fire of the\nrefused instant itself, so there is no escalation and no double-fire.\n\n3. AND THE REASON WAS THROWN AWAY.\n\n`fire_outbox` mapped every `enqueue_outbox` error to `Denied` with `map_err(|_| …)`.\nThe caller must still see only `Denied` (a firing must not probe the caps wall), but a\nmissing capability and a store failure want completely different fixes and looked\nidentical in the log — which is why bug 1 presented as an unexplained `denied=1`. The\nunderlying error is now logged at `warn` with the reminder, target, action and effect id.\n\nTests: `reminders_reactor_test` 11, `reminder_fire_test` 8, `reminders_mcp_test` 6,\n`reminder_range_test` 1 — all green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(reminder): split the fire-time authorization tests out of the reactor test\n\n`reminders_reactor_test.rs` covers the reactor''s SCHEDULING behaviour (advance, skip,\ncatch-up, max-runs, workspace isolation). The two tests about what an author''s GRANTS\ndo at the moment of firing — the bare-handle resolve and the recovery from a denial —\nare a different responsibility, and adding the second pushed the file past its\nbaseline, which FILE-LAYOUT caught.\n\n`reminder_grant_resolution_test.rs` takes both, with a module doc naming the shared\n`subject()` helper as the load-bearing detail: it parses the principal sub the way\n`grants.assign` does, so a grant in a test lands where a real grant lands. Seeding\nthem anywhere else is what hid the bug in the first place.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8449b90a63cbbe2132c6430e561483bc9facfcdd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/trees/8449b90a63cbbe2132c6430e561483bc9facfcdd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/git/commits/c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024","verification":{"payload":"tree 8449b90a63cbbe2132c6430e561483bc9facfcdd\nparent c978b455f3c887ab2e6a56d5e3150cda952da55a\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1787010888 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787010888 +1000\n\nfeat(outbox): attachments, recipient fan-out, and payload-authored words on the email target (#176)\n\n* feat(outbox): attachments, recipient fan-out, and payload-authored words on the email target\n\nThe email transport shipped (#118) and works, but the TARGET in front of it could\nonly ever deliver one thing. `EmailTarget::deliver` read `payload.email` — exactly\none address — rendered subject and body from the `invite.email.*` catalog under a\nhardcoded `action == \"send_invite\"` arm, had no notion of an attachment, and fell\nthrough to the literal subject \"Notification\" with an EMPTY body for anything else.\n`EmailMessage` had no `attachments` field at all, even though `lb_mail::MailMessage`\none layer down has had one since the transport landed.\n\nSo the deferred half of the reports scope had nowhere to land: rubix-ai renders a\nreport to PDF on a schedule, stores it as a workspace asset, and enqueues\n`{target: \"email\", action: \"report\", payload: {assetId, recipients, subject, body}}`\n— a row this target could not read, address, or attach.\n\nAttaching a file and mailing several people are things ANY producer wants, so\nneither goes behind an `if action == \"report\"`:\n\n- fan out to every address in `recipients` (plus the legacy single `email`),\n  de-duplicated, ONE message each, so every recipient has its own delivery outcome\n  and its own delivered-ledger row. A retry after a partial failure re-sends only\n  to whoever missed out;\n- `payload.assetId` (or an `attachments[]` array) resolves at delivery time into\n  `EmailAttachment { filename, mime, bytes }`, carried on `EmailMessage` and passed\n  through by BOTH providers — SMTP into `MailMessage.attachments`, Postmark into its\n  base64 `Attachments` array (without which a Postmark host would silently receive a\n  report email containing no report);\n- a producer that authored a subject gets that subject; one that authored none falls\n  back to `{action}.email.subject|body|body_html` in the effect''s locale, so a new\n  emailed action ships translated copy by adding CATALOG KEYS, not a match arm.\n  `send_invite` keeps its legacy `invite.` prefix — those keys are in every shipped\n  translation and renaming them would break all of them.\n\nThe bytes travel by reference, never in the row: a multi-megabyte PDF inlined into a\ndurable queue row would be re-written on every retry and kept forever in a dead\nletter. The asset read is workspace-walled and principal-free, the same posture as\nthe SMTP credential read — the workspace comes from the payload and is never\ndefaulted (rule 6). A missing asset is a PERMANENT failure resolved BEFORE the first\nsend, so an effect with a bad reference mails nobody: a report email that arrives\nwith no report is worse than a visible dead letter, because it looks like it worked.\n\n`email_target.rs` was at 397 of its 400 allowed lines, so the payload, the words, the\nattachments and the dev providers each moved to their own file.\n\nAlso re-export `lb_telemetry::SinkConfig` from `lb-node` as `TelemetrySink`.\n`BootConfig.telemetry` was a field an embedder was REQUIRED to fill with a type it\ncould not name, so the only reachable value was the embed default `Off` — which\ninstalls no tracing subscriber, silently discarding every info!/warn! the core emits\nin an embedded node. A host that had dropped every email looked exactly like one that\nhad sent them, and the warning saying so was itself invisible. `Off` stays the\ndefault; opting in is now one line.\n\nTests: 27 lib unit (fan-out, per-recipient dedup, missing asset, authored-vs-catalog\nwords) plus `email_attachment_wire_test` — a real PDF asset through a real\n`relay_outbox` pass and a real `SmtpEmailProvider` into the sanctioned in-test SMTP\nserver, asserting the base64 of the PDF and its `filename=` appear in the bytes BOTH\nrecipients'' sessions actually put on the wire.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(outbox): split SmtpTransportConfig out of provider_smtp.rs\n\nThe attachment mapping pushed provider_smtp.rs to 448 lines, past both its\ngrandfathered baseline (442) and the 400-line FILE-LAYOUT limit — CI caught it.\nExtending a file that is already over is exactly what the rule exists to stop.\n\nSplit by responsibility, not by line count: `provider_smtp.rs` owns the SENDING\n(resolve a credential per send, mint an OAuth token, map a transport error onto the\noutbox''s retry classification), and `smtp_transport_config.rs` owns the DESCRIPTION\nof a relay — which has a different reader, an operator writing deployment config\nrather than a maintainer reading the send path. The \"names only, never values\" doc\nand the test asserting the struct stays Debug-safe travel with it, since that\nproperty is about the config, not the provider.\n\nprovider_smtp.rs 448 -> 345; the baseline is trimmed accordingly.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(inbox-outbox): cross-link the attachments/fan-out scope from the transport scope\n\nThe transport scope''s ''every email goes nowhere'' framing is now two levels stale:\nthe transport shipped, and the target in front of it grew fan-out and attachments.\nRecord which of its gaps closed, and the reason the boot warning it added was never\nseen on an embedded node.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(reminder): a firing resolves its author''s grants, and a denial no longer kills the schedule\n\nTwo bugs, found while proving the scheduled-report email path end to end. Together\nthey mean NO reminder authored by a real user has ever fired — any action kind,\nany workspace — and that the failure was invisible.\n\n1. THE GRANT LOOKUP USED THE WRONG KEY.\n\n`Principal::sub()` is `\"user:test\"`; the durable grant store keys on the BARE handle\n(`resolve_caps` re-wraps its argument as `Subject::User`). The session mint has always\nstripped the prefix and says so in a comment (`role/gateway/src/session/mint_session.rs`).\n`resolve_fire_principal` did not — so it looked up `Subject::User(\"user:test\")`, a key\nnothing in production ever writes, and got ZERO caps back. Every action''s own gate then\nrefused, every time.\n\nObserved live: an admin holding 339 resolved caps (including `mcp:outbox.enqueue:call`\nfrom BOTH `role:member` and `role:workspace-admin`) had a reminder that fired with\n`n=0` caps and was denied.\n\nIt went unnoticed because the tests seeded their grants under the prefixed key too —\n`Subject::User(\"user:test\")` — so the fixtures agreed with the bug. Those helpers now\nPARSE the subject exactly as the `grants.assign` verb does, which is what makes the\nnew `a_firing_resolves_the_authors_grants_under_the_bare_handle_the_store_keys_on`\nregression test meaningful (mutation-checked: it fails against the unfixed resolver).\n\n2. A DENIED FIRING WEDGED THE REMINDER PERMANENTLY.\n\nOn `Denied` the reactor left `next_attempt_ts` untouched, commented as \"the reminder\nwaits\" for the grant to come back. It could not wait: the idempotency job for that\ninstant had already been written, so every later scan re-selected the same instant,\nfound the job, and skipped. One denial killed the schedule for good — after a single\n`denied=1` line, the reactor said nothing ever again.\n\nA denied firing is now RESCHEDULED to the next slot. Deliberately `reschedule`, not\n`advance`: nothing ran, so `runs` must not move and a `max_runs` one-shot must not be\nburned by a denial it never executed. Skipping a missed instant is already this\nreactor''s documented policy (fire-once-then-skip, no backfill), so this is the\nbehaviour the original comment was reaching for. The job still blocks a re-fire of the\nrefused instant itself, so there is no escalation and no double-fire.\n\n3. AND THE REASON WAS THROWN AWAY.\n\n`fire_outbox` mapped every `enqueue_outbox` error to `Denied` with `map_err(|_| …)`.\nThe caller must still see only `Denied` (a firing must not probe the caps wall), but a\nmissing capability and a store failure want completely different fixes and looked\nidentical in the log — which is why bug 1 presented as an unexplained `denied=1`. The\nunderlying error is now logged at `warn` with the reminder, target, action and effect id.\n\nTests: `reminders_reactor_test` 11, `reminder_fire_test` 8, `reminders_mcp_test` 6,\n`reminder_range_test` 1 — all green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(reminder): split the fire-time authorization tests out of the reactor test\n\n`reminders_reactor_test.rs` covers the reactor''s SCHEDULING behaviour (advance, skip,\ncatch-up, max-runs, workspace isolation). The two tests about what an author''s GRANTS\ndo at the moment of firing — the bare-handle resolve and the recovery from a denial —\nare a different responsibility, and adding the second pushed the file past its\nbaseline, which FILE-LAYOUT caught.\n\n`reminder_grant_resolution_test.rs` takes both, with a module doc naming the shared\n`subject()` helper as the load-bearing detail: it parses the principal sub the way\n`grants.assign` does, so a grant in a test lands where a real grant lands. Seeding\nthem anywhere else is what hid the bug in the first place.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqg59ICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANrkQAG+hEHOwaBRjA4BTEFemmkvP\nvmTdp58SXyOpVvrhx1RCmHL1cWlNes8Pf1GABJgzRnQGfrcrzsQhsd7yYyF949VS\naJ69zAff01N05o1jgboPi7MmkZnAOAiuNOqBuhekmzwyqn1BzfKFIQqOKCfSnidu\ngdMpq8wFLJzAErDHxoAbszzqgkelivUgT27+F7IgPn0iHoVnVcfeSbEzBmLf9W7s\nLU/b5w90hd/fal+32YJ//5kAWnFqyfmQj5XP3z+dHFpIZHgkcK8ea+wrKl6gnflz\nyGGeu9TgZHXJRZ0YofmySRR2qVIgxyPqAHGsvT71rDR/huJmYBlBspO0ppkyjjs6\n+cbLtKkH0JNTiGc8Cs3Xw2YyX6Cis4fcfaBtNkz+7ns5qzUlEj0BLU24NkFDWvms\nTjeEaVv3LC6PK3eTOBBL+UZhZXlt3R1+uLKXm1H5vcN1reT5zlhbUIKoJJKdS2Ty\nRRFPK4L8mk6NBEDE8kpEz1ib3c+050QDj00tUL+mlfJMbNEOgd2QaY3EplGwNqUb\niD9hhTgE+VsD5yLgYfkTuzjOO0Ajxnh9BpSgcn8tAUHNwfrEkcsItsc6myXbG+37\nB2zS7t1kw5EfdWiNTOiPnZXaNT3+EFlQcHgly2LASpZ9ftSlS2/axHd9odrSfQKf\n2HT8j+wCMJqUE+AbNyBL\n=sx+L\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-17T23:54:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024","node_id":"C_kwDOTFiyodoAKGM2ZGJlY2JjY2EwNTY4OTliY2I4YmY4ZTI2MmZmMzY2ODYwMjAwMjQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/lb/commit/c978b455f3c887ab2e6a56d5e3150cda952da55a","sha":"c978b455f3c887ab2e6a56d5e3150cda952da55a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/c978b455f3c887ab2e6a56d5e3150cda952da55a"}],"sha":"c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/lb/commits/c6dbecbcca056899bcb8bf8e262ff36686020024"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/49853921?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/brabeem/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/brabeem/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/brabeem/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/brabeem/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"